s the Shakespeare’s Globe production of “King Lear” that can be seen — and, if you’re a quick study, maybe sung along with — at the Skirball Center for the Performing Arts. This is not the only disconcertingly festive note that distinguishes this otherwise mostly undistinguished production. Before the play begins, cast members can be seen chatting with the audience, swapping, I don’t know, idle observations about the weather or the subway traffic? (Nobody sidled up to me, so I can’t be sure.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/02/arts/LEAR/LEAR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 483, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/02/arts/LEAR/LEAR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 483}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/02/arts/LEAR/LEAR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 824, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/02/arts/LEAR/LEAR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1648, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/02/arts/LEAR/LEAR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/02/arts/LEAR/LEAR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/02/arts/LEAR/LEAR-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {