ial': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dbd35574-2c78-515e-abb9-e5b21d044c83', 'word_count': 901, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dbd35574-2c78-515e-abb9-e5b21d044c83'}, {'abstract': 'A woman struggles toward independence after her husband’s unexpected death in Colm Toibin’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/05/books/review/nora-webster-by-colm-toibin.html', 'snippet': 'A woman struggles toward independence after her husband’s unexpected death in Colm Toibin’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For years now, I’ve been blathering to any aspiring writer who would listen about my notions of what makes a literary character “work.” The gist of my advice has been this: Assail a character’s privacy from every possible angle, revealing to the reader that person’s impulses, wishes, personal history, habits of mind. The more fully we enter into a character’s emotional and psychological state, I’ve maintained, the more deeply invested we’ll become in her story. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05egan-sub/05egan-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 638, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05egan-sub/05egan-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 638}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05egan-sub/05egan-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 638, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05egan-sub/05egan-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 638, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05egan-sub/05egan-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05egan-sub/05egan-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05egan-sub/05egan-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Finding a Voice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Finding a Voice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Egan, Jennifer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Toibin, Colm', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-02T19:25:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Egan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac89a4a5-e1f6-5182-8457-0235e18b0444', 'word_count': 1279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac89a4a5-e1f6-5182-8457-0235e18b0444'}, {'abstract': 'A history of the digital age, and of the collaborators and lone tinkerers who helped usher it in.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/05/books/review/the-innovators-by-walter-isaacson.html', 'snippet': 'A history of the digital age, and of the collaborators and lone tinkerers who helped usher it in.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the H-bomb testing frenzy of the 1950s, a RAND Corporation researcher named Paul Baran became concerned about the fragility of America’s communications networks. The era’s telephone systems required users to connect to a handful of major hubs, which the Soviets would doubtless target in the early hours of World War III. So Baran dreamed up a less vulnerable alternative: a decentralized network that resembled a vast fishnet, with an array of small nodes that were each linked to a few others. These nodes could not only receive signals but also route them along to their neighbors, thereby creating countless possible paths for data to keep flowing should part of the network be destroyed. This data would travel across the structure in tiny chunks, called “packets,” that would self-\xadassemble into coherent wholes upon reaching their destinations.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/books/review/05koerner/05koerner-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 328, 'width': 500, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/books/review/05koerner/05koerner-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 328, 'width': 500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/books/review/05koerner/05koerner-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/25/books/review/05koerner/05koerner-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/books/review/05koerner/05koerner-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Geek Squad', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Geek Squad', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Isaacson, Walter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-02T19:30:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brendan I. Koerner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brendan', 'middlename': 'I.', 'lastname': 'Koerner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/665013b9-63e4-5f52-a610-52968f283ee7', 'word_count': 1310, 'uri': 'nyt://article/665013b9-63e4-5f52-a610-52968f283ee7'}, {'abstract': 'The menu at Com Tam Ninh Kieu in the Bronx focuses on broken-rice dishes and noodle soups.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/08/dining/rice-and-noodles-in-many-guises-.html', 'snippet': 'The menu at Com Tam Ninh Kieu in the Bronx focuses on broken-rice dishes and noodle soups.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The specialty at Com Tam Ninh Kieu is broken rice, the lowliest rice, grains splintered in the milling process, shunted aside and sold cheap. In Vietnam, it was poor man’s food until the rich realized what they were missing. Turns out the fragments are clingier and earthier than their grander, polished cousins, all the better for soaking up the flavors of whatever is flung on top.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rice and Noodles in Many Guises', 'kicker': 'Hungry City', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rice and Noodles in Many Guises', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mishan, Ligaya', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Com Tam Ninh Kieu (Bronx, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-02T19:31:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ligaya Mishan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ligaya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mishan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/90e12e0f-b6d5-58cc-bf16-c7b9d02e041a', 'word_count': 707, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90e12e0f-b6d5-58cc-bf16-c7b9d02e041a'}, {'abstract': 'Many observers compare today’s protests in Hong Kong to the events of 1989 in Tiananmen Square. Will the authorities crack down as they did in 1989?', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/02/is-hong-kongs-umbrella-revolution-a-new-tiananmen/', 'snippet': 'Many observers compare today’s protests in Hong Kong to the events of 1989 in Tiananmen Square. Will the authorities crack down as they did in 1989?', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the people of Hong Kong gathered over the past week in the city’s central business district staging the biggest pro-democracy protest in a Chinese-controlled area in decades, headlines around the world compared today’s movement to the 1989 student demonstrations in Beijing’s Tiananmen Square.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/02/blogs/02HONGKONG/02HONGKONG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 598, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/02/blogs/02HONGKONG/02HONGKONG-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 598}}, {'ra