889-6d5e-56c3-a199-a7e7c53e0760'}, {'abstract': 'We’ll never know what medical breakthroughs were missed because young scientists were not provided with resources.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/03/opinion/young-brilliant-and-underfunded.html', 'snippet': 'We’ll never know what medical breakthroughs were missed because young scientists were not provided with resources.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — EVERY year the National Institutes of Health receives almost $30 billion in federal funds to invest in biomedical research. The bulk of that money goes to researchers who are in many cases esteemed in their fields — but also, in many cases, beyond the age when most scientists make their most important contributions to their fields. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/03/opinion/03harris/03harris-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 550, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/03/opinion/03harris/03harris-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 550}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/03/opinion/03harris/03harris-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 939, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/03/opinion/03harris/03harris-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1781, 'width': 1942, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/03/opinion/03harris/03harris-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/03/opinion/03harris/03harris-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/03/opinion/03harris/03harris-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Young, Brilliant and Underfunded', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Young, Brilliant and Underfunded', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harris, Andy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Institutes of Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Age, Chronological', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Health and Human Services Department', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-02T23:17:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3858769-9f2a-5abb-a988-b8a2897fe082', 'word_count': 898, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3858769-9f2a-5abb-a988-b8a2897fe082'}, {'abstract': 'General Motors is recalling almost 118,000 of its 2013-14 model vehicles including the Chevrolet Tahoe and Cadillac Escalade.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/03/business/gm-recalls-more-cars-over-possible-stalling.html', 'snippet': 'General Motors is recalling almost 118,000 of its 2013-14 model vehicles including the Chevrolet Tahoe and Cadillac Escalade.', 'lead_paragraph': 'General Motors is recalling almost 118,000 of its 2013-14 model vehicles because they may stall or fail to start, the company said on Thursday. The action includes almost 98,000 vehicles in the United States, Alan Adler, a G.M. spokesman, wrote in an email. The remaining vehicles are in Canada, Mexico and some export markets. Slivers of metal in the chassis control module could cause an electrical short, Mr. Adler said. He said the automaker was not aware of any accidents or injuries related to the defect. The affected models are the 2013-14 Chevrolet Tahoe and Suburban; 2013-14 Cadillac CTS; 2013-14 GMC Yukon and Yukon XL; 2013-14 Cadillac Escalade and Escalade ESV; 2014 Chevrolet Traverse; 2014 GMC Acadia; 2014 Buick Enclave; 2014 Chevrolet Express and GMC Savana and the 2014 Chevrolet Silverado HD and GMC Sierra HD. This is the automaker’s 69th recall this year and brings the number of vehicles recalled in the United States to just over 26 million. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'G.M. Recalls More Cars Over Possible Stalling ', 'kicker': 'Business Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'G.M. Recalls More Cars Over Possible Stalling', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jensen, Christopher', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recalls and Bans of Products', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-02T23:19:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9cc73e5-228e-51f1-b026-0df36796fcb2', 'word_count': 160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9cc73e5-228e-51f1-b026-0df36796fcb2'}, {'abstract': 'In Texas’s long legal battle over its sweeping abortion law, an appeals court gave the state permission to require clinics to meet the same standards as hospital-style surgical centers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/03/us/appeals-court-ruling-closes-13-abortion-clinics-in-texas.html', 'snippet': 'In Texas’s long legal battle over its sweeping abortion law, an appeals court gave the state permission to require clinics to meet the same standards as hospital-style surgical centers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DALLAS — Thirteen abortion clinics in Texas were forced to close immediately after a federal appellate court on Thursday sided with Texas in its yearlong legal battle over its sweeping abortion law and allowed the state to enforce one of the law’s toughest provisions while the case was being appealed.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Decision Allows Abortion Law, Forcing 13 Texas Clinics to Close ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Decision Allows Abortion Law, Forcing 13 Texas Clinics to Close', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fernandez, Manny', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appeals Courts (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-02T23:22:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manny Fernandez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fernandez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5b7b67d-3536-5b03-9f50-2f31ae76f35d', 'word_count': 1085, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5b7b67d-3536-5b03-9f50-2f31ae76f35d'}, {'abstract': 'The lack of vetting and accountability makes us less secure, not more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/03/opinion/a-perilous-dependence-on-contractors.html', 'snippet': 'The lack of vetting and accountability makes us less secure, not more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE director of the Secret Service has resigned after, among other problems, the revelation that, in a visit to Atlanta on Sept. 16, President Obama rode in an elevator with a private security contractor who was carrying a gun and had an arrest record. The episode raises a crucial question: How thoroughly does the government vet\xa0the private security contractors that an increasing number of agencies employ? ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Perilous Dependence on Contractors', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Perilous Dependence on Contractors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Security and Warning Systems', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Employees', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Secret Service', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'M