ne, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Labor Department (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T12:45:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Rappeport', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rappeport', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/deed151a-4fd1-58f4-b0cd-a62d5651fa7a', 'word_count': 68, 'uri': 'nyt://article/deed151a-4fd1-58f4-b0cd-a62d5651fa7a'}, {'abstract': 'Daily Clip Report is a collection of articles about the media and The Times that is emailed each day to senior executives and newsroom managers here.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/times-insider/2014/10/03/daily-clip-report-33/', 'snippet': 'Daily Clip Report is a collection of articles about the media and The Times that is emailed each day to senior executives and newsroom managers here.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Good morning. Here is Daily Clip Report, a collection of articles about The Times and the media sent by email each morning to senior executives and newsroom editors. The email is produced by the Corporate Communications Department at The Times.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Daily Clip Report', 'kicker': 'Times Insider', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T12:45:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jordan Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jordan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac0d5c1d-b57a-521d-89aa-09759a3576d3', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac0d5c1d-b57a-521d-89aa-09759a3576d3'}, {'abstract': 'The bright sulfur shelf mushroom, known as the chicken of the woods, grows in the New York region from April to November.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/03/sulfur-shelf-mushroom-tastes-like-chicken/', 'snippet': 'The bright sulfur shelf mushroom, known as the chicken of the woods, grows in the New York region from April to November.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just one encounter with the chicken of the woods, or sulfur shelf mushroom, is often enough to turn the average naturalist into a raving mushroom fan. The sulfur shelf (Laetiporus sulphureus) is virtually unmistakable, generally emerging from the wood of living or dead trees as stemless, vivid orange and yellow fans.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sulfur Shelf Mushroom Tastes Like Chicken', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mushrooms', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'NEW YORK CITY', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T12:45:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Taft', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d1e345c-faf5-5655-803f-06f616f5ed2d', 'word_count': 526, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d1e345c-faf5-5655-803f-06f616f5ed2d'}, {'abstract': 'Metropolitan Diary: A poetic account of the sounds and sights on the subway that keep a sleeper awake and change with every eye opening.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/03/subway-sleep-a-poem/', 'snippet': 'Metropolitan Diary: A poetic account of the sounds and sights on the subway that keep a sleeper awake and change with every eye opening.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Dear Diary:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Subway Sleep: A Poem', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Commuting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sleep', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subways', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T12:51:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Poku', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Poku', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b28da63a-aa08-58ed-bf21-adf77089b839', 'word_count': 163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b28da63a-aa08-58ed-bf21-adf77089b839'}, {'abstract': 'For her latest project, seen here for the first time, the New York–based, Peruvian-born artist Grimanesa Amorós takes inspiration from perpetual surveillance.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/03/grimanesa-amoros-light-installation/', 'snippet': 'For her latest project, seen here for the first time, the New York–based, Peruvian-born artist Grimanesa Amorós takes inspiration from perpetual surveillance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New York–based, Peruvian-born artist Grimanesa Amorós has built a reputation for dazzling light installations that suggest the topographies of distant planets. In the past, Amorós’s sculptures have taken inspiration from otherworldly phenomena ranging from Iceland’s Aurora Borealis to the red sands of New Mexico. Her Uros collection, inspired by the indigenous people who live on floating reed islands in Peru’s Lake Titicaca, the world’s highest body of water, appeared everywhere from the Issey Miyake store in TriBeCa to the 2011 Venice Biennale. For her newest work, “Surveillance,” shown here for the first time, the artist has selected a bleaker, less exotic inspiration: homeland security.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/t-magazine/15amaros-feinstein-1/15amaros-feinstein-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/15/t-magazine/15amaros-feinstein-1/15amaros-feinstein-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/t-magazine/15amaros-feinstein-1/15amaros-feinstein-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/t-magazine/15amaros-feinstein-1/15amaros-feinstein-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/t-magazine/15amaros-feinstein-1/15amaros-feinstein-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/15/t-magazine/15amaros-feinstein-1/15amaros-feinstein-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'imag