me': 'persons', 'value': 'Hiaasen, Carl', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T14:29:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jen Doll', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doll', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7833de27-4efa-5ed7-86e0-ab9801c533f7', 'word_count': 931, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7833de27-4efa-5ed7-86e0-ab9801c533f7'}, {'abstract': 'Oprah Winfrey has propelled many books onto the best-seller list, but her collection “What I Know for Sure” — No. 6 on the advice and how-to list — is the first best seller that is all her own.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/12/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Oprah Winfrey has propelled many books onto the best-seller list, but her collection “What I Know for Sure” — No. 6 on the advice and how-to list — is the first best seller that is all her own.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reading Time: From 1996 to 2011, the most powerful woman in publishing didn’t work in publishing; she worked in TV. I’m talking of course about Oprah Winfrey, whose stamp of approval helped sell tens of millions of books during the 15-year run of her original book club. (She has since introduced a lower-key version she calls Oprah’s Book Club 2.0.) Winfrey’s name is pretty much guaranteed to propel a title onto the list — but she didn’t have a best seller all her own until last month, when her inspirational collection “What I Know for Sure” made its debut at No. 1 on the advice and how-to list, available online. The book, currently No. 6 in its fourth week, gathers Winfrey’s columns for O, The Oprah Magazine, mixing gauzy self-help mantras (“Your spirit,” she writes, “is whispering its greatest desires for you”) with folksy glimpses of her personal life (she grows radishes “so big I call them baboon butts”). She also writes about her love of books. “Everything I do all day, I do in preparation for my reading time,” she says. “Give me a great novel or memoir, some tea and a cozy spot to curl up in, and I’m in heaven. . . . Insight, information, knowledge, inspiration, power: All that and more can come through a good book.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/02/books/review/02oprah/02oprah-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 452, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/02/books/review/02oprah/02oprah-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 452, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/02/books/review/02oprah/02oprah-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/02/books/review/02oprah/02oprah-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/02/books/review/02oprah/02oprah-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T14:30:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/700a28b6-953a-581c-ad4b-57072a6863bb', 'word_count': 505, 'uri': 'nyt://article/700a28b6-953a-581c-ad4b-57072a6863bb'}, {'abstract': 'A young woman with a past of hardship and suffering makes a new start in Gilead, Iowa.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/05/books/review/lila-by-marilynne-robinson.html', 'snippet': 'A young woman with a past of hardship and suffering makes a new start in Gilead, Iowa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Regionalism has always played an important part in American literature, with, say, William Faulkner’s Yoknapatawpha County the iconic Southern example. Those who have read Marilynne Robinson’s radiant Pulitzer Prize-winning novel, “Gilead,” will remember that imaginary town in southernmost Iowa, near the conjunction of Missouri, Kansas and Nebraska — Plains country, partially Southern in spirit and looking west, thereby broadly embodying the essential rural Midwestern America at a seminal period, from the Depression to around 1950. Although American literature isn’t usually known for its religious and philosophical novels, we might think of certain essential works, particularly of Melville and Hawthorne, that concern grace and redemption. Robinson’s new novel, “Lila,” combines these regional and spiritual strains of American writing. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05BKSJOHNSON/1005-bks-Johnson-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 570, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05BKSJOHNSON/1005-bks-Johnson-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 570}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05BKSJOHNSON/1005-bks-Johnson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 973, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05BKSJOHNSON/1005-bks-Johnson-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1946, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05BKSJOHNSON/1005-bks-Johnson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05BKSJOHNSON/1005-bks-Johnson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05BKSJOHNSON/1005-bks-Johnson-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Moral of the Story', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Moral of the Story', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Lila (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Diane', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robinson, Marilynne', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T14:30:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Diane Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Diane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d1858b6-0e9b-5671-b169-73098ea5fb9c', 'word_count': 1221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d1858b6-0e9b-5671-b169-73098ea5fb9c'}, {'abstract': 'In this memoir, the Times columnist Charles M. Blow describes how he surmounted his rage at childhood mistreatment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/05/books/review/charles-m-blows-fire-shut-up-in-my-bones.html', 'snippet': 'In this memoir, the Times columnist Charles M. Blow describes how he surmounted his rage at childhood mistreatment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Charles Blow was only 24 when he was asked by The New York Times to direct its graphics department — apparently the youngest department head in the paper’s history. His elegant charts, distillations of political and social complexity, jolted readers with their logic, lucidity and sheer beauty. Before long, he ascended yet again, reinventing himself — and configuring a new genre of journalism — as the paper’s “visual Op-Ed columnist.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/SUB-05williams/SUB-05williams-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 482, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/SUB-05williams/SUB-05williams-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 482}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/SUB-05williams/SUB-05williams-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 822, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/SUB-05williams/SUB-05williams-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1644, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/SUB-05williams/SUB-05williams-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/SUB-05williams/SUB-05williams-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/SUB-05williams/SUB-05williams-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Putting Down the Gun', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Putting Down the Gun', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Fire Shut Up in My Bones (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blow, Charles M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T14:30:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia J. Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad93e7ab-d70f-58c5-a5ad-204f29a0afcb', 'word_count': 1309, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad93e7ab-d70f-58c5-a5ad-204f29a0afcb'}, {'abstract': 'Auschwitz serves as the backdrop to Martin Amis’s novel of intimacy and moral collapse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/05/books/review/martin-amiss-zone-of-interest.html', 'snippet': 'Auschwitz serves as the backdrop to Martin Amis’s novel of intimacy and moral collapse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Elie Wiesel approached the \xadauthor François Mauriac in the 1950s with a draft of the memoir that would become “Night,” Mauriac was skeptical — not of the book’s quality, but of its necessity. What on earth could “this personal rec\xadord, coming as it does after so many others and describing an abomination such as we might have thought no longer had any secrets for us,” have to add to the already vast body of literature about the Holocaust? he wondered. One reads this now with an ironic chuckle. As we approach the 70th anniversary of the liberation of Auschwitz, the Holocaust, among all its other perverse distinctions, has become the most documented genocide in history. There are memoirs by both survivors and high-ranking Nazis; diaries of life under Nazi rule; collections of letters between SS \xadofficers and their families; specific investigations of the Nazi doctors, the last few months of the war and the structure of the SS; and multiple biographies of figures major and minor. And that list includes only the books Martin Amis mentions in the afterword to his new novel.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05BKSFRANKLIN/1005-bks-FRANKLIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 511, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05BKSFRANKLIN/1005-bks-FRANKLIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 511}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05BKSFRANKLIN/1005-bks-FRANKLIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 871, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05BKSFRANKLIN/1005-bks-FRANKLIN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1199, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05BKSFRANKLIN/05BKSFRANKLIN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05BKSFRANKLIN/05BKSFRANKLIN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05BKSFRANKLIN/05BKSFRANKLIN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Viper’s Nest', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Viper’s Nest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Zone of Interest (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Franklin, Ruth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'AMIS, MARTIN', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T14:31:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ruth Franklin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ruth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Franklin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b258edda-6edd-5371-b46a-600bbcc2c90b', 'word_count': 1326, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b258edda-6edd-5371-b46a-600bbcc2c90b'}, {'abstract': 'In “Women in Clothes,” more than 600 women respond to a survey about how they present themselves.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/05/books/review/women-in-clothes-by-sheila-heti-heidi-julavits-leanne-shapton-and-many-more.html', 'snippet': 'In “Women in Clothes,” more than 600 women respond to a survey about how they present themselves.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early on a Sunday morning, I passed an elderly woman crossing the street, dressed, arrestingly, for church. She wore a flowing black skirt that fell to her midcalf, and a peach blouse with ample sleeves, a high neck, and a fringe of black ruffle that made a stripe across her chest and arms. The effect was simultaneously cheeky and demure, and its boldness made me wonder: How did she gain this confidence, and how had she retained it? The next thought: How could I be more like her?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05weiss/05weiss-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 610, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05weiss/05weiss-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 610}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05weiss/05weiss-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1008, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05weiss/05weiss-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1688, 'width': 1660, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05weiss/05weiss-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subTy