.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/03/video-reviews-of-men-women-children-the-blue-room-and-keep-on-keepin-on/', 'snippet': 'Times critics on “Men, Women & Children,” “The Blue Room” and “Keep On Keepin’ On.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this week’s video, Times critics share their thoughts on “Men, Women & Children,” “The Blue Room” and “Keep On Keepin’ On.” See all of this week’s reviews.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video Reviews of ‘Men, Women & Children,’ ‘The Blue Room’ and ‘Keep On Keepin’ On’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T14:36:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89ff7571-ebd4-561f-913d-ccc42f4c5ca6', 'word_count': 28, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89ff7571-ebd4-561f-913d-ccc42f4c5ca6'}, {'abstract': 'What happens when instant gratification becomes the guiding force in our social, political and economic lives?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/05/books/review/the-impulse-society-by-paul-roberts.html', 'snippet': 'What happens when instant gratification becomes the guiding force in our social, political and economic lives?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paul Roberts thinks a society that wants it now is untenable, and he has written a prophecy to tell us why. He begins “The Impulse Society” with a parable: a visit to a rehab center for online gaming addicts. We come to see a player’s outlook, largely a matter of finding suitable opponents, may be changed by the recognition that there is more to life than that. But are we not all players, Roberts asks, when we surf the web and respond yes or no to the “choices” we are spoon-fed?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-BROMWICH/1005-bks-BROMWICH-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 313, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-BROMWICH/1005-bks-BROMWICH-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 313}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-BROMWICH/1005-bks-BROMWICH-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 535, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-BROMWICH/1005-bks-BROMWICH-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1070, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-BROMWICH/1005-bks-BROMWICH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-BROMWICH/1005-bks-BROMWICH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-BROMWICH/1005-bks-BROMWICH-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Market-Driven Behavior', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Market-Driven Behavior', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roberts, Paul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T14:37:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Bromwich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bromwich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8af724e-ed31-5416-8344-1752421485e1', 'word_count': 1218, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8af724e-ed31-5416-8344-1752421485e1'}, {'abstract': 'A historian argues that the slave states were not a world apart but were crucial to American development.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/05/books/review/the-half-has-never-been-told-by-edward-e-baptist.html', 'snippet': 'A historian argues that the slave states were not a world apart but were crucial to American development.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For residents of the world’s pre-\xademinent capitalist nation, American historians have produced remarkably few studies of capitalism in the United States. This situation was exacerbated in the 1970s, when economic history began to migrate from history to economics departments, where it too often became an exercise in scouring the past for numerical data to plug into computerized models of the economy. Recently, however, the history of American capitalism has emerged as a thriving cottage industry. This new work portrays capitalism not as a given (something that “came in the first ships,” as the historian Carl Degler once wrote) but as a system that developed over time, has been constantly evolving and penetrates all aspects of society.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05foner/05foner-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05foner/05foner-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05foner/05foner-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05foner/05foner-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 777, 'width': 1165, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05foner/05foner-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05foner/05foner-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05foner/05foner-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Brutal Process', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Brutal Process', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foner, Eric', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baptist, Edward E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T14:37:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Foner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Foner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2b3a74e-fc85-54c5-b2ae-9f7e972991ec', 'word_count': 1260, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2b3a74e-fc85-54c5-b2ae-9f7e972991ec'}, {'abstract': 'Thirteen stories explore the realities of advancing age and vitality on the decline.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/05/books/review/lovely-dark-deep-by-joyce-carol-oates.html', 'snippet': 'Thirteen stories explore the realities of advancing age and vitality on the decline.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Readers will forgive a book almost anything if it has vitality. That’s why “The Goldfinch” has been so indomitably successful, despite its half-drawn gangsters and its slippage toward melodrama, and that’s why Dickens transcends the mawkishness of Little Nell and John Jarndyce. That’s why Shakespeare’s detractors, from Tolstoy to Ira Glass, always look foolish. The sheer livingness of those writers eclipses their faults.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-FInch-sub/1005-bks-FInch-sub-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 394, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-FInch-sub/1005-bks-FInch-sub-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 394}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-FInch-sub/1005-bks-FInch-sub-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 673, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-FInch-sub/1005-bks-FInch-sub-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 997, 'width': 1517, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-FInch-sub/1005-bks-FInch-sub-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-FInch-sub/1005-bks-FInch-sub-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-FInch-sub/1005-bks-FInch-sub-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mortal Coil', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mortal Coil', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oates, Joyce Carol', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T14:37:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Finch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/085829cc-3aee-5a57-b38a-e9e7290d0739', 'word_count': 568, 'uri': 'nyt://article/085829cc-3aee-5a57-b38a-e9e7290d0739'}, {'abstract': 'Archer Mayor’s “Proof Positive” is about the death of a reclusive hoarder, a photojournalist who had suffered a brain injury in Vietnam.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/05/books/review/archer-mayors-proof-positive-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Archer Mayor’s “Proof Positive” is about the death of a reclusive hoarder, a photojournalist who had suffered a brain injury in Vietnam.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joe Gunther, one of the most honorable lawmen you’d ever hope to meet, is the designated mourner in Archer Mayor’s rugged regional mysteries. Over the course of some two dozen novels, the former Brattleboro cop, now a senior officer with the Vermont Bureau of Investigation, has borne witness to all the scourges of modern times — from the meth epidemic to freakish acts of nature like Hurricane Irene — that have laid havoc to his home state. But in the background, there have always been glimpses of another painful legacy — the human fallout from the Vietnam War — that now steps out from the shadows in PROOF POSITIVE (Minotaur, $25.99). No one in the village of Dummerston gave much thought to an eccentric hoarder named Benjamin Kendall until a hapless thief burgles his old farmhouse and finds Kendall buried under the towering mounds of detritus. A sad business, but not a case for a major crimes unit like the V.B.I., you’d think; until another body is pulled from the artfully arranged rubble. As it turns out, the reclusive hoarder was a photojournalist who had suffered a brain injury in Vietnam. Thanks to an enterprising art student who arranged for an exhibition of his war photos at the University of Vermont in Burlington, Kendall had finally come to the attention of the art world — and of an enemy who would kill to keep the past buried in the past.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-CRIME/1005-bks-CRIME-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 784, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-CRIME/1005-bks-CRIME-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 784}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-CRIME/1005-bks-CRIME-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 784, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-CRIME/1005-bks-CRIME-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1568, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-CRIME/1005-bks-CRIME-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-CRIME/1005-bks-CRIME-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/1005-bks-CRIME/1005-bks-CRIME-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dirty Secrets', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dirty Secrets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mayor, Archer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Anne', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Higashino, Keigo', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Longworth, M L', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T14:37:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9122371-b1c8-52c5-8bfa-31f18823c0f1', 'word_count': 903, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9122371-b1c8-52c5-8bfa-31f18823c0f1'}, {'abstract': 'Daniele Gatti will take the post in 2016, succeeding Mariss Jansons, who is to step down in March.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/03/gatti-named-chief-conductor-of-royal-concertgebouw-orchestra/', 'snippet': 'Daniele Gatti will take the post in 2016, succeeding Mariss Jansons, who is to step down in March.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amsterdam’s prestigious Royal Concertgebouw Orchestra announced Friday that Daniele Gatti would become its chief conductor in 2016, succeeding Mariss Jansons, who will step down from the post in March.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gatti Named Chief Conductor of Royal Concertgebouw Orchestra', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gatti, Daniele', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Concertgebouw Orchestra', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T14:39:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/00e76f00-d083-59b9-a857-ce716108e14f', 'word_count': 152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00e76f00-d083-59b9-a857-ce716108e14f'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: A correspondent travels with the French Army in Germany in 1939.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/03/1939-french-army-in-high-spirits/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: A correspondent travels with the French Army in Germany in 1939.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WITH THE FRENCH ARMY IN GERMANY — For the first time since the Napoleonic wars the French armies are fighting on German territory. I have had this morning [Oct. 2] the unique and highly enjoyable experience of entering Germany without a German visa and without the knowledge of the Gestapo. No Nazi official was present at the frontier to examine my passport, to question me as to the amount of money I had on my person. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/04/blogs/04iht-oldoct04-gamelin/04iht-oldoct04-gamelin-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 428, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/04/blogs/04iht-oldoct04-gamelin/04iht-oldoct04-gamelin-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 428}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/04/blogs/04iht-oldoct04-gamelin/04iht-oldoct04-gamelin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/04/blogs/04iht-oldoct04-gamelin/04iht-oldoct04-gamelin-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 909, 'width': 1275, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': No