fore midterm elections, and they show a job market that is recovering steadily but surely.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Remember a month ago, when a crummy August jobs report raised some questions about just how robust the labor market recovery truly was? Never mind.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/04/upshot/04up-jobs1-1412341259579/04up-jobs1-1412341259579-articleLarge.png', 'height': 445, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/04/upshot/04up-jobs1-1412341259579/04up-jobs1-1412341259579-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 445}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/04/upshot/04up-jobs1-1412341259579/04up-jobs1-1412341259579-jumbo.png', 'height': 759, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/04/upshot/04up-jobs1-1412341259579/04up-jobs1-1412341259579-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1245, 'width': 1679, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/04/upshot/04up-jobs1-1412341259579/04up-jobs1-1412341259579-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/04/upshot/04up-jobs1-1412341259579/04up-jobs1-1412341259579-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/04/upshot/04up-jobs1-1412341259579/04up-jobs1-1412341259579-thumbLarge-v2.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Strong Jobs Report, Charted', 'kicker': 'The Labor Market', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T14:41:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Irwin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Irwin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/29033f12-e8c8-559d-bc81-2460923f2f92', 'word_count': 794, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29033f12-e8c8-559d-bc81-2460923f2f92'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/05/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HATCHING TWITTER: A True Story of Money, Power, Friendship, and Betrayal,  by Nick Bilton. (Portfolio/Penguin, $17.) This perceptive, fast-paced account, by a columnist and reporter for The New York Times, centers on Twitter’s four founders — Silicon Valley friends who developed the social media service as a side venture in 2006, then fought bitterly over control of a multibillion-dollar empire that revolutionized the way we communicate. “These are characters made for the big screen,” Maud Newton wrote here.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T14:43:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbd0644d-5c3e-57a1-b7dd-a51ec1b8158f', 'word_count': 453, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbd0644d-5c3e-57a1-b7dd-a51ec1b8158f'}, {'abstract': 'If I had pertussis, how many people would I infect?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/05/magazine/a-new-mothers-fear-about-coughing-fits.html', 'snippet': 'If I had pertussis, how many people would I infect?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the first year after my son was born, I read three histories of smallpox. While he was napping, I looked at photos of toddlers with diphtheria and read the accounts of parents who lost teenagers to influenza. What started, before my son’s birth, as an inquiry into vaccination became a fascination with infectious disease. Images of smallpox victims who lost parts of their faces did not bother me, but I had to turn away from the video of an infant with pertussis who was struggling for breath.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/magazine/05lives/mag-05Lives-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/05/magazine/05lives/mag-05Lives-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 574}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/magazine/05lives/mag-05Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 980, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/magazine/05lives/mag-05Lives-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1597, 'width': 1668, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/magazine/05lives/mag-05Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/05/magazine/05lives/mag-05Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/magazine/05lives/mag-05Lives-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A New Mother’s Fear About Coughing Fits', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Coughing Fits', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Whooping Cough', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies and Infants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vaccination and Immunization', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coughs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T14:45:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eula Biss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Biss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c7b9ccf-0e2a-56fe-9f07-2db75345d783', 'word_count': 780, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c7b9ccf-0e2a-56fe-9f07-2db75345d783'}, {'abstract': 'To uncover patterns that might steer care, doctors and scientists are dipping into the medical records of thousands of patients.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/05/magazine/can-big-data-tell-us-what-clinical-trials-dont.html', 'snippet': 'To uncover patterns that might steer care, doctors and scientists are dipping into the medical records of thousands of patients.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When a helicopter rushed a 13-year-old girl showing symptoms suggestive of kidney failure to Stanford’s Packard Children’s Hospital, Jennifer Frankovich was the rheumatologist on call. She and a team of other doctors quickly diagnosed lupus, an autoimmune disease. But as they hurried to treat the girl, Frankovich thought that something about the patient’s particular combination of lupus symptoms — kidney problems, inflamed pancreas and blood vessels — rang a bell. In the past, she’d seen lupus patients with these symptoms develop life-threatening blood clots. Her colleagues in other specialties didn’t think there was cause to give the girl anti-clotting drugs, so Frankovich deferred to them. But she retained her suspicions. “I could not forget these cases,” she says.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/magazine/05eureka/mag-05Eureka-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/05/magazine/05eureka/mag-05Eureka-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 720}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/magazine/05eureka/mag-05Eureka-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 854, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/magazine/05eureka/mag-0