, 'value': 'Medtronic Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T14:58:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Gelles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gelles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/953a9eb3-44ea-57ad-bf6e-91152638ec91', 'word_count': 514, 'uri': 'nyt://article/953a9eb3-44ea-57ad-bf6e-91152638ec91'}, {'abstract': 'Don’t make excuses for really bad arguments.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/03/bill-grosses-idealized-and-actually-existing/', 'snippet': 'Don’t make excuses for really bad arguments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brad DeLong tries at some length to rationalize Bill Gross’s insistence in 2011 that interest rates were about to spike. But while it’s nice to be charitable, to attempt to put the best face on someone else’s arguments, it’s also important to look at the argument someone was actually making. And the reasoning of Gross and others was much cruder and a lot more foolish than Brad acknowledges. I know because I was involved in the debate in real time.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bill Grosses, Idealized and Actually Existing', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T14:59:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66d6fc1b-3d49-5da2-b7ca-fa7a3485ebf6', 'word_count': 242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66d6fc1b-3d49-5da2-b7ca-fa7a3485ebf6'}, {'abstract': 'On the occasion of their latest album, the iconic Scottish lo-fi duo, famed for inspiring Kurt Cobain, share playlists of the songs that most excite them.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/03/vaselines-band-eugene-kelly-francis-mckee-favorite-songs-playlists/', 'snippet': 'On the occasion of their latest album, the iconic Scottish lo-fi duo, famed for inspiring Kurt Cobain, share playlists of the songs that most excite them.', 'lead_paragraph': '“There are certain songs you hear that simply make you tingle,” says Frances McKee, who with Eugene Kelly comprises the cherished Scottish indie-pop duo the Vaselines. She knows of what she speaks: For nearly three decades of an on-again, off-again career, her band has cranked out infectious little gems that have influenced countless other musicians — most notably Kurt Cobain, who covered several of their songs with Nirvana. The duo’s latest album, “V for Vaselines” (out Oct. 7 from Rosary Music), is its third since Kelly and McKee reunited in 2006. Featuring contributions from members of Belle and Sebastian and Teenage Fanclub and recorded in the studio of Mogwai, it’s a reflection of the close-knit musical community in the Vaselines’s hometown of Glasgow. What’s truly great about the album, however, is the way it captures Kelly and McKee’s mastery at creating shambolic tunes that are equal parts hooks and heartbreak.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/03/t-magazine/03ashlock_vaselines/03ashlock_vaselines-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/03/t-magazine/03ashlock_vaselines/03ashlock_vaselines-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/03/t-magazine/03ashlock_vaselines/03ashlock_vaselines-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/03/t-magazine/03ashlock_vaselines/03ashlock_vaselines-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/03/t-magazine/03ashlock_vaselines/03ashlock_vaselines-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/03/t-magazine/03ashlock_vaselines/03ashlock_vaselines-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/03/t-magazine/03ashlock_vaselines/03ashlock_vaselines-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Legendary Vaselines on the Tunes That Inspire Them', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reed, Lou (1942-2013)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Belle and Sebastian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mogwai (Music Group)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nirvana (Music Group)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Teenage Fanclub', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T15:00:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By MATT DIEHL', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'DIEHL', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1f01d8a-2f5b-5f97-94a1-f9f057c7d18f', 'word_count': 734, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1f01d8a-2f5b-5f97-94a1-f9f057c7d18f'}, {'abstract': 'An author of a commentary questioning the value of a hard danger threshold for planetary temperature defends his views.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/03/getting-over-the-2-degree-limit-on-global-warming/', 'snippet': 'An author of a commentary questioning the value of a hard danger threshold for planetary temperature defends his views.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, Oct. 6, 11:56 p.m. | Joe Romm has posted a long reaction to David Victor’s post, including an update from Stefan Rahmstorf.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/03/blogs/dottherm/dottherm-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 225, 'width': 214, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/03/blogs/dottherm/dottherm-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 225, 'width': 214, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/03/blogs/dottherm/dottherm-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/03/blogs/dottherm/dottherm-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/03/blogs/dottherm/dottherm-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Getting Beyond the 2-Degree Threshold on Global Warming', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Air Pollution', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oceans and Seas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Temperature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Treaties', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hansen, James E', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romm, Joe', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Center for American Progress', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Aeronautics and Space Administration', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nature (Journal)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T15:03:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25bb1892-82d4-54b2-9e49-d9aabc05af28', 'word_count': 6619, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25bb1892-82d4-54b2-9e49-d9aabc05af28'}, {'abstract': 'The endless pings of incoming emails. Then, there’s work and family obligations. Tony Schwartz, in the Life@Work column, offers some ideas about the best ways to use your time and attention.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/10/03/prioritizing-in-a-world-of-overwhelming-demand/', 'snippet': 'The endless pings of incoming emails. Then, there’s work and family obligations. Tony Schwartz, in the Life@Work column, offers some ideas about the best ways to use your time and attention.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was a Saturday morning, and there was nothing I urgently needed to accomplish. But there was a lot I hoped to do. Over breakfast, the possibilities began to clamor for attention in my brain. I wanted to continue reading The New York Times. I also wanted to work out, run a series of errands, check in with a couple of friends, finish reading a book, take the dog for a walk, visit my grandson and find time for a nap. On the work side, I hoped to take some quiet time to reflect on a couple of important unresolved issues I hadn’t addressed in the office. I literally couldn’t keep these competing desires straight in my head, and this was the weekend. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/03/business/dbpix-schwartz-checks-phone/dbpix-schwartz-checks-phone-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/03/business/dbpix-schwartz-checks-phone/dbpix-schwartz-checks-phone-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/03/business/dbpix-schwartz-checks-phone/dbpix-schwartz-checks-phone-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/03/