 'headline': {'main': 'Michelle Obama Both Asset and Liability on Campaign Trail', 'kicker': 'First Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Michelle', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-03T16:23:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By First Draft', 'person': [{'firstname': 'First', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Draft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e351d57-7571-5600-bcb9-3571671d3245', 'word_count': 79, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e351d57-7571-5600-bcb9-3571671d3245'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Lewis, a founder of Southern hip-hop events, keeps it simple on Sundays, spending most of them in Clinton Hill, Brooklyn, where she lives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/05/nyregion/erika-lewis-the-partys-over-time-for-church.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Lewis, a founder of Southern hip-hop events, keeps it simple on Sundays, spending most of them in Clinton Hill, Brooklyn, where she lives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2010, Erika Lewis and her friends Alzo and Maurice Slade saw a hole in the New York dance party scene where Southern hip-hop should be. So they introduced Grits and Biscuits, a pop-up event that has since expanded to Chicago, Los Angeles and other cities. In 2011, the trio, known as E.Z.Mo Breezy, introduced Saturday Morning Cartoons, another dance party that plays music from the 1980s and ’90s. The next one, which Ms. Lewis described as “a safe place to get loose,” will take place at the Music Hall of Williamsburg on Oct. 11. Ms. Lewis, 40, who works full time in marketing, keeps it simple on Sundays, spending most of them in Clinton Hill, Brooklyn, where she lives.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The Ne