e days on the Hong Kong protests, the Chinese Communist Party mouthpiece warned Saturday of “turmoil” if the rule of law is not respected.', 'lead_paragraph': 'People’s Daily, the mouthpiece of the Chinese Communist Party, has said that pursuing a color revolution in the interior of China  through the democracy protests in Hong Kong is a “daydream,” reflecting Beijing’s longstanding fear of Western-style political change from below and of contagion from more democratic Hong Kong.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Color Revolution’ in China a ‘Daydream,’ People’s Daily Tells Protesters', 'kicker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Central', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "People's Daily", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hong Kong', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-04T03:17:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Didi Kirsten Tatlow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Didi', 'middlename': 'Kirsten', 'lastname': 'Tatlow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bec2c75e-d3ca-5fc6-80dd-800b9a8ca96b', 'word_count': 790, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bec2c75e-d3ca-5fc6-80dd-800b9a8ca96b'}, {'abstract': 'Doug O’Neill, the trainer of the 2012 Kentucky Derby winner, I’ll Have Another, will not be allowed to enter horses in this year’s Breeders’ Cup after being suspended by the New York State Gaming Commission in connection with a positive drug test last year on one of his horses at Belmont Park.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/04/sports/breeders-cup-bars-trainer.html', 'snippet': 'Doug O’Neill, the trainer of the 2012 Kentucky Derby winner, I’ll Have Another, will not be allowed to enter horses in this year’s Breeders’ Cup after being suspended by the New York State Gaming Commission in connection with a positive drug test ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Doug O’Neill, the trainer of the 2012 Kentucky Derby winner, I’ll Have Another, will not be allowed to enter horses in this year’s Breeders’ Cup after being suspended by the New York State Gaming Commission in connection with a positive drug test last year on one of his horses at Belmont Park.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Breeders’ Cup Bars Trainer', 'kicker': 'Sports Briefing | Horse Racing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Breeders’ Cup Bars Trainer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Neill, Douglas F", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doping (Sports)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Breeders' Cup (Horse Races)", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York State Gaming Commission', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kentucky Derby', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Belmont Park', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jockeys, Drivers and Trainers', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-04T04:23:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Drape', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Drape', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d8bfb61-b444-5097-82ff-761468b96728', 'word_count': 154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d8bfb61-b444-5097-82ff-761468b96728'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/04/arts/television/whats-on-tv-saturday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (Flix) GREEN CARD (1990) Brontë (Andie MacDowell), an American botanist, and George (Gérard Depardieu), a French musician, above, construct an instant marriage so she can get a plant-filled New York apartment unavailable to a single person and he can find work in the United States in this comedy directed by Peter Weir. Then the Immigration and Naturalization Service comes knocking. “The film’s charms are as picture-perfect as anything George and Brontë manage to manufacture,” Janet Maslin wrote in The New York Times. In “Amélie” (2001), at 10, Audrey Tautou, in her breakthrough role, plays a French sprite working as a waitress who lays out elaborate practical jokes as payback for those who offend her friends. When she falls in love with Nino (Mathieu Kassovitz), Amélie finds herself unable to reveal her feelings and instead turns Paris, recreated through sets and computer-generated imagery, into a kind of treasure hunt as she leaves clues about her identity and he trails after her. Writing in The Times, Elvis Mitchell called this comedy from Jean-Pierre Jeunet whose original title was “Le Fabuleux Destin d’Amélie Poulain,”“a sugar-rush of a movie.” It has, he said, “a hypnotic sense of romance; it’s a fable filled with longing, with a heroine who constantly flirts with failure.” Mr. Mitchell added that there is no denying “that ‘Amélie’ is, to paraphrase its title, fabulous.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Saturday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Saturday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-04T05:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad81ba86-ef80-5d57-b736-457528bf7445', 'word_count': 703, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad81ba86-ef80-5d57-b736-457528bf7445'}, {'abstract': 'Mong Kok, one of the most crowded neighborhoods in the world, was one of two Hong Kong districts where pro-democracy protesters came under attack Friday night.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/04/video-scenes-of-chaos-in-hong-kong/', 'snippet': 'Mong Kok, one of the most crowded neighborhoods in the world, was one of two Hong Kong districts where pro-democracy protesters came under attack Friday night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A week after Hong Kong’s wave of protests began with a student rally, the pro-democracy movement and the authorities alike were showing signs of wear and desperation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video: Scenes of Chaos in Hong Kong', 'kicker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of China', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beijing (China)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hong Kong', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-04T05:20:22+0000', 'document_type': 'ar