ct', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iceland', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Reykjavik (Iceland)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-04T15:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine LaGrave', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'LaGrave', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ba956d5-90a2-5e98-abaf-bd569af68e5b', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ba956d5-90a2-5e98-abaf-bd569af68e5b'}, {'abstract': 'More Gross misconceptions.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/04/nobody-understands-the-liquidity-trap-still/', 'snippet': 'More Gross misconceptions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A correspondent points me to Bill Gross in 2010, declaring that we are in a liquidity trap — and that therefore the Fed’s expansionary policies won’t create jobs, but will simply cause inflation. There’s only one thing to say:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nobody Understands the Liquidity Trap, Still', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-04T15:04:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e86f9613-286d-5218-8743-6e12403a4efd', 'word_count': 205, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e86f9613-286d-5218-8743-6e12403a4efd'}, {'abstract': 'Emily Cox and Henry Rathvon come out swinging.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/04/variety-a-swinging-acrostic/', 'snippet': 'Emily Cox and Henry Rathvon come out swinging.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VARIETY PUZZLE — Wow, talk about not being in my wheelhouse. Today’s acrostic puzzle took me longer to complete than any other one I’ve ever done, I believe. Not that there’s anything wrong with that; I just had to fight a bit harder to make the puzzle fall. Your mileage may vary, of course.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Variety: A Swinging Acrostic', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Acrostic Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-04T16:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee1f8fbf-9228-533e-a94a-17a847f8666d', 'word_count': 347, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee1f8fbf-9228-533e-a94a-17a847f8666d'}, {'abstract': 'American Football brings back its melancholy, dry indie rock for its reunion tour, stopping at Webster Hall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/05/arts/music/american-football-brings-its-reunion-tour-to-webster-hall.html', 'snippet': 'American Football brings back its melancholy, dry indie rock for its reunion tour, stopping at Webster Hall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is an emo revival afoot, and that’s great news, of course, especially for those apt to get lost inside their feeling caves and who believe guitars are great emotional communication tools. But while the new stuff is beginning to catch fire, there’s been a surge in interest in the old stuff, too, which means it’s time for reunion tours. A couple of weeks ago, it was the cherished Texas band Mineral coming through town to trigger tears. This week, it’s American Football, which released one full-length album in 1999 (recently rereleased with bonus tracks by Polyvinyl) that remains a touchstone. It’s a spacious, aching album: melancholy, dry indie rock, cut through with flickers of jazz. Onstage, it’s likely to inspire hushed reverence and old band T-shirts now a size too small. (Friday through next Sunday, Webster Hall, 125 East 11th Street, East Village; websterhall.com.) ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tickle Me, Emo: the New-Old Angst', 'kicker': 'Pop', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tickle Me, Emo: The New-Old Angst', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Webster Hall', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East Village (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-04T16:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a038ff5e-d3a1-57b9-b6cc-304ce057e1d4', 'word_count': 145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a038ff5e-d3a1-57b9-b6cc-304ce057e1d4'}, {'abstract': 'Another Sunday morning.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/04/my-head-talks-yet-again-2/', 'snippet': 'Another Sunday morning.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Update: Change of plan. Apparently it’s going to be all about Ebola, and I’m off the hook.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'My Head Talks Yet Again — Or Actually Not', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-04T16:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4338eec2-c26a-54ca-8a52-4308436f7a5f', 'word_count': 39, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4338eec2-c26a-54ca-8a52-4308436f7a5f'}, {'abstract': 'As Ebola ravages West Africa, Liberians are losing an integral part of their culture, in which the double-cheek kiss was once the standard greeting.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/05/world/africa/ebolas-cultural-casualty-hugs-in-hands-on-liberia.html', 'snippet': 'As Ebola ravages West Africa, Liberians are losing an integral part of their culture, in which the double-cheek kiss was once the standard greeting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONROVIA, Liberia — It is hard enough to push away family and friends, shunning an embrace or even a shake of the hand to protect yourself from Ebola.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/world/05LIBERIA/LIBERIA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/05/world/05LIBERIA/LIBERIA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/world/05LIBERIA/LIBERIA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, '