and Girls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'White House Building (Washington, DC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gonzalez, Omar J (1972- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-04T18:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maureen Dowd', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maureen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dowd', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7016b8be-f577-559d-afb1-a5123cf34a44', 'word_count': 1153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7016b8be-f577-559d-afb1-a5123cf34a44'}, {'abstract': 'The Department of Transportation should have set standards more than 20 years ago.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/05/opinion/sunday/still-dawdling-on-trucker-training.html', 'snippet': 'The Department of Transportation should have set standards more than 20 years ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While delays in issuing federal regulations are not unusual, the delay in setting common-sense training standards for new truck drivers stands out. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Still Dawdling on Trucker Training', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Still Dawdling on Trucker Training', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Traffic Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Transportation Department (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drivers Education', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foxx, Anthony R', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Citizens for Reliable and Safe Highways', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Brotherhood of Teamsters', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Advocates for Highway and Auto Safety', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trucks and Trucking', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-04T18:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/85a5dc9e-f3e9-5b20-a420-63d5f3c2e3f6', 'word_count': 338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85a5dc9e-f3e9-5b20-a420-63d5f3c2e3f6'}, {'abstract': 'It’s time for the United States to disabuse Egyptian leaders of the notion that they are entitled to aid in perpetuity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/05/opinion/sunday/reining-in-egypts-military-aid.html', 'snippet': 'It’s time for the United States to disabuse Egyptian leaders of the notion that they are entitled to aid in perpetuity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Egyptian leaders have come to see the annual $1.3 billion American military aid package as an entitlement they are due in perpetuity for having signed a peace agreement with Israel in 1979. The United States has done little to disabuse them of that notion. It’s time it does. Failing to make significant cuts to the program later this year, when the Obama administration will confront tough choices regarding Egypt’s future, would be indefensible. Since Abdel Fattah el-Sisi took control in Egypt though a military coup in July 2013, the country has returned to its authoritarian moorings by jailing political opponents, silencing critics and vilifying peaceful Islamists. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'm