_paragraph': 'President Obama and India’s new prime minister, Narendra Modi, have done the minimum needed to infuse new energy into a bilateral relationship that had run aground. During their talks last week, both men spoke of their democracies as natural partners, an idea that every president since Bill Clinton has tried to capitalize on, with limited success. To address the challenges before them, including a rising China, Mr. Obama and Mr. Modi are going to have to work harder, so relations don’t atrophy or become more strained. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'India and America, Beginning Again', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'India and America, Beginning Again', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Modi, Narendra', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Protectionism (Trade)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Trade Organization', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-06T01:14:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e0b40e0-1b07-591e-b8b1-945e708d97e7', 'word_count': 498, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e0b40e0-1b07-591e-b8b1-945e708d97e7'}, {'abstract': 'A favorable credit report shouldn’t be a condition for employment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/06/opinion/bad-credit-big-problem.html', 'snippet': 'A favorable credit report shouldn’t be a condition for employment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Millions of Americans have lousy credit. Some lost their jobs in the 2008 financial crisis and survived on credit cards. Some left college buried in loans. Others live in minority neighborhoods that are preyed upon by unscrupulous lenders. Still others had the bad luck to be sick and uninsured. Does membership in the undiscriminating club of beleaguered borrowers inevitably make someone a risky hire or an unreliable employee? Hardly. Does it justify the increasingly common practice of employers using credit checks to reject prospective hires? No. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bad Credit? Big Problem ', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bad Credit? Big Problem', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit Scores', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lander, Brad', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'City Council (NYC)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-06