 'By Jennifer Dunning and William McDonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dunning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McDonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9a89fe7-ce6f-5305-a505-500ac6308446', 'word_count': 1603, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9a89fe7-ce6f-5305-a505-500ac6308446'}, {'abstract': 'The country is a good bet and deserves America’s maximum effort.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/06/tunisia-where-the-arab-spring-still-shows-promise/', 'snippet': 'The country is a good bet and deserves America’s maximum effort.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the chaos of the Middle East, there is still one place that’s not a disaster zone. It is Tunisia, where the Arab Spring was born and where the dream of co-existence between Islam and democracy continues to be championed — and put into practice — by people like Rashid Gannouchi, the founder of Ennadha, the country’s main Islamist Party.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tunisia: Where the Arab Spring Still Shows Promise', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ben Ali, Zine El-Abidine', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ennahda Party (Tunisia)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tunisia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-06T14:08:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Giacomo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Giacomo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0460512-abd0-5a85-b8f9-f9ed96d3a26f', 'word_count': 597, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0460512-abd0-5a85-b8f9-f9ed96d3a26f'}, {'abstract': '“I’ll show you mine, you show yours” has been around for a long time. It’s the medium that makes it different and scary.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/06/the-s-in-sexting-isnt-the-problem-its-the-t-that-should-worry-parents/', 'snippet': '“I’ll show you mine, you show yours” has been around for a long time. It’s the medium that makes it different and scary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New research suggests that in teenagers, sexting isn’t necessarily an indicator of risky sexual behavior, like having multiple partners, using drugs or alcohol before a sexual encounter, or failing to use protection against an unwanted pregnancy. Instead, researchers conducting a longitudinal study of an ethnically diverse group of adolescent students from Southeast Texas suggest that sexting (which they define narrowly as sending a nude image of oneself by text) is both a common and a normal part of adolescent sexual development. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Issue With Sexting Isn’t the Sex. It’s the Text.', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sexting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teenagers and Adolescence', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Text Messaging', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-06T14:15:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastna