umbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/16/fashion/16iht-acaj-grace16-art/16iht-acaj-grace16-art-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1206, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/16/fashion/16iht-acaj-grace16-art/16iht-acaj-grace16-art-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/05/16/fashion/16iht-acaj-grace16-art/16iht-acaj-grace16-art-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/05/16/fashion/16iht-acaj-grace16-art/16iht-acaj-grace16-art-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Picture Day Quandary: Can She Wear the Tiara?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-06T15:59:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1082cab7-3764-5915-a2c2-710906fcee06', 'word_count': 250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1082cab7-3764-5915-a2c2-710906fcee06'}, {'abstract': 'Can you solve William Rowan Hamilton’s classic icosian game?', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/06/icosian/', 'snippet': 'Can you solve William Rowan Hamilton’s classic icosian game?', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week’s puzzle was suggested by Pádraig Kirwan, a professor of mathematics at Ireland’s Waterford Institute of Technology, to commemorate an act of graffiti that took place on Oct. 16, 1843. We’ll hear more about the vandal responsible in a moment, but let’s first celebrate another anniversary: the fiftieth birthday of one of the most valuable mathematics resources online, the Online Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hamilton’s Icosian Game', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Creativity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mathematics', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Puzzles', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gosper, Bill', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rutgers, The State University of New Jersey', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Dublin (Ireland)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-06T16:00:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Antonick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Antonick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1877a870-6606-5746-b7dd-fe24dfdfaa6a', 'word_count': 1601, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1877a870-6606-5746-b7dd-fe24dfdfaa6a'}, {'abstract': 'These D.I.Y. magazines are on the rise in Los Angeles, where artists are using them as a showcase for their work.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/06/zines-spiralogues-sumi-ink-club-skylight-books/', 'snippet': 'These D.I.Y. magazines are on the rise in Los Angeles, where artists are using them as a showcase for their work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While insurrectionary pamphlets have existed since the invention of the printing press, zines only came of age in the 1970s thanks to punk culture and cheap Xerox copying. Today, they embrace different styles (pointillism, poetry) and subjects (gay goths, Italian dogs), but almost all are still self-published in limited runs. In Los Angeles, artists are rediscovering these D.I.Y. publications as a way to experiment and showcase their work. For “Photographs by Spot,” Ed Templeton combines laser-printed photos of the SoCal punk and beach scenes with covers that have been Risographed — an ’80s copying technique that uses a stencil and single-color ink. Travis Diehl’s “Spiralogues” documents a trip to Robert Smithson’s earthwork “Spiral Jetty.” Sumi Ink Club, by Luke Fischbeck and Sarah Rara of the band Lucky Dragons, reproduces details from the illustrations they make at public drawing events. “Any way you can cheat Kinko’s is smiled upon,” says Jenn Witte, of Skylight Books in Los Feliz, where stapled and stitched zines are displayed on a clothes rack.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '64', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/t-magazine/19ten-look-zines/19ten-look-zines-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/19/t-magazine/19ten-look-zines/19ten-look-zines-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/t-magazine/19ten-look-zines/19ten-look-zines-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'supe