llectual Property', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-06T20:47:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/db6ab2c4-48c2-51ed-b60f-278d8f15900b', 'word_count': 54, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db6ab2c4-48c2-51ed-b60f-278d8f15900b'}, {'abstract': 'In “Worthy Fights,” Leon E. Panetta, the former defense secretary, makes clear his disagreements with the Obama White House over decisions concerning Syria and Iraq.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/07/books/worthy-fights-by-leon-e-panetta.html', 'snippet': 'In “Worthy Fights,” Leon E. Panetta, the former defense secretary, makes clear his disagreements with the Obama White House over decisions concerning Syria and Iraq.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The most interesting news in former Defense Secretary Leon E. Panetta’s memoir, “Worthy Fights,” concerns his disagreements with the Obama White House over Syria, Iraq and the budget crisis — disagreements that have been outlined in recent interviews and in testimony before Congress. Still, Mr. Panetta elaborates on such subjects here, and these passages — in what is otherwise an often opaque and evasive book — shed light on the distressing events now unfolding in the Middle East as the Islamic State, also known as ISIS, rolls through large sections of Syria and Iraq. They also illuminate decisions made by the Obama administration, which, in the view of Mr. Panetta and many military observers, contributed to (or at least failed to help inhibit) these sobering developments. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/07/arts/07BOOKPANETTA-1412632502542/07BOOKPANETTA-1412632502542-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 840, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name':