', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Study Reassesses Dengue’s Impact in India ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Study Reassesses Dengue’s Impact in India', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dengue Fever', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Calcutta (India)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vaccination and Immunization', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brandeis University', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-07T08:38:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gardiner Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gardiner', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7398629e-09f9-5f90-b213-7a488ec4fd08', 'word_count': 556, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7398629e-09f9-5f90-b213-7a488ec4fd08'}, {'abstract': 'Bad P.R. is only part of it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/12/magazine/whats-the-nfls-incentive-to-change.html', 'snippet': 'Bad P.R. is only part of it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The N.F.L., from at least one perspective, has had a pretty rough month. In the span of a few days, as most everyone knows, a video surfaced depicting the Baltimore Ravens running back Ray Rice knocking his future wife unconscious; then the Minnesota Vikings superstar Adrian Peterson was booked for purportedly whipping his 4-year-old son with a tree branch. All the while, a clutch of other players faced the consequences of their own ostensible involvement in domestic-violence incidents. But as pundits wondered if the scandals could mark the beginning of the end for America’s favorite sport, the N.F.L.’s television ratings surpassed their levels from a year earlier. The uptick points to a surprising reality: Despite all its current problems, pro football is positioned to not only weather its current storm, but also to sail through it toward greater prosperity.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/magazine/12economy/12economy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/12/magazine/12economy/12economy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/magazine/12economy/12economy-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/magazine/12economy/12economy-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/magazine/12economy/12economy-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/12/magazine/12economy/12economy-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/magazine/12economy/12economy-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s the N.F.L.’s Incentive to Change?', 'kicker': "It's the Economy", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Goal-Line Stand', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McGugan, Ian', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rice, Ray (1987- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Peterson, Adrian', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-07T08:58:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Mcgugan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mcgugan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aae81ac1-f946-5b5e-9fab-3b29f6dd6512', 'word_count': 1238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aae81ac1-f946-5b5e-9fab-3b29f6dd6512'}, {'abstract': 'Inside the Obama administration’s standoff with Republicans, the food industry and the nation’s lunch ladies over the future of the cafeteria.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/12/magazine/how-school-lunch-became-the-latest-political-battleground.html', 'snippet': 'Inside the Obama administration’s standoff with Republicans, the food industry and the nation’s lunch ladies over the future of the cafeteria.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The lunch ladies loved Marshall Matz. For more than 30 years, he worked the halls and back rooms of Washington for the 55,000 dues-paying members of the School Nutrition Association, the men and still mostly women who run America’s school-lunch programs. They weren’t his firm’s biggest clients — that would have been companies like General Mills or Kraft — but Matz, wry and impish even in his late 60s, lavished the lunch ladies with the kind of respect they didn’t always get in school cafeterias. Many of the association’s members considered him a dear colleague. “He would tell everybody: ‘You are a much better lobbyist than I am. You are how we get things done,’ ” said Dorothy Caldwell, who served a term as the association’s president in the early 1990s. “And people liked that.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/magazine/12lunch1/12lunch1-articleLarge-v8.jpg', 'height': 318, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/12/magazine/12lunch1/12lunch1-articleLarge-v8.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 318}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/magazine/12lunch1/12lunch1-jumbo-v8.jpg', 'height': 543, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/magazine/12lunch1/12lunch1-superJumbo-v8.jpg', 'height': 1085, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/magazine/12lunch1/12lunch1-thumbStandard-v7.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/12/magazine/12lunch1/12lunch1-thumbStandard-v7.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/magazine/12lunch1/12lunch1-thumbLarge-v7.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 't