': 'Georgia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-07T16:15:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc1229ba-c9a4-518e-8fa7-142da21bc1e7', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc1229ba-c9a4-518e-8fa7-142da21bc1e7'}, {'abstract': 'New restaurants in New York City are offering cocktail lists that are becoming more creative, and not in a good way.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/08/dining/highbrow-house-cocktails-that-aim-too-high.html', 'snippet': 'New restaurants in New York City are offering cocktail lists that are becoming more creative, and not in a good way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'All mixed drinks can be divided into two categories: good and not good. A cocktail that you finish involuntarily, that moves to your lips again and again without requiring you to decide to raise your arm, is a good one. A cocktail that you finish because you hate to waste alcohol, or one that you don’t finish at all, is not good. This is the binary theory of cocktail criticism.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/08/dining/08notebook/08notebook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 456, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/08/dining/08notebook/08notebook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 456}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/08/dining/08notebook/08notebook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 778, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/08/dining/08notebook/08notebook-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1556, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/08/dining/08notebook/08notebook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/08/dining/08notebook/08notebook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/08/dining/08notebook/08notebook-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Restaurant Cocktails That Aim Too High', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Highbrow Cocktails That Aim Too High', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALCOHOLIC BEVERAGES', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cocktails', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bartenders', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-07T16:25:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pete Wells', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pete', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wells', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/24f26b7a-2a66-5d71-a590-11e024ab22cb', 'word_count': 1440, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24f26b7a-2a66-5d71-a590-11e024ab22cb'}, {'abstract': 'With the midterm elections weeks away, the rulings over laws that Republican-led state governments passed to more tightly regulate voting are changing procedures and could affect outcomes in some states.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/08/us/politics/midterm-elections-voting-laws-court.html', 'snippet': 'With the midterm elections weeks away, the rulings over laws that Republican-led state governments passed to more tightly regulate voting are changing procedures and could affect outcomes in some states.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NILES, Ohio — Just weeks before elections that will decide control of the Senate and crucial governors’ races, a cascade of court rulings about voting rules, issued by judges with an increasingly partisan edge, are sowing confusion and changing voting procedures with the potential to affect outcomes in some states.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/08/us/VOTING-web4/VOTING-web4-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/08/us/VOTING-web4/VOTING-web4-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/08/us/VOTING-web4/VOTING-web4-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/08/us/VOTING-web4/VOTING-web4-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/08/us/VOTING-web4/VOTING-web4-thumbStandard-v5.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/08/us/VOTING-web4