'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cline, Emma', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cline, Emma', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Random House', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-07T18:03:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandra Alter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f831132d-0a40-5bc7-8408-54ec2cdb733a', 'word_count': 281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f831132d-0a40-5bc7-8408-54ec2cdb733a'}, {'abstract': 'The project “Funk, God, Jazz & Medicine: Black Radical Brooklyn” tells the story of Weeksville, a black village founded in the 19th century.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/08/arts/design/funk-god-jazz-medicine-black-heritage-in-brooklyn.html', 'snippet': 'The project “Funk, God, Jazz & Medicine: Black Radical Brooklyn” tells the story of Weeksville, a black village founded in the 19th century.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York City so knows how to lose — and save, and lose again — its history. Among notable rescues of the past several decades were material remains of the vanished 19th-century African-American village of Weeksville in Brooklyn, snatched from the jaws of 1960s urban renewal. Once in parts of what are now Bedford-Stuyvesant and Crown Heights, the village is currently getting fresh and needed attention in an art project organized by the Weeksville Heritage Center and Creative Time called “Funk, God, Jazz & Medicine: Black Radical Brooklyn,” which runs Friday through Sunday. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Time-Traveling to a Corner of Brooklyn’s Past', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Time-Traveling to a Corner of Brooklyn’s Past', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cotter, Holland', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Weeksville Heritage Center (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Creative Time', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Young, Bradford (1977- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bailey, Xenobia', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gentrification', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-07T18:17:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb57ed98-e478-5c56-9043-abd2193f8fbe', 'word_count': 1157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb57ed98-e478-5c56-9043-abd2193f8fbe'}, {'abstract': 'He said the Supreme Court’s non-decision was “tragic and indefensible.”', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/07/ted-cruz-on-same-sex-marriage/', 'snippet': 'He said the Supreme Court’s non-decision was “tragic and indefensible.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many Republicans responded with silence to the same-sex marriage news yesterday. After the Supreme Court declined to review challenges to appellate decisions overturning marriage bans in Virginia, Indiana, Wisconsin, Oklahoma and Utah, effectively allowing gay couples to enter into legally recognized partnerships in those states, they seemed to realize that the fight was lost.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ted Cruz on Same-Sex Marriage', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cruz, Ted', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-07T18:23:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Lapidos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lapidos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0232052-486a-5e7c-a72c-e8298e9df6e6', 'word_count': 224, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0232052-486a-5e7c-a72c-e8298e9df6e6'}, {'abstract': 'If you keep a journal, it’s not the record of the momentous events that will bring you the most pleasure later. It’s the little things.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/07/the-surprising-value-of-journaling-an-ordinary-day/', 'snippet': 'If you keep a journal, it’s not the record of the momentous events that will bring you the most pleasure later. It’s the little things.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Apparently, if you keep a diary or a date book or a blog, or if you chronicle your days on social media, it’s not the big news posts and entries that will make you happiest to look back on. It’s the little things. As Anna North has written on Op-Talk here in The Times, “new research suggests that recording our run-of-the-mill, daily experiences rather than just our highs and lows, could bring us unexpected joy.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/07/us/motherlode-diary/motherlode-diary-articleLarge.png', 'height': 263, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/07/us/motherlode-diary/motherlode-diary-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 263}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/07/us/motherlode-diary/motherlode-diary-jumbo.png', 'height': 267, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/07/us/motherlode-diary/motherlode-diary-superJumbo.png', 'height': 267, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/07/us/motherlode-diary/motherlode-diary-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/07/us/motherlode-diary/motherlode-diary-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/07/us/motherlode-diary/motherlode-diary-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Surprising Value of Journaling an Ordinary Day', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diaries', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-07T18:26:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ef6d267-34e7-580a-a844-daf085b82698', 'word_count': 281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ef6d267-34e7-580a-a844-daf085b82698'}, {'abstract': 'In the latest installment of T’s “What Made Me” video series, the influential art director recalls his Austrian childhood, his “terrible” teenage band and how his interest in album covers led to creating them.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/07/stefan-sagmeister-video-album-covers-profile-designer-prog-rock/', 'snippet': 'In the latest installment of T’s “What Made Me” video series, the influential art director recalls his Austrian childhood, his “terrible” teenage band and how his interest in album covers led to creating them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For more than two decades, the Austrian-born, New York–based art director Stefan Sagmeister has shaped the look of graphic design through his collaborations with the art and music worlds. Though his work encompasses everything from logos to books, he’s perhaps best known for the album covers he’s designed for such clients as Lou Reed and David Byrne; his collaborations with the latter have even earned him two Grammy awards. In the latest installment of T’s “What Made Me” video series by Poppy de Villeneuve, Sagmeister — now the co-founder of the agency Sagmeister & Walsh — recalls his Alpine childhood, his “terrible” teenage band and how his interest in album covers led to designing them (and much more).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stefan Sagmeister on How Prog Rock Shaped His Design Career', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject',