ble for younger readers. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/08/business/media/laura-hillenbrand-jon-meacham-adapt-titles-for-children.html', 'snippet': 'Inspired by the booming market for young adult novels, a growing number of biographers and historians are retrofitting their works to make them palatable for younger readers. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Of all the horrors Louis Zamperini endured during World War II — a plane crash into the Pacific, 47 days stranded at sea, two years in a prisoner-of-war camp — the one experience that truly haunted him was when a Japanese guard tortured and killed an injured duck.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/08/arts/JP-NONFICTION3/JP-NONFICTION3-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 497, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/08/arts/JP-NONFICTION3/JP-NONFICTION3-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 497}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/08/arts/JP-NONFICTION3/JP-NONFICTION3-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 849, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/08/arts/JP-NONFICTION3/JP-NONFICTION3-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1697, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/08/arts/JP-NONFICTION3/JP-NONFICTION3-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/08/arts/JP-NONFICTION3/JP-NONFICTION3-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/08/arts/JP-NONFICTION3/JP-NONFICTION3-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'To Lure Young Readers, Nonfiction Writers Sanitize and Simplify ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Lure Young Readers, Nonfiction Writers Sanitize and Simplify', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teenagers and Adolescence', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'History (Academic Subject)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hillenbrand, Laura', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Meacham, Jon', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Atkinson, Rick', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-08T01:29:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandra Alter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b13f6b0-5c14-58df-80a6-7dfe4ead21a2', 'word_count': 1344, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b13f6b0-5c14-58df-80a6-7dfe4ead21a2'}, {'abstract': 'Brooks and Collins on recent works by Hillary Clinton, Leon Panetta and Bob Gates.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/07/the-peculiar-art-of-the-washington-memoir/', 'snippet': 'Brooks and Collins on recent works by Hillary Clinton, Leon Panetta and Bob Gates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'David Brooks: I don’t know about you, but I stink at book titles. I sweat and struggle and I just can’t think of anything even slightly mediocre for my own opuses. I try to get inspired by the Internet lists of the best book titles ever. Some of them are humorous or mordant: “Do Androids Dream of Electric Sheep?” or “Are You There, Vodka? It’s Me, Chelsea” or “My Heart Is an Idiot.” Some of them are just beautiful: “Everything Is Illuminated” or “One Hundred Years of Solitude” or “The Unbearable Lightness of Being.” They are all 500 times better than anything I’ve ever come up with.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-theconversation/opinionator-pog-theconversation-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-theconversation/opinionator-pog-theconversation-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-theconversation/opinionator-pog-theconversation-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Peculiar Art of the Washington Memoir', 'kicker': 'The Conversation', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gates, Robert M', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Panetta, Leon V', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-08T01:30:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks and Gail Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Gail', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff5d79ef-c18b-5220-8cc5-c1a770d51cf3', 'word_count': 1447, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff5d79ef-c18b-5220-8cc5-c1a770d51cf3'}, {'abstract': 'The proposals would lighten a burden for many New Yorkers wading through the bureaucratic labyrinths of employment applications and pension benefits, among others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/08/nyregion/easing-the-law-for-new-yorkers-shifting-gender.html', 'snippet': 'The proposals would lighten a burden for many New Yorkers wading through the bureaucratic labyrinths of employment applications and pension benefits, among others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York City is poised to redefine what constitutes a transition from one sex to another, allowing a person’s own identity, not anatomy, to be the determining factor.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Easing the Law for New Yorkers Shifting Gender', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Easing the Law for New Yorkers Shifting Gender', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transgender and Transsexuals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'City Council