4-10-08T21:10:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65fcc24d-eea7-5ab6-80ab-eacd8ced08c5', 'word_count': 773, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65fcc24d-eea7-5ab6-80ab-eacd8ced08c5'}, {'abstract': 'The region, known for its garment and leather goods factories, has lately become home to a host of upstart labels.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/08/portugal-mens-wear-scene/', 'snippet': 'The region, known for its garment and leather goods factories, has lately become home to a host of upstart labels.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I think Porto always had a strong aesthetic identity,” says Jose Miguel de Abreu, a native of Portugal’s second-largest city and one of the founders of the local clothing brand La Paz, which he launched in 2012. “Since I was a young kid, I’ve always noticed a tendency for people to dress up and try different things around here.” Now, a spate of new labels — many in the men’s wear realm — is springing up across the region. And as the fashion industry grows increasingly aware of the value of local production, Porto is well-positioned for a resurgence, thanks to northern Portugal’s extensive network of textile and leather factories.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/08/t-magazine/08porto-navarro/08porto-navarro-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/08/t-magazine/08porto-navarro/08porto-navarro-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/08/t-magazine/08porto-navarro/08porto-navarro-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 614, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/08/t-magazine/08porto-navarro/08porto-navarro-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1229, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/08/t-magazine/08porto-navarro/08porto-navarro-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/08/t-magazine/08porto-navarro/08porto-navarro-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/08/t-magazine/08porto-navarro/08porto-navarro-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Porto, Portugal, a Men’s Wear Scene Blossoms', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-08T21:15:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chadner Navarro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chadner', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Navarro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b8fd9e1-6c5f-5d29-9430-5610d836024b', 'word_count': 405, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b8fd9e1-6c5f-5d29-9430-5610d836024b'}, {'abstract': 'We know it simply as “the pill,” but as Jonathan Eig’s cultural history shows, its genesis was anything but simple.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/12/books/review/jonathan-eig-the-birth-of-the-pill-review.html', 'snippet': 'We know it simply as “the pill,” but as Jonathan Eig’s cultural history shows, its genesis was anything but simple.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For much of the first half of the 20th century, women approached Margaret Sanger with a plea: “Do tell me the secret.” They wrote letters, too: “Doctors are men and have not had a baby so they have no pitty [sic] for a poor sick mother.” But she had no secret to not getting pregnant when you didn’t want to. By Sanger’s time, modern medicine had improved upon the crocodile dung ancient Egyptians used as vaginal plugs and the lemon half Casanova recommended as a cervical cap — but not by much. Diaphragms were faulty and ill-used. And condoms depended on men’s will, at a time when a doctor could advise a woman to sleep on her roof to avoid her husband’s advances. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/1012-bks-CARMON-sub/1012-bks-CARMON-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 613, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/1012-bks-CARMON-sub/1012-bks-CARMON-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 613}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/1012-bks-CARMON-sub/1012-bks-CARMON-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1003, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/1012-bks-CARMON-sub/1012-bks-CARMON-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1439, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/1012-bks-CARMON-sub/1012-bks-CARMON-sub-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/1012-bks-CARMON-sub/1012-bks-CARMON-sub-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/1012-bks-CARMON-sub/1012-bks-CARMON-sub-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Masters of Sex', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Masters of Sex', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carmon, Irin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eig, Jonathan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birth Control and Family Planning', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-08T21:22:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Irin Carmon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Irin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carmon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f42ddf55-6905-53be-b4fe-508adcdf74f6', 'word_count': 1250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f42ddf55-6905-53be-b4fe-508adcdf74f6'}, {'abstract': 'Gail Sheehy’s memoir recalls New York magazine and the New Journalism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/12/books/review/gail-sheehy-daring-my-passages-review.html', 'snippet': 'Gail Sheehy’s memoir recalls New York magazine and the New Journalism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After finishing Gail Sheehy’s new memoir, “Daring: My Passages,” I revisited “Passages,” her hugely influential 1976 pop-psychology best seller about the stages of adult life. There I was surprised to be reminded that she had a brother, and that he’d been killed in Vietnam. He appears nowhere in the 484 pages of the new book, despite an early chapter about her childhood and detailed attention to her sister and parents. Of course, no memoir can be entirely comprehensive, particularly one by a person with as storied a life as Sheehy, who, in her spectacularly successful journalistic career, has written about everyone from the Maharishi Mahesh Yogi to Margaret Thatcher and Hillary Clinton. Still, it seems a strange thing to leave out.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/01/books/review/12goldberg/12goldberg-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 404, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/01/books/review/12goldberg/12goldberg-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 404}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/01/books/review/12goldberg/12goldberg-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 689, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/01/books/review/12goldberg/12goldberg-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1378, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/01/books/review/12goldberg/12goldber