ce.html', 'snippet': 'In Jonathan Royse Windham’s “Creatures of Habit,” character sketches converge and interact.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The title of Jonathan Royse Windham’s “Creatures of Habit” pretty much describes the cast. Each of the seven dancers is willfully idiosyncratic, defined by an obsessive tick. There’s Adam (Niall Lessard), who is daydreamy and disoriented. There’s Louis (Elliott Reiland), who has an anger problem, and the sweet Steven (Reed Tankersley), who can’t stop smiling. Carl (Eric Berey) is afraid of everything, and Edwin (Christopher Ralph) has a severe hangover or some other crippling condition. Matthew Marc-Louis Frances Scott IV (Joshua Stansbury) is eager to please, compulsively nodding, while a nameless character (Clinton Edward) tries to mimic his every move.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/09/arts/creatures/creatures-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/09/arts/creatures/creatures-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/09/arts/creatures/creatures-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/09/arts/creatures/creatures-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/09/arts/creatures/creatures-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/09/arts/creatures/creatures-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/09/arts/creatures/creatures-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Personalities Mix and Mingle, or Bump', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Personalities Mix and Mingle, or Bump', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dixon Place', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Windham, Jonathan Royse', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-08T22:10:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Siobhan Burke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Siobhan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/60641140-9501-59dd-b952-7f74c844aeee', 'word_count': 410, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60641140-9501-59dd-b952-7f74c844aeee'}, {'abstract': 'Considered a food desert not so long ago, Detroit is now a culinary oasis. Here’s a look at four of the city’s new restaurants.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/12/travel/in-detroit-revitalizing-taste-by-taste.html', 'snippet': 'Considered a food desert not so long ago, Detroit is now a culinary oasis. Here’s a look at four of the city’s new restaurants.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xadKeeping up with the dining scene in Detroit these days is a full-time job. It seems that every time I return from my travels, a flurry of emails from foodie friends informs me of yet another restaurant or distillery that has opened, or gastronomic “event” I have missed, like a “Book & Bread” dinner at the letterpress studio, Salt & Cedar, where diners enjoy a feast of locally grown ingredients before hand-sewing a journal.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/travel/12-choice-top/12-choice-top-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/12/travel/12-choice-top/12-choice-top-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/travel/12-choice-top/12-choice-top-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/travel/12-choice-top/12-choice-top-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/travel/12-choice-top/12-choice-top-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/12/travel/12-choice-top/12-choice-top-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/travel/12-choice-top/12-choice-top-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Detroit, Revitalizing Taste by Taste', 'kicker': 'Choice Tables', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Revitalizing, Taste by Taste', 'name