ed wind-generating techniques. The natural breeze helped and hindered. A child walking by recovered a rogue ball. Other people stopped to watch.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/09/arts/09GROISMAN2/09GROISMAN2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/09/arts/09GROISMAN2/09GROISMAN2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/09/arts/09GROISMAN2/09GROISMAN2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/09/arts/09GROISMAN2/09GROISMAN2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1368, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/09/arts/09GROISMAN2/09GROISMAN2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/09/arts/09GROISMAN2/09GROISMAN2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/09/arts/09GROISMAN2/09GROISMAN2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Putting One Step Forward Would Be Too Obvious', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Putting One Step Forward Would Be Too Obvious', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Walking', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Groisman, Michel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-09T16:11:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Siobhan Burke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Siobhan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c7e264c-0988-51e8-bad7-a004ee580d9e', 'word_count': 412, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c7e264c-0988-51e8-bad7-a004ee580d9e'}, {'abstract': 'An Australian opera company says it won’t stage “Carmen” because of a sponsorship deal with a government health agency.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/09/where-theres-smoke-theres-no-opera/', 'snippet': 'An Australian opera company says it won’t stage “Carmen” because of a sponsorship deal with a government health agency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An Australian opera company’s decision to ban performances of “Carmen” for two years because it depicts smoking is provoking criticism and debate in Australia. The West Australian Opera has said it will not stage the opera in order to comply with a sponsorship deal with Healthway, a state health agency, The Associated Press reported.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where There’s Smoke, There’s No Opera', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Where There’s Smoke, There’s No Opera', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smoking and Tobacco', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'West Australian Opera', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Australia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-09T16:20:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9b73bcf-21a6-5142-a366-0d108c561469', 'word_count': 233, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9b73bcf-21a6-5142-a366-0d108c561469'}, {'abstract': 'The Dublin company Dead Centre brings its unusual play “Lippy,” based on the true story of four Irish women who committed suicide by starvation, to the Abrons Arts Center.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/12/theater/dead-centre-of-dublin-brings-lippy-to-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'The Dublin company Dead Centre brings its unusual play “Lippy,” based on the true story of four Irish women who committed suicide by starvation, to the Abrons Arts Center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The reviews were mainly good, though you might not know it from some of the quotations: “sometimes confounding,” “dense, difficult, chewy stuff,” “will probably infuriate as many people as it moves.” But “Lippy,” an avant-garde theater piece created by the inventive Dublin company Dead Centre, comes to New York this week following award-winning stops at the Dublin Fringe Festival in 2013 and at the Edinburgh Festival Fringe this year.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/arts/12LIPPY1/12LIPPY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 386, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/12/arts/12LIPPY1/12LIPPY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight':