': 'Zalando SE', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-09T16:21:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Quentin Webb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Quentin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0b4c024-c5e8-5052-9dd8-30b99ffe8c8f', 'word_count': 385, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0b4c024-c5e8-5052-9dd8-30b99ffe8c8f'}, {'abstract': 'The writer and director of “Whiplash” discusses the opening sequence from his film.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/09/damien-chazelle-narrates-a-scene-from-whiplash/', 'snippet': 'The writer and director of “Whiplash” discusses the opening sequence from his film.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It all begins with a drum beat. That sound, over a black screen, is how the writer and director Damien Chazelle chose to start his film “Whiplash,” about a young jazz drummer (Miles Teller) and his not-so-kind instructor (J. K. Simmons). The movie played the New York Film Festival ahead of opening in theaters Oct. 10. In this video, Mr. Chazelle discusses his directorial decisions behind the film’s opening and the mood he wanted to create.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Damien Chazelle Narrates a Scene From ‘Whiplash’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chazelle, Damien', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Simmons, J K', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Teller, Miles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-09T16:21:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7114bf46-a91c-57d3-8ea8-e68cb138f677', 'word_count': 76, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7114bf46-a91c-57d3-8ea8-e68cb138f677'}, {'abstract': 'Lance Coadie Williams discusses his role in the play “Bootycandy.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/12/theater/lance-coadie-williams-on-bootycandy.html', 'snippet': 'Lance Coadie Williams discusses his role in the play “Bootycandy.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The actor Lance Coadie Williams was almost named Harvey, after his father. But then came an intervention from his maternal grandmother, a devotee of the soap opera “The Young and the Restless” who swooned over the character Lance Prentiss, one of the show’s late-’70s heartthrobs. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Happy to Preach to the Audience ', 'kicker': 'Snapshot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Happy to Preach to the Audience', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Piepenburg, Erik', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Lance Coadie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Hara, Robert", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Playwrights Horizons', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Bootycandy (Play)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-09T16:22:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': '