cords of Bessie Smith and Fats Waller? That’s what Molly Ringwald told the audience at Café Carlyle on Wednesday evening in her introduction to a saucy, tough rendition of the 1929 song “Mean to Me” by Fred E. Ahlert and Roy Turk. Delivered as a combative retort, it conveyed the attitude of a woman confidently standing her ground in a domestic conflict.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10RINGWALD/RINGWALD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 833, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10RINGWALD/RINGWALD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 833}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10RINGWALD/RINGWALD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 738, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10RINGWALD/RINGWALD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1999, 'width': 1439, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10RINGWALD/10RINGWALD-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10RINGWALD/10RINGWALD-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10RINGWALD/10RINGWALD-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Decisive, Not Demure, in Her Way With a Songbook', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Decisive, Not Demure, in Her Way With a Songbook', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ringwald, Molly', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-09T21:58:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9bc68ecf-ca24-5337-997d-71b5d4337fe7', 'word_count': 328, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9bc68ecf-ca24-5337-997d-71b5d4337fe7'}, {'abstract': 'India Eisley stars as an avenging killer in the film “Kite,” based on Yasuomi Umetsu’s 1998 anime.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/10/movies/kite-is-based-on-yasuomi-umetsus-1998-anime.html', 'snippet': 'India Eisley stars as an avenging killer in the film “Kite,” based on Yasuomi Umetsu’s 1998 anime.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The dreary action film “Kite” takes place in a dystopian city with an impressive range of accents and acting styles. Children are trafficked in sex slavery by “flesh-cartels.” Sawa (India Eisley) makes it her mission to free them and to avenge her parents’ deaths. She’s aided by her father’s former partner, Karl (Samuel L. Jackson), a cop who supplies her with weapons and plies her with a memory-erasing drug.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10KITE/KITE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10KITE/KITE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10KITE/KITE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 456, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10KITE/KITE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 911, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10KITE/KITE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10KITE/KITE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10KITE/KITE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kill Your Target, Then Forget All About It', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kill Your Target, Then Forget All About It', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ziman, Ralph', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eisley, India', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Kite (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Samuel L', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Umetsu, Yasuomi', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-09T22:01:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Kenigsberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kenigsberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6de8d5bb-33d0-53aa-a5c9-4a277a1ffb4e', 'word_count': 257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6de8d5bb-33d0-53aa-a5c9-4a277a1ffb4e'}, {'abstract': 'The show, which opened at Soho Rep in May, will run as part of the Theater’s new season, and will retain the actress Amber Gray and the director Sarah Benson.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/09/theater-for-a-new-audience-to-present-an-octoroon/', 'snippet': 'The show, which opened at Soho Rep in May, will run as part of the Theater’s new season, and will retain the actress Amber Gray and the director Sarah Benson.', 'lead_paragraph': '“An Octoroon,” Branden Jacobs-Jenkins’s acclaimed play, is coming back in a new venue. The Theater for a New Audience announced Thursday that it is expanding its season with a presentation of the play, which opened at Soho Rep in May.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/05/arts/jpoctoroon/jpoctoroon-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 403, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/05/arts/jpoctoroon/jpoctoroon-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 403}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/05/arts/jpoctoroon/jpoctoroon-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 689, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/05/arts/jpoctoroon/jpoctoroon-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1377, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/05/arts/jpoctoroon/jpoctoroon-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/05/arts/jpoctoroon/jpoctoroon-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/05/arts/jpoctoroon/jpoctoroon-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Theater for a New Audience to Present ‘An Octoroon’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘An Octoroon’ Returns in February', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jacobs-Jenkins, Branden', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Theater for a New Audience', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-09T22:03:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/127a526d-cbd0-5510-9cd2-0801ca036555', 'word_count': 275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/127a526d-cbd0-5510-9cd2-0801ca036555'}, {'abstract': 'The Assembly’s “That Poor Dream” is an expressive, uneven update of Charles Dickens’s “Great Expectations.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/10/theater/that-poor-dream-an-update-of-great-expectations.html', 'snippet': 'The Assembly’s “That Poor Dream” is an expressive, uneven update of Charles Dickens’s “Great Expectations.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Metro-North operates the Hudson line, the Harlem line, the New Haven line. But the Assembly’s “That Poor Dream,” an expressive, uneven update of Charles Dickens’s “Great Expectations,” is set on twistier rails; call it the flashback line. Pip (Edward Bauer), a college senior, is chugging back toward Miss Havisham’s Connecticut manse when he meets Magwitch (Terrell Wheeler), the felon who has anonymously backed Pip’s hefty trust fund. As the train clatters on, Pip journeys into the past, with scenes recollecting Magwitch’s hidden influence. “It was me,” Magwitch explains. “I lived rough so that you could live smooth.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10POORDREAM/POORDREAM-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10POORDREAM/POORDREAM-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10POORDREAM/POORDREAM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10POORDREAM/POORDREAM-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10POORDREAM/10POORDREAM-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10POORDREAM/10POORDREAM-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10POORDREAM/10POORDREAM-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Train to Fairfield, Revelations', 'kicker': 'Theater Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On the Train to Fairfield, Revelations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Assembly Theater Project, The', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dickens, Charles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chayes, Jess', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bauer, Edward', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wheeler, Terrell', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'That Poor Dream (Play)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-09T22:05:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexis Soloski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soloski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/504b49c9-b82b-5668-8d41-3d8f23c1b775', 'word_count': 341, 'uri': 'nyt://article/504b49c9-b82b-5668-8d41-3d8f23c1b775'}, {'abstract': 'A new initiative in jazz programming, it will bring jazz to audiences through its website, video and webcasts, as well as on radio.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/09/jazz-night-in-america-makes-its-debut/', 'snippet': 'A new initiative in jazz programming, it will bring jazz to audiences through its website, video and webcasts, as well as on radio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Featuring interviews with jazz greats, and concert video-casts from festivals and clubs nationwide “Jazz Night in America,” a new initiative in jazz programming, began Wednesday. A joint endeavor of National Public Radio, Jazz at Lincoln Center and the Newark radio station WBGO (88.3 FM), it will bring jazz to audiences through its website, video and webcasts, as well as on radio.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Jazz Night in America’ Makes Its Debut', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bringing Jazz To the People', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Armand, Gabrielle', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marsalis, Wynton', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McBride, Christian', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jazz at Lincoln Center', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-09T22:06:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41391fd8-2ac0-5968-8ee8-f21d1fc20861', 'word_count': 315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41391fd8-2ac0-5968-8ee8-f21d1fc20861'}, {'abstract': '“Kill the Messenger” tells the story of a real investigative reporter, Gary Webb, whose reporting about the C.I.A., the contras and drug dealers came under attack.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/10/movies/kill-the-messenger-a-film-about-the-reporter-gary-webb.html', 'snippet': '“Kill the Messenger” tells the story of a real investigative reporter, Gary Webb, whose reporting about the C.I.A., the contras and drug dealers came under attack.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At some point in almost every movie set in the world of journalism, someone drops a big speech about the Truth. “Nothing’s riding on this,” an editor growls at Woodward and Bernstein in the film “All the President’s Men” amid the horror-flick gloom, “except the First Amendment of the Constitution, freedom of the press and maybe the future of the country.” The rare exception to this big speech rule may be “His Girl Friday,” the greatest movie ever set in a newsroom, which ditched sanctimony for the syncopated report of wits firing at each other like Tommy guns.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/KILLTHEMESSENGER/KILLTHEMESSENGER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/KILLTHEMESSENGER/KILLTHEMESSENGER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/KILLTHEMESSENGER/KILLTHEMESSENGER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/KILLTHEMESSENGER/KILLTHEMESSENGER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/KILLTHEMESSENGER/KILLTHEMESSENGER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/KILLTHEMESSENGER/KILLTHEMESSENGER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/KILLTHEMESSENGER/KILLTHEMESSENGER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Reporter in the Crosshairs', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Reporter in the Crosshairs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuesta, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Renner, Jeremy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Webb, Gary', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Landesman, Peter', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Michael Kenneth', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vega, Paz', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Kill the Messenger (Movie)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schou, Nick', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garcia, Andy (1956- )', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-09T22:08:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/70e53d45-7072-5bd5-b4b3-882d4e5bdb0b', 'word_count': 618, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70e53d45-7072-5bd5-b4b3-882d4e5bdb0b'}, {'abstract': 'The Guggenheim’s “Zero: Countdown to Tomorrow, 1950s-60s” is an alternately dazzling and thin walk-in history lesson about the three Zero artists of postwar Germany.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/10/arts/zero-a-look-at-a-movement-at-the-guggenheim.html', 'snippet': 'The Guggenheim’s “Zero: Countdown to Tomorrow, 1950s-60s” is an alternately dazzling and thin walk-in history lesson about the three Zero artists of postwar Germany.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the wake of World War II, many European artists felt an urgent need to start from scratch, but few summed up the ambition as succinctly as three young German artists who called themselves Zero. The word, denoting a location somewhere behind square one, was selected in 1957 by Heinz Mack and Otto Piene, painters who had met at the Düsseldorf Art Academy. They were shortly joined by Günther Uecker, although he didn’t formally become the third and final member until 1961. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '3 Men and a Posse, Chasing Newness', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '3 Men and a Posse, Chasing Newness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Guggenheim, Solomon R, Museum', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Piene, Otto (1928-2014)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Uecker, Gunther', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mack, Heinz', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-09T22:10:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c5426a2-7608-5685-9efd-ae4a52ee2e86', 'word_count': 1168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c5426a2-7608-5685-9efd-ae4a52ee2e86'}, {'abstract': '“I Am Ali,” a documentary about the boxing legend, covers some familiar territory but offers enticing vintage clips.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/10/movies/i-am-ali-a-documentary-about-the-boxer.html', 'snippet': '“I Am Ali,” a documentary about the boxing legend, covers some familiar territory but offers enticing vintage clips.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every Muhammad Ali documentary has its own hook. The Oscar-winning “When We Were Kings” (1996) chronicled his 1974 Rumble in the Jungle with George Foreman. “The Trials of Muhammad Ali” (2013) zeroed in on his politics and refusal to fight in Vietnam. “I Am Ali” focuses on his personal life, showcasing private audio recordings he made for posterity. It’s divided into chapters by interview: “The Brother’s Story,” for Rahman Ali; “The Trainer’s Story,” for Angelo Dundee; “The Rookie’s Story,” for Mike Tyson, and so on.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10IAMALI/IAMALI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 797, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10IAMALI/IAMALI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 797}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10IAMALI/IAMALI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 771, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10IAMALI/IAMALI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1542, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10IAMALI/IAMALI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10IAMALI/IAMALI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10IAMALI/IAMALI-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Memory, Forever Floating Like a Butterfly', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Memory, Forever Floating Like a Butterfly', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lewins, Clare', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALI, MUHAMMAD', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boxing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'I Am Ali (Movie)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-09T22:12:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Kenigsberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kenigsberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a04789a-15b1-5de7-a380-52dbb976de93', 'word_count': 256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a04789a-15b1-5de7-a380-52dbb976de93'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Benning increases the optical pop of her relief-like paintings in her show at Callicoon Fine Arts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/10/arts/design/sadie-benning-patterns.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Benning increases the optical pop of her relief-like paintings in her show at Callicoon Fine Arts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sadie Benning continues to explore the possibilities of her eye-catching, distinctively physical relief-like paintings, heightening the optical pop of her latest efforts with a wall-to-wall Kelly green carpet. As before, every part of each work is a separate entity, cut from and then fit back onto a larger sheet of composition board, after each has been covered with resin and color and sanded down. The effect seems firm yet also slightly upholstered, finished yet full of odd irregularities — and these tensions draw the eye.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10SADIEBENNING-GALLERY3/SADIEBENNING-GALLERY3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 813, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10SADIEBENNING-GALLERY3/SADIEBENNING-GALLERY3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 813}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10SADIEBENNING-GALLERY3/SADIEBENNING-GALLERY3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 756, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10SADIEBENNING-GALLERY3/SADIEBENNING-GALLERY3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1833, 'width': 1353, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10SADIEBENNING-GALLERY3/SADIEBENNING-GALLERY3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10SADIEBENNING-GALLERY3/SADIEBENNING-GALLERY3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10SADIEBENNING-GALLERY3/SADIEBENNING-GALLERY3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sadie Benning: ‘Patterns’', 'kicker': 'Art in Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sadie Benning: ‘Patterns’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benning, Sadie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-09T22:13:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b431cac2-d6bb-5899-af07-d627ca126911', 'word_count': 300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b431cac2-d6bb-5899-af07-d627ca126911'}, {'abstract': 'Discussing how to handle the bad behavior of a son and his friends, what to do about surname coincidences and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/12/fashion/social-qs-whats-in-a-name.html', 'snippet': 'Discussing how to handle the bad behavior of a son and his friends, what to do about surname coincidences and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I discovered some text messages on my teenage son’s phone that implicated him and his friends in drug use and shoplifting. Rather than telling the boys’ parents what I knew, I suggested they check their sons’ phones. Instead, the parents asked the boys whether there was anything on their phones they wanted to tell them about. The boys denied all wrongdoing. Now, my son is being ostracized as a snitch, and the other boys’ bad behavior continues. Your thoughts?', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 599}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Parental Fortitude', 'kicker': 'Social Q’s', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Parental Fortitude', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-09T22:18:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip Galanes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Galanes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bf27814-7cee-5a01-acbe-519c23b9d8b6', 'word_count': 824, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bf27814-7cee-5a01-acbe-519c23b9d8b6'}, {'abstract': '“Henri Matisse: The Cut-Outs,” at the Museum of Modern Art, presents about 100 painted-paper works, produced during a difficult era for the artist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/10/arts/design/henri-matisse-the-cut-outs-a-victory-lap-at-moma.html', 'snippet': '“Henri Matisse: The Cut-Outs,” at the Museum of Modern Art, presents about 100 painted-paper works, produced during a difficult era for the artist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Near the end of his life, Henri Matisse’s preferred attire was evening wear, by which I mean pajamas. They were the ideal uniform for the invalid, insomniac night worker and waking dreamer he had become in the decade before his death at age 84 in 1954. And it is the dreamer and worker we meet in “Henri Matisse: The Cut-Outs,” a marvelous, victory-lap show that arrives from London, where it drew more than 500,000 viewers at the Tate Modern last summer, and opens in a larger form at the Museum of Modern Art on Sunday. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wisps From an Old Man’s Dreams ', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wisps From an Old Man’s Dreams', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cotter, Holland', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Modern Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Matisse, Henri', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-09T22:25:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8dbd919f-1762-5178-b455-1f00620a251f', 'word_count': 1506, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8dbd919f-1762-5178-b455-1f00620a251f'}, {'abstract': 'A guide to cultural and recreational events on Long Island.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/12/nyregion/events-on-long-island-for-oct-12-18-2014.html', 'snippet': 'A guide to cultural and recreational events on Long Island.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A guide to cultural and recreational events on Long Island. Items for the calendar should be sent at least three weeks in advance to lical@nytimes.com.', 'print_section': 'LI', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/nyregion/12HILI3/12HILI3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/12/nyregion/12HILI3/12HILI3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/nyregion/12HILI3/12HILI3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/nyregion/12HILI3/12HILI3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}