4f89-a2d1-570d-a3d6-7ca220043a87', 'word_count': 485, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17f74f89-a2d1-570d-a3d6-7ca220043a87'}, {'abstract': '“The Disappearance of Eleanor Rigby: Him/Her,” by Ned Benson, is actually two movies in one release, a variation on an earlier film by the director.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/10/movies/the-disappearance-of-eleanor-rigby-him-her.html', 'snippet': '“The Disappearance of Eleanor Rigby: Him/Her,” by Ned Benson, is actually two movies in one release, a variation on an earlier film by the director.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If “The Disappearance of Eleanor Rigby: Him/Her” sounds familiar, there’s a reason. Last month, a variation on the same story — and featuring the same actors, locations and so on — opened under the title “The Disappearance of Eleanor Rigby.” Running a bit over two hours, that “Eleanor Rigby” tells the sad tale of a broken marriage from the blended perspectives of a husband and wife after the death of their baby. The director and writer Ned Benson calls this first movie “Them.” “Him/Her” is actually two movies that are being released in a single 3 hour and 16 minute heave that splits the drama in half, with each part centered on one spouse’s point of view. (Their order depends on when and where you see the movie — you could see “Her/Him”.) ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10DISAPPEARANCE/DISAPPEARANCE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 289, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10DISAPPEARANCE/DISAPPEARANCE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 289}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/arts/10DISAPPEARANCE/DISAPPEARANCE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 494, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_na