lue': 'Freedom of the Press', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sankei', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kato, Tatsuya', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Park Geun-hye', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-10T00:52:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martin Fackler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fackler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3b8fee2-c77c-5bb8-949e-7adea0e5e3bb', 'word_count': 344, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3b8fee2-c77c-5bb8-949e-7adea0e5e3bb'}, {'abstract': 'By failing to order a fair trial for five men convicted of rape, the new president sent a signal that a corrupt judicial system is good enough.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/10/opinion/grim-executions-in-afghanistan.html', 'snippet': 'By failing to order a fair trial for five men convicted of rape, the new president sent a signal that a corrupt judicial system is good enough.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By any standard, the trial of five men convicted of a gang rape in Afghanistan was a travesty. That a group of assailants robbed and sexually assaulted four women on the outskirts of Kabul last August was not in doubt. But the suspects testified that they confessed under torture; the defense lawyers representing them received death threats; the victims were paraded before journalists, exposing them to shame. Even before the men had been convicted, Hamid Karzai, the president who recently left office, promised their execution. He signed the death orders on Sept. 27, his last weekend in office. ', '