ion_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/84518d75-81d2-5d66-a0d7-23d450e5d6cf', 'word_count': 284, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84518d75-81d2-5d66-a0d7-23d450e5d6cf'}, {'abstract': 'A federal judge in Texas was scathing in striking down the state’s voter identification law.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/politics/first-draft/2014/10/10/texas-judge-recites-states-history-of-discrimination/', 'snippet': 'A federal judge in Texas was scathing in striking down the state’s voter identification law.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A federal judge provided a scathing reminder of Texas’ history of discrimination against blacks and Hispanics in striking down the state’s voter identification law on Thursday evening. She had plenty of room to do it: Her opinion ran to almost 150 pages.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Texas Judge Recites State’s History of Discrimination', 'kicker': 'First Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voter Registration and Requirements', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ramos, Nelva Gonzales', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-10T04:44:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manny Fernandez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fernandez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af9175d7-6c1e-5481-a145-31fbfd593685', 'word_count': 274, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af9175d7-6c1e-5481-a145-31fbfd593685'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/10/arts/television/whats-on-tv-friday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '9 P.M. (13) THE NANCE Nathan Lane, whose performance earned him a Tony nomination, portrays Chauncey Miles, a closeted burlesque actor in 1930s New York who plays a caricature of his clandestine self onstage — the only place he seems truly happy. Jonny Orsini is the rough-edged Ned, whom Chauncey loves in secret, in Douglas Carter Beane’s drama, directed by Jack O’Brien and produced by Lincoln Center Theater. But Chauncey’s private and personal lives collide when the city cracks down on burlesque houses, especially those featuring the effeminate, innuendo-rich characters that he specializes in. “Mr. Beane, Mr. O’Brien and their creative associates have obviously done their research,” Ben Brantley wrote in The New York Times about the play, which is seen here in a “Live From Lincoln Center” presentation, directed for the screen by Matthew Diamond. “As a document of both a vanished form of theater and forbidden love in an age of repression, ‘The Nance’ — which has been designed with beautifully shabby flair by a team that includes John Lee Beatty (sets) and Ann Roth (costumes) — has been shaped with imaginative empathy.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Friday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Friday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-10T05:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b3d9413-208d-51f8-a226-52e263b6c785', 'word_count': 640, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b3d9413-208d-51f8-a226-52e263b6c785'}, {'abstract': 'A new website offers a look at the Chinese government as it wants to be seen. Meanwhile, a business group has set up an “economic reform scorecard” to track some of its promises.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/10/two-new-views-of-the-chinese-state-authorized-and-not/', 'snippet': 'A new website offers a look at the Chinese government as it wants to be seen. Meanwhile, a business group has set up an “economic reform scorecard” to track some of its promises.', 'lead_paragraph': 'English.gov.cn, a new English-language website set up by the Chinese State Council, the apex of the government that is headed by Prime Minister Li Keqiang, offers the world an English-language look at the work of the state\xa0— as the state wants to be seen.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/09/world/09sino-WEBSITE/09sino-WEBSITE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/09/world/09sino-WEBSITE/09sino-WEBSITE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/09/world/09sino-WEBSITE/09sino-WEBSITE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/09/world/09sino-WEBSITE/09sino-WEBSITE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/09/world/09sino-WEBSITE/09sino-WEBSITE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'h