:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David W. Chen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73d27bd9-96d8-5d41-94d0-142967210fee', 'word_count': 1546, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73d27bd9-96d8-5d41-94d0-142967210fee'}, {'abstract': 'For an optimistic contingent of buyers and renters willing to coexist with cacophony, the incentives outweigh the inconveniences.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/12/realestate/manhattan-apartments-next-to-construction-sites.html', 'snippet': 'For an optimistic contingent of buyers and renters willing to coexist with cacophony, the incentives outweigh the inconveniences.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If all the world is a stage, the name of the show running in an endless loop in Manhattan, Shakespearean in proportion and on occasion fraught with drama, is “The Construction Zone.” Sometimes the zone doubles as the view from a previously prized bedroom window; sometimes the zone is the insult that assaults the eye and ear right outside a formerly serene residential lobby. Much like an over-the-top new neighbor, it is hard to ignore.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Investing in Future Quiet ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Investing in Future Quiet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Finn, Robin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Building (Construction)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hudson Yards (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Second Avenue (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hudson Square (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-10T15:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Finn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e324736e-6623-5833-9c2d-50bfe28660dc', 'word_count': 2389, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e324736e-6623-5833-9c2d-50bfe28660dc'}, {'abstract': 'The Pop Art–obsessed designer has created clothing for the staff at Kappo Masa, the gallerist’s new eatery created in collaboration with the Japanese chef.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/10/kappo-masa-larry-gagosian-masa-takayama-new-restaurant-lisa-perry-uniforms/', 'snippet': 'The Pop Art–obsessed designer has created clothing for the staff at Kappo Masa, the gallerist’s new eatery created in collaboration with the Japanese chef.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lisa Perry has never been shy about her love for, or her vast collection of, art: She’s got Jeff Koons on her terrace, Damien Hirst in her bedroom and Lichtenstein on her namesake label’s T-shirts. Now, the designer has collaborated with her friend and art dealer Larry Gagosian to design the staff uniforms for Kappo Masa, a new restaurant opening in partnership with the chef Masayoshi Takayama on the Upper East Side.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/10/t-magazine/10masa-bobb/10masa-bobb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': '