ppet': 'David Caspe, the creator of “Marry Me,” told executives in pitch meetings that he planned to propose to Casey Wilson to help sell the show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Last year, a few weeks after ABC canceled “Happy Endings,” a sitcom with a cult following but weak ratings, its creator, David Caspe, needed a new premise. Pitching season was beginning, and this would be a chance to get another show on the air. He looked no further than a “Happy Endings” cast member, Casey Wilson, at the time his girlfriend and now his wife, and the path it took to merge their lives. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/arts/12MARRY1/12MARRY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 892, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/12/arts/12MARRY1/12MARRY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 892}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/arts/12MARRY1/12MARRY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 689, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/arts/12MARRY1/12MARRY1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1378, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/arts/12MARRY1/12MARRY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/12/arts/12MARRY1/12MARRY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/a