e': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/1012-bks-SHORT/1012-bks-SHORT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/1012-bks-SHORT/1012-bks-SHORT-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Location, Location', 'kicker': 'The Shortlist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Location, Location', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daum, Meghan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Makkai, Rebecca', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coll, Susan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thornton, Margaret Bradham', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Charleston (Book)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-10T16:35:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Meghan Daum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Meghan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Daum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4636284-e34f-5074-a5bd-7ef66f1ce835', 'word_count': 932, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4636284-e34f-5074-a5bd-7ef66f1ce835'}, {'abstract': 'S. C. Gwynne, whose “Rebel Yell,” about Stonewall Jackson, is No. 10 on the hardcover nonfiction list, said he was interested in his subject’s transformation from eccentric college professor to Confederate leader.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/19/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'S. C. Gwynne, whose “Rebel Yell,” about Stonewall Jackson, is No. 10 on the hardcover nonfiction list, said he was interested in his subject’s transformation from eccentric college professor to Confederate leader.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Blue and Gray and Read All Over: The Civil War ended almost a century and a half ago. But it’s still going strong on the best-seller lists — in 2014 alone, the conflict has appeared on the graphic books list, the hardcover fiction list, the children’s picture books list and the e-books nonfiction list. (One digital best seller, Tim Rowland’s “Strange and Obscure Stories of the Civil War,” features two photo illustrations on the cover: one of a bearded lady with the caption “Sarah Emma Edmonds was a complicated girl,” the other of a battle scene with the caption “Unleashing a maelstrom of fiery hell at a pheasant.” I believe I am warming to e-books.) The latest title to join this march is S. C. Gwynne’s “Rebel Yell: The Violence, Passion, and Redemption of Stonewall Jackson,” new on the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 10. Gwynne — who spent more than a year on the paperback list with his previous book, “Empire of the Summer Moon,” about the Comanche chief Quanah Parker — told The Austin American-Statesman he was especially interested in how Thomas Jackson went, in 14 months, from being an eccentric and unsuccessful college professor to leading the Rebel forces in the Shenandoah Valley campaign. “What fascinates me about Jackson is the idea of transformation,” he said. “He became the most famous man of the war.” Gwynne knows something about personal transformation. In the early 1980s he was working as a banker and dreaming of a writing career when his wife went on the game show “Tic-Tac-Dough” and won $60,000. “That money lasted for years,” he told The American-Statesman. “So I quit banking.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12gwynne/12gwynne-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12gwynne/12gwynne-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12gwynne/12gwynne-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12gwynne/12gwynne-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12gwynne/12gwynne-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gwynne, S C', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-10T16:35:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68c9d79a-f1ed-59c2-a186-862ced72a9bf', 'word_count': 496, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68c9d79a-f1ed-59c2-a186-862ced72a9bf'}, {'abstract': 'A look back at The Times’s coverage of Virginia Woolf.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/12/books/review/worthwhile-woolf.html', 'snippet': 'A look back at The Times’s coverage of Virginia Woolf.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New York Times and Virginia Woolf didn’t get off to a great start. In an unsigned review of her first novel, “The Voyage Out,” in the daily Times in 1920, the critic found, “aside from a certain cleverness,” “little in this offering to make it stand out from the ruck of mediocre novels which make far less literary pretension.” The review did note “a possibility of something worthwhile from the same pen in the future.” Indeed.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/1012-bks-OPENBOOK/1012-bks-OPENBOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 264, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/1012-bks-OPENBOOK/1012-bks-OPENBOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 264}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/1012-bks-OPENBOOK/1012-bks-OPENBOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 449, 'width': 1020, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/1012-bks-OPENBOOK/1012-bks-OPENBOOK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 449, 'width': 1020, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/1012-bks-OPENBOOK/1012-bks-OPENBOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/1012-bks-OPENBOOK/1012-bks-OPENBOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/1012-bks-OPENBOOK/1012-bks-OPENBOOK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Worthwhile Woolf', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Worthwhile Woolf', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-10T16:35:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a70a9c8d-c33b-5c3c-8a26-e6f2e15f0196', 'word_count': 477, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a70a9c8d-c33b-5c3c-8a26-e6f2e15f0196'}, {'abstract': 'A plain, poor British teenager transforms herself into a music critic in Caitlin Moran’s “How to Build a Girl.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/12/books/review/caitlin-morans-how-to-build-a-girl.html', 'snippet': 'A plain, poor British teenager transforms herself into a music critic in Caitlin Moran’s “How to Build a Girl.”', 'lead_paragraph': '“The thing I want to be hasn’t been invented,” laments 14-year-old Johanna Morrigan, the narrator of Caitlin Moran’s new novel, “How to Build a Girl.” She does know a few of the details: Her future self lives in London, is super hot and has an Oscar Wilde-like ability to light up a room with her wit and charm. She has tacked magazine photos of her celebrity crushes and quotations from her literary idols to her walls, but they seem only to remind her of how far she has to go. She’s a chubby teenager whose large family lives on government assistance in a working-class city in middle England. Her most fulfilling sexual relationship has been with the handle of her hairbrush. She isn’t even sure where to begin.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12Friedman-SUB/1012-bks-Friedman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 702, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12Friedman-SUB/1012-bks-Friedman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 702}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12Friedman-SUB/1012-bks-Friedman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 876, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12Friedman-SUB/1012-bks-Friedman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2006, 'width': 1715, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12Friedman-SUB/1012-bks-Friedman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12Friedman-SUB/1012-bks-Friedman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12Friedman-SUB/1012-bks-Friedman-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'About a Girl', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'About a Girl', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'How to Build a Girl (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moran, Caitlin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-10T16:38:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Friedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/248a5dde-a2f2-5cf2-9fac-80275ca468b7', 'word_count': 1171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/248a5dde-a2f2-5cf2-9fac-80275ca468b7'}, {'abstract': 'A playwright on how to write and create when life — and children — constantly intervene.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/12/books/review/sarah-ruhls-100-essays-i-dont-have-time-to-write.html', 'snippet': 'A playwright on how to write and create when life — and children — constantly intervene.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “A Room of One’s Own,” Virginia Woolf considers the difficulties facing the unhistoried, culturally marginalized woman writer: “Perhaps the first thing she would find, setting pen to paper, was that there was no common sentence ready for her use. . . . Moreover, a book is not made of sentences laid end to end, but of sentences built, if an image helps, into arcades or domes. And this shape too has been made by men out of their own needs for their own uses.” If women’s literature were to have a future, she continues, there is a “great part which must be played in that future . . . by physical conditions. The book has somehow to be adapted to the body, and at a venture one would say that women’s books should be shorter, more concentrated, than those of men, and framed so that they do not need long hours of steady and uninterrupted work. For interruptions there will always be.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12CUSK/12cusk-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 850, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12CUSK/12cusk-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 850}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12CUSK/12cusk-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 723, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12CUSK/12cusk-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2000, 'width': 1412, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12CUSK/12cusk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12CUSK/12cusk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12CUSK/12cusk-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Other Self', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Other Self', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cusk, Rachel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ruhl, Sarah', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-10T16:38:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Cusk', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cusk', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c23ecf06-0e7c-542a-aaaa-8057f2bd1a34', 'word_count': 943, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c23ecf06-0e7c-542a-aaaa-8057f2bd1a34'}, {'abstract': 'Sheila Weller chronicles the triumphs and setbacks of three powerful women in the male-dominated realm of network news.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/12/books/review/the-news-sorority-by-sheila-weller.html', 'snippet': 'Sheila Weller chronicles the triumphs and setbacks of three powerful women in the male-dominated realm of network news.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the early 1950s, the idea that a woman could become a TV news reporter, let alone an anchor, was considered a pipe dream. Even as late as 1970, the president of NBC News, Reuven Frank, warned that American audiences weren’t inclined to get the news from “a woman’s voice.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12bolonik-1/12bolonik-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12bolonik-1/12bolonik-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12bolonik-1/12bolonik-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12bolonik-1/12bolonik-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12bolonik-1/12bolonik-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12bolonik-1/12bolonik-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/12/books/review/12bolonik-1/12bolonik-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Prime Time', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Prime Time', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weller, Sheila', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Couric, Katie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sawyer, Diane', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'AMANPOUR, CHRISTIANE', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-10T16:38:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By