: 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-10T23:09:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Josh Katz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Josh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Katz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6fc8c52b-b7b7-5f31-bda0-0f8e8078ee82', 'word_count': 284, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6fc8c52b-b7b7-5f31-bda0-0f8e8078ee82'}, {'abstract': 'The Nobel Peace Prize awarded to Ms. Yousafzai, 17, underscores the disproportionate expectations surrounding a rights activist who has yet to graduate from high school.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/11/world/asia/malala-yousafzai-youngest-nobel-peace-prize-winner-adds-to-her-achievements-and-expectations.html', 'snippet': 'The Nobel Peace Prize awarded to Ms. Yousafzai, 17, underscores the disproportionate expectations surrounding a rights activist who has yet to graduate from high school.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though Malala Yousafzai is 17, she does not use Facebook or even a mobile phone lest she lose focus on her studies. She spent her summer vacation flying to Nigeria to campaign for the release of girls kidnapped by the extremist Islamist group Boko Haram, but also worrying about her grades, which recently took a worrisome dip. She confronted President Obama about American drone policy in a meeting last year, but finds it difficult to befriend her fellow students in Birmingham, England.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/11/world/11malala/11malala-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'imag