', 'value': 'Soderbergh, Steven', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-13T14:14:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brent Staples', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brent', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Staples', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6412a036-0edf-5c56-9167-d2ff852a0368', 'word_count': 645, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6412a036-0edf-5c56-9167-d2ff852a0368'}, {'abstract': 'In a third straight victory this year, the conservative prime minister’s party won control of all county assemblies and all but one of the largest cities, including the capital.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/14/world/europe/viktor-orban-hungary-elections.html', 'snippet': 'In a third straight victory this year, the conservative prime minister’s party won control of all county assemblies and all but one of the largest cities, including the capital.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Prime Minister Viktor Orban continued his steady consolidation of power in Hungary in local and municipal elections on Sunday with his governing Fidesz party winning control of all county assemblies and all but one of the largest cities, including the capital, Budapest.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/14/world/14hungary/14hungary-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/14/world/14hungary/14hungary-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 395}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/14/world/14hungary