260-5ae4-9192-dc66431c8f29'}, {'abstract': 'Twenty-seven wrongful death claims filed with the company’s victim compensation program have been deemed eligible for payment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/14/business/death-total-tied-to-gm-flaw-rises-to-27-twice-first-estimate.html', 'snippet': 'Twenty-seven wrongful death claims filed with the company’s victim compensation program have been deemed eligible for payment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The death toll linked to General Motors’ defective ignition switch has now more than doubled from the company’s original estimate. Twenty-seven wrongful death claims filed with the company’s victim compensation program have been deemed eligible for payment, according to a weekly update posted on the program’s website on Monday. For months after it began recalling cars with the faulty switch, G.M. estimated that 13 people had died in accidents tied to the defect, though executives held out the possibility that the number could rise. The faulty switch can cause power to cut out in a moving car, disabling air bags, power steering and power brakes. This year, G.M. has recalled 2.6 million cars that could have the defect. As of Friday, the program had received a total of 1,371 claims, 178 of them for wrongful deaths, and had accepted a total of 52 claims for payment. Many of the others are still under review. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Death Total Tied to G.M. Flaw Rises to 27, Twice First Estimate', 'kicker': 'Business Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Death Total Tied To G.M. Flaw Rises To 27, Twice First Estimate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-13T22:20:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilary Stout', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stout', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/92a54574-7c48-5816-a25e-9a2128604993', 'word_count': 154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92a54574-7c48-5816-a25e-9a2128604993'}, {'abstract': 'Dr. Jim Yong Kim, the World Bank president, has driven the normally lumbering bank to act on Ebola with uncharacteristic speed and become a key figure in the crisis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/14/science/a-bank-chief-makes-ebola-his-mission.html', 'snippet': 'Dr. Jim Yong Kim, the World Bank president, has driven the normally lumbering bank to act on Ebola with uncharacteristic speed and become a key figure in the crisis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Some of the world’s most influential health officials gathered on the 12th floor of the World Bank’s glass-sheathed headquarters here last month to talk about Ebola. The failure of the international effort to contain its spread hung heavy.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/14/science/14JPJIMK/14JPJIMK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/14/science/14JPJIMK/14JPJIMK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/14/science/14JPJIMK/14JPJIMK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/14/science/14JPJIMK/14JPJIMK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 