 'subject', 'value': 'Ebola Virus', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chappatte, Patrick', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Airport Security', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-14T11:49:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3cf23d85-4aaa-531c-b0e5-8520f7f55c05', 'word_count': 7, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3cf23d85-4aaa-531c-b0e5-8520f7f55c05'}, {'abstract': 'JPMorgan Chase posts a third-quarter profit. | Warren E. Buffett promotes the Berkshire Hathaway brand. | Calculating the cost of Ebola | Derivatives change only goes so far.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/10/14/morning-agenda-jpmorgan-swings-to-a-profit/', 'snippet': 'JPMorgan Chase posts a third-quarter profit. | Warren E. Buffett promotes the Berkshire Hathaway brand. | Calculating the cost of Ebola | Derivatives change only goes so far.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JPMorgan Chase swung to a profit in the third quarter, but the announcement on Tuesday morning was marred by an early release of the results on a third-party website, DealBook’s Nathaniel Popper writes. The bank earned $5.57 billion in the quarter, or $1.36 a share, compared with a loss of $380 million in the quarter a year earlier, when the bank booked $7.2 billion in legal costs. Net revenue rose 5 percent, to $25.2 billion, from the third quarter of 2013. The earnings fell short of analysts’ estimates of $1.38 a share, according to a survey by Thomson Reuters. Earnings were reduced by $1.1 billion in legal expenses, or 26 cents a share, after taxes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: JPMorgan Swings to a Profit', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-14T11:58:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6812ea5-1cd4-52e2-a274-5845639eb675', 'word_count': 2416, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6812ea5-1cd4-52e2-a274-5845639eb675'}, {'abstract': 'Lively, vivid language is always welcome, in features or news stories. But slang, colloquialisms and insider jargon are often jarring and inappropriate in straight-news contexts.', 'web_url': 'https://afterdeadline.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/14/the-slang-patrol-3/', 'snippet': 'Lively, vivid language is always welcome, in features or news stories. But slang, colloquialisms and insider jargon are often jarring and inappropriate in straight-news contexts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lively, vivid language is always welcome, in features or news stories. But slang, colloquialisms and insider jargon are often jarring and inappropriate in straight-news contexts. Even in features, slang may seem trite or forced rather than fresh and compelling.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Slang Patrol', 'kicker': 'After Deadline', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-14T12:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Times Topics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip B. Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5fb5c86-36c2-5f12-8057-8c7d8dc4cf75', 'word_count': 1308, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5fb5c86-36c2-5f12-8057-8c7d8dc4cf75'}, {'abstract': 'The “Saturday Night Live” alumna in a scene from the comedy about political sex scandals.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/14/in-performance-rachel-dratch-of-tail-spin/', 'snippet': 'The “Saturday Night Live” alumna in a scene from the comedy about political sex scandals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week’s video features the former “Saturday Night Live” actress Rachel Dratch in a scene from “Tail! Spin!,” a political comedy that draws on interviews, emails and speeches by and about male politicians involved in sex scandals. In this scene, taken from a Barbara Walters interview, Ms. Dratch plays three characters: Ms. Walters; Jenny Sanford, the wife of the former South Carolina governor Mark Sanford; and Mr. Sanford’s Argentine girlfriend, María Belén Chapur, with whom he admitted having an extramarital affair. (Last month Mr. Sanford announced the breakup of his engagement to Ms. Chapur.) Written by Mario Correa and directed by Dan Knechtges, “Tail! Spin!” is at the Lynn Redgrave Theater at Culture Project.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Performance: Rachel Dratch of ‘Tail! Spin!’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chapur, Maria Belen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dratch, Rachel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sanford, Jenny', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sanford, Mark', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walters, Barbara', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-14T12:00:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None,