'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palmer, Carson', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-14T21:43:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Raducanu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Raducanu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/62093150-9eff-571d-a33b-a5fad00b4056', 'word_count': 1044, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62093150-9eff-571d-a33b-a5fad00b4056'}, {'abstract': 'Volker Schlöndorff’s “Diplomacy” imagines an evening’s conversation that saved Paris from being blown up by a retreating Nazi army in 1944.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/15/arts/diplomacy-directed-by-volker-schlondorff.html', 'snippet': 'Volker Schlöndorff’s “Diplomacy” imagines an evening’s conversation that saved Paris from being blown up by a retreating Nazi army in 1944.', 'lead_paragraph': '“We’ll always have Paris,” Rick promised Ilsa at the end of “Casablanca,” recalling a romantic idyll cruelly interrupted by the German occupation. In the real world, in the late summer of 1944, as the Allied armies drew closer, the Nazis decided that if they could not have Paris, nobody else would, either. Hitler ordered the complete destruction of the city. Bridges and monuments — the Opera, the Eiffel Tower, the Louvre — were to be blown up; he hoped to leave tens of thousands dead amid the rubble and erase centuries of art, architecture and civilization.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/15/arts/DIPLOMACY/DIPLOMACY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/201