oll, to Oscar’s venerable head.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/arts/19KITTREDGE/19KITTREDGE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/19/arts/19KITTREDGE/19KITTREDGE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/arts/19KITTREDGE/19KITTREDGE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/arts/19KITTREDGE/19KITTREDGE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/arts/19KITTREDGE/19KITTREDGE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/19/arts/19KITTREDGE/19KITTREDGE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/arts/19KITTREDGE/19KITTREDGE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Star Who Has No Time for Vanity', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Star Who Has No Time for Vanity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McDormand, Frances', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strout, Elizabeth', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Olive Kitteridge (TV Program)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-15T12:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Bruni', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bruni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a97a332-bd2e-5116-9278-45c8a87360b4', 'word_count': 1680, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a97a332-bd2e-5116-9278-45c8a87360b4'}, {'abstract': 'Former Senators Trent Lott and Tom Daschle return from retirement with ideas on how to make the Senate more productive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/politics/first-draft/2014/10/15/a-plan-to-save-the-senate-from-two-leaders-no-longer-in-it/', 'snippet': 'Former Senators Trent Lott and Tom Daschle return from retirement with ideas on how to make the Senate more productive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the Senate’s leaders during the impeachment trial of President Bill Clinton, Trent Lott, a Republican, and Tom Daschle, a Democrat, worked together to prevent the chamber from being torn apart in a tumultuous time. Now they are trying to save the Senate again, this time from the outside.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Plan to Save the Senate, From Two Leaders No Longer in It', 'kicker': 'First Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Filibusters and Debate Curbs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daschle, Tom', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lott, Trent', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-15T12:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy W. Peters', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Peters', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8072defe-cf84-5f07-8af6-78d2e45ba175', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8072defe-cf84-5f07-8af6-78d2e45ba175'}, {'abstract': 'Citigroup encourages its chairman in Mexico to retire after a second scandal. | AbbVie is reconsidering its deal for Shire. | Boards surrender to investor demands. | The Fed aims to keep its Doomsday Book a secret.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/10/15/morning-agenda-citigroups-sticky-situation/', 'snippet': 'Citigroup encourages its chairman in Mexico to retire after a second scandal. | AbbVie is reconsidering its deal for Shire. | Boards surrender to investor demands. | The Fed aims to keep its Doomsday Book a secret.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new scandal has erupted at Citigroup’s Mexican unit just months after a $400 million fraud involving a well-connected client, Michael Corkery and Jessica Silver-Greenberg write in DealBook. Now the sprawling global bank is wrestling with how to get its house in order in one of its oldest foreign operations. A crucial part of that decision rests on how to nudge aside the most powerful executive overseeing Mexico. What makes that decision particularly difficult is that the Mexican unit, Banamex, mints money. Still, the scandals have exposed the bank to risk at a time when Citigroup is trying to reduce its legal and regulatory burdens.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: Citigroup’s Sticky Situation', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-15T12:04:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7457ab75-b85d-5fb1-b549-3881fa59a437', 'word_count': 2358, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7457ab75-b85d-5fb1-b549-3881fa59a437'}, {'abstract': 'Investors continue to pour money into BlackRock’s index funds, and the firm’s 44 percent operating margin shows how lucrative the business of collecting and managing large pools of assets has become.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/10/15/blackrock-shows-strong-earnings-growth/', 'snippet': 'Investors continue to pour money into BlackRock’s index funds, and the firm’s 44 percent operating margin shows how lucrative the business of collecting and managing large pools of assets has become.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 9:02 a.m. | BlackRock, the world’s largest asset manager, delivered another strong quarter of earnings growth on Wednesday as investors continued to pour money into its index funds.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/15/business/dbpix-blackrock1/dbpix-blackrock1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/15/business/dbpix-blackrock1/dbpix-blackrock1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, '