 [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'RUSSO', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/7c11be6b-3302-53e9-9960-37f3fe5dfab3', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/7c11be6b-3302-53e9-9960-37f3fe5dfab3'}, {'abstract': 'James Risen argues that America’s open society has been a casualty of the war on terror.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/books/review/james-risens-pay-any-price.html', 'snippet': 'James Risen argues that America’s open society has been a casualty of the war on terror.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Pay Any Price: Greed, Power, and Endless War,” James Risen holds up a mirror to the United States in the 13 years since 9/11, and what it reveals is not a pretty sight. Risen, a Pulitzer Prize-\xadwinning reporter at The New York Times, documents the emergence of a “homeland \xadsecurity-industrial complex” more pervasive and more pernicious than the “military-industrial complex” Dwight Eisenhower warned against. With the power and passion of Zola’s “J’Accuse,” he chronicles the abandonment of America’s cherished open society in a never-satiated search for security from an ill-defined threat.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26richardson/26richardson-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 313, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26richardson/26richardson-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 313}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26richardson/26richardson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 534, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}