de518252c', 'word_count': 421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13df6036-3dd6-5c5f-95b2-9e2de518252c'}, {'abstract': 'For its third project, the artistic trio comprised of Cyril Duval, Avena Gallagher and Babak Radboy created “fake art” to market a very real mansion.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/15/frieze-shanzhai-biennial-100-hamilton-terrace/', 'snippet': 'For its third project, the artistic trio comprised of Cyril Duval, Avena Gallagher and Babak Radboy created “fake art” to market a very real mansion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Frieze week kicks off in London, so does the third Shanzhai Biennial — which does not occur every two years, but instead irregularly, whenever the artistic trio made up of Cyril Duval, Avena Gallagher and Babak Radboy comes together to focus their laser wits on the increasingly porous interstices of art, fashion and branding. Shanzhai Biennial is not an exhibition in any conventional sense of the word, nor is Shanzhai an actual Chinese locale. It’s a term that means “mountain stronghold,” slang for the breed of counterfeit products stockpiled in lofty, hard-to-access villages: name-brand knockoffs that are famous worldwide for their absurd, sometimes even poetic perversions of Western language.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/15/t-magazine/15shanzai-symonds/15shanzai-symonds-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/15/t-magazine/15shanzai-symonds/15shanzai-symonds-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/15/t-magazine/15shanzai-symonds/15shanzai-symonds-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 