dadcf2-e94b-52d3-ab82-1bdb9f0e975a', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dadadcf2-e94b-52d3-ab82-1bdb9f0e975a'}, {'abstract': 'The French writer Patrick Modiano, winner of the Nobel Prize in Literature, does not have many readers beyond France. When the literature laureate is largely unknown outside his native country, what does the accolade mean?', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/16/what-is-a-nobel-prize-really-worth/', 'snippet': 'The French writer Patrick Modiano, winner of the Nobel Prize in Literature, does not have many readers beyond France. When the literature laureate is largely unknown outside his native country, what does the accolade mean?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The French writer Patrick Modiano was awarded the Nobel Prize in\xa0Literature last week. His first novel, “La Place de L’Étoile,” was published in 1968 and has been followed by almost 30\xa0books, many of which have met critical and public acclaim in France. His work, however, is not well known abroad and largely unavailable in English. That a relatively unknown writer was selected has raised questions about the Swedish Academy’s regional allegiances and the purpose and status of the prize.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/15/opinion/nyto-faulkner/nyto-faulkner-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/15/opinion/nyto-faulkner/nyto-faulkner-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 594}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/15/opinion/nyto-faulkner/nyto-faulkner-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1013, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/15/opinion/