d_paragraph': 'Of Christine Kenneally’s father’s father — a man neither Kenneally nor her father ever knew, a man who did the deed requisite to reproduction and promptly vanished — she asks, “Did he leave anything more significant than the loud bang of a door shut down the generations?” Of course he did. He left his DNA and a granddaughter determined to draw from modern genetics and hard-won family history a coherent account of her roots. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-COVER/1019-bks-COVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-COVER/1019-bks-COVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 375}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-COVER/1019-bks-COVER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-COVER/1019-bks-COVER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1279, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-COVER/1019-bks-COVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-COVER/1019-bks-COVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-COVER/1019-bks-COVER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Christine Kenneally’s ‘Invisible History of the Human Race’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Story of Us', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kenneally, Christine', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dobbs, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Invisible History of the Human Race: How DNA and History Shape Our Identities and Our Futures (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-16T23:11:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Dobbs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dobbs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/24d003e6-1757-573f-be2c-6cc27b3f2502', 'word_count': 1224, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24d003e6-1757-573f-be2c-6cc27b3f2502'}, {'abstract': 'Nicholas Kristof and Sheryl WuDunn show how to make a difference in the lives of the disadvantaged.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/19/books/review/a-path-appears-by-nicholas-kristof-and-sheryl-wudunn.html', 'snippet': 'Nicholas Kristof and Sheryl WuDunn show how to make a difference in the lives of the disadvantaged.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the wrong hands, “A Path Appears” is a dangerous book: You wouldn’t want to leave it lying around where your teenager might glance at it. He might get diverted from that reassuring ambition to be a banker. Frankly, only scoundrels and saints can read this book safely: Everyone else will find it upsetting and uplifting in equal measure. I certainly did. If you want to carry on with your life just as it is, best give it a miss.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/04/books/review/19Collier/19Collier-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 564, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/04/books/review/19Collier/19Collier-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 564}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/04/books/review/19Collier/19Collier-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 962, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/04/books/review/19Collier/19Collier-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1624, 'width': 1728, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/04/books/review/19Collier/19Collier-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/04/books/review/19Collier/19Collier-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/04/books/review/19Collier/19Collier-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘A Path Appears,’ by Nicholas Kristof and Sheryl WuDunn', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Give Better', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kristof, Nicholas D', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wudunn, Sheryl', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-16T23:14:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Collier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/27c22e9a-5873-5714-8521-b294be9c1386', 'word_count': 1326, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27c22e9a-5873-5714-8521-b294be9c1386'}, {'abstract': '“Goya: Order and Disorder,” at the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, offers the broadest view of this artist’s career in America in more than two decades.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/17/arts/goya-order-and-disorder-at-museum-of-fine-arts-boston.html', 'snippet': '“Goya: Order and Disorder,” at the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, offers the broadest view of this artist’s career in America in more than two decades.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — The snapshots of savagery that the news media withholds — of mass murder, torture and rape in progress — Francisco Goya placed front and center in his art. In the print series known as “The Disasters of War,” he is a virtuoso of violence: shading it, savoring it, prodding it toward us with blunt intent. He wants to make us feel sick at being the species we are.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cosmic Grumbling, Awash With Acid ', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline':