ate': '2014-10-16T23:33:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jess Bidgood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jess', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bidgood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ade57ed-071a-520e-b9fe-b244ba3db1e0', 'word_count': 314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ade57ed-071a-520e-b9fe-b244ba3db1e0'}, {'abstract': 'Companies should disclose the information they sell about us.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/17/opinion/the-dark-market-for-personal-data.html', 'snippet': 'Companies should disclose the information they sell about us.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BALTIMORE — THE reputation business is exploding. Having eroded privacy for decades, shady, poorly regulated data miners, brokers and resellers have now taken creepy classification to a whole new level. They have created lists of victims of sexual assault, and lists of people with sexually transmitted diseases. Lists of people who have Alzheimer’s, dementia and AIDS. Lists of the impotent and the depressed.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/17/opinion/1017OPEDpotts/1017OPEDpotts-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 933, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/17/opinion/1017OPEDpotts/1017OPEDpotts-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 933}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/17/opinion/1017OPEDpotts/1017OPEDpotts-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 659, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': 