ame': 'organizations', 'value': 'Our Walmart', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-17T01:52:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi and Steven Greenhouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenhouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/06e2127a-bcb2-596a-a8eb-7ff8fdfabd74', 'word_count': 948, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06e2127a-bcb2-596a-a8eb-7ff8fdfabd74'}, {'abstract': 'A new study found that the caliber of the words spoken to young children was more valuable than the number of words heard in the development of a child’s language skills.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/17/us/quality-of-words-not-quantity-is-crucial-to-language-skills-study-finds.html', 'snippet': 'A new study found that the caliber of the words spoken to young children was more valuable than the number of words heard in the development of a child’s language skills.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has been nearly 20 years since a landmark education study found that by age 3, children from low-income families have heard 30 million fewer words than more affluent children, putting them at an educational disadvantage before they even began school. The findings led to increased calls for publicly funded prekindergarten programs and dozens of campaigns urging parents to get chatty with their children.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/17/us/LANGUAGE1/LANGUAGE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 