tells her husband that she’s spending her leisure time “breeding and feeding.” It’s an inside joke — Stephen, her husband, loves birding, and “breeding and feeding” is how he describes the avian lifestyle, “making them sound like sex-obsessed gluttons (that is, human beings).”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19ROMM/19ROMM-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 897, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19ROMM/19ROMM-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 897}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19ROMM/19ROMM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 685, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19ROMM/19ROMM-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1370, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19ROMM/19ROMM-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19ROMM/19ROMM-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19ROMM/19ROMM-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nell Zink’s ‘Wallcreeper’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Allure of the Wild', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zink, Nell', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-17T14:52:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Romm', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Romm', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/84c8210e-784a-5193-817e-cae5959f0c1f', 'word_count': 1157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84c8210e-784a-5193-817e-cae5959f0c1f'}, {'abstract': 'Each October brings a shelf full of Best American anthologies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/19/books/review/a-bevy-of-the-best.html', 'snippet': 'Each October brings a shelf full of Best American anthologies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his introduction to “The Best Short Stories of 1915,” Edward J. O’Brien asked: “Has the time not come at last to cease lamenting the pitiful gray shabbiness of American fiction?” The anthology was his answer. “It is my faith and hope that this annual volume of mine may do something toward disengaging the honest good from the meretricious mass of writing with which it is mingled.” The honest good that year included stories by Maxwell Struthers Burt, Newbold Noyes and Elsie Singmaster.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19openbook/19openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 258, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19openbook/19openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 258}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19openbook/19openbook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 441, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19openbook/19openbook-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 1859, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19openbook/19openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19openbook/19openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19openbook/19openbook-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Bevy of the Best', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Bevy of the Best', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-17T14:52:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7082a84b-771c-527a-9fa7-7e53db1bed62', 'word_count': 453, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7082a84b-771c-527a-9fa7-7e53db1bed62'}, {'abstract': 'From a distant farm to the African veldt, these picture books celebrate the limitless nature of creativity and imagination.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/19/books/review/marla-frazees-the-farmer-and-the-clown-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'From a distant farm to the African veldt, these picture books celebrate the limitless nature of creativity and imagination.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The lines and verses are only the outward garments of the poem. . . . The real poem is the soul within them.” So says L. M. Montgomery’s beloved Anne of Green Gables, insisting that she can call a picture a poem. If that is true, then certainly the wordless picture book is vis\xadual poetry. An artist of a wordless picture book must be meticulous, carefully using the tools of color and composition to elicit an emotional experience just as a poet uses words.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19BKSLIN1/19BKSLIN1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 436, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19BKSLIN1/19BKSLIN1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 436}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19BKSLIN1/19BKSLIN1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 743, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19BKSLIN1/19BKSLIN1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1487, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19BKSLIN1/1019-bks-LIN-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75