ub_date': '2014-10-17T14:53:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Asimov', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Asimov', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/787f1adb-b6aa-5ad6-b440-aea409b102d3', 'word_count': 1093, 'uri': 'nyt://article/787f1adb-b6aa-5ad6-b440-aea409b102d3'}, {'abstract': 'Two novels featuring adoption and foster care show how bonds are formed despite less than ideal circumstances.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/19/books/review/kinda-like-brothers-and-half-a-world-away.html', 'snippet': 'Two novels featuring adoption and foster care show how bonds are formed despite less than ideal circumstances.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was a family living near my junior high school that fostered a gang of seven children; they fought viciously among their sibling pack, but pity the outsider who glared at a single one of them. I thought of them while reading “Kinda Like Brothers” and “Half a World Away.” Both show how fragile yet electric the cord that joins families through foster care and adoption can be.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Kinda Like Brothers’ and ‘Half a World Away’', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fierce Attachments', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Booth, Coe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kadohata, Cynthia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-17T14:53:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rita Williams-Garcia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rita', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams-Garcia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1bafa87-8637-5855-a1a4-c8a00d3276f5', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1bafa87-8637-5855-a1a4-c8a00d3276f5'}, {'abstract': 'The fourth annual TEDx conference devoted to Broadway and theater will be held on Feb. 23 and focus on the question, “What is the best Broadway can be?”, producers said.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/17/ted-conference-to-ponder-broadways-best/', 'snippet': 'The fourth annual TEDx conference devoted to Broadway and theater will be held on Feb. 23 and focus on the question, “What is the best Broadway can be?”, producers said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The fourth annual TEDx conference devoted to Broadway and theater will be held on Feb. 23 and focus on the question, “What is the best Broadway can be?,” organizers of the daylong event said on Friday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'TED Conference to Ponder Broadway’s Best', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'TED Conference', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-17T14:53:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f1db408-33d8-5e74-af76-95d4326770dc', 'word_count': 120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f1db408-33d8-5e74-af76-95d4326770dc'}, {'abstract': 'Characters in two dark urban fairy tales for middle-grade readers uncover events hidden in the past.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/19/books/review/gabriel-finley-and-the-ravens-riddle-and-heap-house.html', 'snippet': 'Characters in two dark urban fairy tales for middle-grade readers uncover events hidden in the past.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I am a  dark urban fairy tale with strangely behaving birds and even more strangely behaving furniture. I am also the middle-grade debut for an author of two previous books for adults. What is my title?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Gabriel Finley and the Raven’s Riddle’ and ‘Heap House’', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'They’ve Got Secrets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carey, Edward', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hagen, George', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-17T14:54:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pseudonymous Bosch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pseudonymous', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dcb51954-a4ff-51fe-9b8e-4ea753faa03d', 'word_count': 745, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dcb51954-a4ff-51fe-9b8e-4ea753faa03d'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of illustrated tales with a sinister bent, and an anthology of stories featuring otherworldly creatures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/19/books/review/through-the-woods-and-monstrous-affections.html', 'snippet': 'A collection of illustrated tales with a sinister bent, and an anthology of stories featuring otherworldly creatures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You don’t scare your children enough.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Through the Woods’ and ‘Monstrous Affections’', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Touch of Evil', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grant, Gavin J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Link, Kelly', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carroll, Emily (1983- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-17T14:54:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Gidwitz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gidwitz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/60386ac8-7a95-5a3f-84c1-26235a62d675', 'word_count': 649, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60386ac8-7a95-5a3f-84c1-26235a62d675'}, {'abstract': 'Marilynne Robinson, whose novel “Lila” is No. 4 on the hardcover fiction list, has found success agreeable. “A Pulitzer Prize is very reassuring, and that’s a fact.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Marilynne Robinson, whose novel “Lila” is No. 4 on the hardcover fiction list, has found success agreeable. “A Pulitzer Prize is very reassuring, and that’s a fact.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gotta Have Faith: Marilynne Robinson’s new novel, “Lila,” enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 4. It’s her fourth book to become a best seller, which is both heartening and a little weird — heartening because Robinson’s sentences are so polished and faceted and clear they could bend light; weird because most of her books engage deeply with the thornier aspects of Calvinist theology. (“She really is not like any other writer,” Cathleen Schine wrote, approvingly, in The New York Review of Books recently. “She really isn’t.”) Robinson first made the list in 2005 with her second novel, “Gilead,” which went on to win the Pulitzer and spent three months on the hardcover and paperback lists. It also laid the groundwork for both of her subsequent novels, which revisit the same characters and small-town setting. Robinson was as surprised as anyone when “Gilead” found a wide popular audience. “If you were to say to someone that you were to write a book about a minister dying in Iowa in 1956 and that he would spend a certain time on theological reflections, they would not think this was a book that would necessarily . . . sell,” she conceded to The St. Louis Post-Dispatch in 2005. Still, she found the success gratifying. “A Pulitzer Prize is very reassuring,” she told The San Francisco Chronicle in 2008, “and that’s a fact.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19list-robinson/19list-robinson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19list-robinson/19list-robinson-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19list-robinson/19list-robinson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19list-robinson/19list-robinson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19list-robinson/19list-robinson-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-17T14:55:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/130a8dcd-5a6b-50b8-9f66-733e5fe6e5e8', 'word_count': 505, 'uri': 'nyt://article/130a8dcd-5a6b-50b8-9f66-733e5fe6e5e8'}, {'abstract': 'Simon Rich’s humorous stories take on the millennial generation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/19/books/review/simon-rich-spoiled-brats-review.html', 'snippet': 'Simon Rich’s humorous stories take on the millennial generation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mel Brooks is an interesting case study for comedy writers. His filmography illustrates a clear rule, as far as writing comedy — and especially satire — goes: You can’t really make fun of something unless you truly, deeply, madly love it.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-OSWALT-1/1019-bks-OSWALT-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 644, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-OSWALT-1/1019-bks-OSWALT-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 644}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-OSWALT-1/1019-bks-OSWALT-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 708, 'width': 660, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-OSWALT-1/1019-bks-OSWALT-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 708, 'width': 660, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-OSWALT-1/1019-bks-OSWALT-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-OSWALT-1/1019-bks-OSWALT-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-OSWALT-1/1019-bks-OSWALT-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Simon Rich’s ‘Spoiled Brats’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In a Post-Post-Post-Ironic World', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rich, Simon (1984- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-17T14:55:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patton Oswalt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patton', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Oswalt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/77689c48-3290-5273-84cf-a17cf652df08', 'word_count': 975, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77689c48-3290-5273-84cf-a17cf652df08'}, {'abstract': 'A lawyer personalizes the struggle against injustice with the story of a man wrongfully convicted of murder.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/19/books/review/just-mercy-by-bryan-stevenson.html', 'snippet': 'A lawyer personalizes the struggle against injustice with the story of a man wrongfully convicted of murder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Unfairness in the Justice system is a major theme of our age. DNA analysis exposes false convictions, it seems, on a weekly basis. The predominance of racial minorities in jails and prisons suggests systemic bias. Sentencing guidelines born of the war on drugs look increasingly draconian. Studies cast doubt on the accuracy of eyewitness testimony. Even the states that still kill people appear to have forgotten how; lately executions have been botched to horrific effect.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19ConoverSUB/19ConoverSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 443, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19ConoverSUB/19ConoverSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 443}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19ConoverSUB/19ConoverSUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 756, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19ConoverSUB/19ConoverSUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1512, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19ConoverSUB/19ConoverSUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19ConoverSUB/19ConoverSUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19ConoverSUB/19ConoverSUB-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Just Mercy,’ by Bryan Stevenson', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Dispossessed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Conover, Ted', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stevenson, Bryan A (1959- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-17T14:55:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ted Conover', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ted', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Conover', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9744bcf1-04a7-5b3a-85a7-0dfad4bd1995', 'word_count': 1367, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9744bcf1-04a7-5b3a-85a7-0dfad4bd1995'}, {'abstract': 'Men follow their compulsions, and sometimes receive their comeuppance, in Paul Theroux’s stories.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/19/books/review/paul-therouxs-mr-bones.html', 'snippet': 'Men follow their compulsions, and sometimes receive their comeuppance, in Paul Theroux’s stories.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Near the conclusion of John Cheever’s masterpiece “Goodbye, My Brother,” the narrator, speaking of his gloomy younger sibling, asks: “Oh, what can you do with a man like that? What can you do? How can you dissuade his eye in a crowd from seeking out the cheek with acne, the infirm hand; how can you teach him to respond to the inestimable greatness of the race, the harsh surface beauty of life; how can you put his finger for him on the obdurate truths before which fear and horror are powerless?” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-PROSE/1019-bks-PROSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 572, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-PROSE/1019-bks-PROSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 572}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-PROSE/1019-bks-PROSE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 976, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-PROSE/1019-bks-PROSE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1952, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-PROSE/1019-bks-PROSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-PROSE/1019-bks-PROSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/1019-bks-PROSE/1019-bks-PROSE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Paul Theroux’s ‘Mr. Bones’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dissecting Civility', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Prose, Francine', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Theroux, Paul', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-17T14:56:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Francine Prose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Francine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Prose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/75479053-68a7-5ee1-9f2f-b6caf150d8ba', 'word_count': 1212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75479053-68a7-5ee1-9f2f-b6caf150d8ba'}, {'abstract': 'Two Soviet Jews — an Israeli politician and a disgraced K.G.B. informer — are reunited decades after a devastating betrayal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/19/books/review/the-betrayers-by-david-bezmozgis.html', 'snippet': 'Two Soviet Jews — an Israeli politician and a disgraced K.G.B. informer — are reunited decades after a devastating betrayal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When was the last time you tore through a work of literary fiction at the rate of a Tom Clancy thriller? Personally, when I discover I’m holding a smart novel that’s also a page turner, I get punchy. Not only have I spotted a unicorn; we are gamboling down the dale hand in hand. A novel of ideas and an engrossing story? It’s the umami experience: salty and sweet, yin and yang, the rocket scientist who is also a looker. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19FISHMAN/19Fishman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 595, 'width': 549, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19FISHMAN/19Fishman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 595, 'width': 549, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19FISHMAN/19FISHMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19FISHMAN/19FISHMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19FISHMAN/19FISHMAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Betrayers,’ by David Bezmozgis', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Yalta at This Time of Year', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fishman, Boris', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bezmozgis, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-17T14:56:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Boris Fishman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Boris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fishman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1eb278a1-bcb4-5253-934c-9ba66eb82e10', 'word_count': 1240, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1eb278a1-bcb4-5253-934c-9ba66eb82e10'}, {'abstract': 'In his writing guide, the Harvard polymath Steven Pinker favors looser, more easygoing grammatical usage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/19/books/review/steven-pinker-the-sense-of-style-review.html', 'snippet': 'In his writing guide, the Harvard polymath Steven Pinker favors looser, more easygoing grammatical usage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steven Pinker, the Harvard linguist and psychologist, is one of that new breed of top-flight scientists and teachers, like the physicist Brian Greene and the evolutionary biologist Richard Dawkins, who also write uncommonly well. To those of us who try to write for a living and couldn’t pass a science course, let alone teach one, such people are a little annoying. And now, not content with just poaching, Pin\xadker has set himself up as a gamekeeper of sorts; he’s bringing out a manual, telling the rest of us how writing ought to be done. The title, “The Sense of Style: The Thinking Person’s Guide to Writing in the 21st Century,” suggests it’s even meant to supplant that classic text “The Elements of Style,” by Will Strunk and E. B. White.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19BKSMCGRATH/1019-bks-McGrath-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 613, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19BKSMCGRATH/1019-bks-McGrath-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 613}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19BKSMCGRATH/1019-bks-McGrath-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1003, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19BKSMCGRATH/1019-bks-McGrath-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1439, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19BKSMCGRATH/1019-bks-McGrath-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19BKSMCGRATH/1019-bks-McGrath-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19BKSMCGRATH/1019-bks-McGrath-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Steven Pinker’s ‘The Sense of Style’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Omit Needless Rules', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McGrath, Charles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pinker, Steven', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-17T14:57:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles McGrath', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGrath', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/70af2b7b-6b0d-5217-8deb-6ce5c254e0b3', 'word_count': 1199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70af2b7b-6b0d-5217-8deb-6ce5c254e0b3'}, {'abstract': 'Jonathan Darman’s history of the 1960s weaves together accounts of the activities of Lyndon Johnson and Ronald Reagan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/19/books/review/landslide-lbj-and-ronald-reagan-at-the-dawn-of-a-new-america-by-jonathan-darman.html', 'snippet': 'Jonathan Darman’s history of the 1960s weaves together accounts of the activities of Lyndon Johnson and Ronald Reagan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lyndon Johnson has come to look far more formidable today than he did in 1968. Current commentators yearn for a time when a master partisan politician worked the levers of power in Washington and won numerous victories, including the Voting Rights Act and the creation of Medicare. Amid disappointment over a promised transformational postpartisan leadership under Barack Obama, the canny Johnson looks like a model of White House command. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19wilentz-sub/19wilentz-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 372, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19wilentz-sub/19wilentz-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 372}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19wilentz-sub/19wilentz-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 969, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19wilentz-sub/19wilentz-sub-superJumbo.jpg'