ome-in-the-west-village.html', 'snippet': 'Mary Carillo’s work as a sports analyst and reporter has taken her around the world, but New York still beckons her.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In her long and distinguished career as a sports analyst and reporter, Mary Carillo has covered skiing, figure skating, basketball, gymnastics, badminton, skeleton, curling, bobsledding, luge and doubles luge, an event she memorably described as “a bar bet gone bad,” and, most particularly, tennis. A former pro, Ms. Carillo, 57, and her childhood friend and neighbor John McEnroe were the mixed doubles champions at the French Open in 1977.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'So, Bowling, Anyone? ', 'kicker': 'What I Love', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'So, Bowling, Anyone?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carillo, Mary', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'West Village (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-17T18:05:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanne Kaufman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaufman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36309b3d-cf6a-5ad0-9373-a1e0d383fb4c', 'word_count': 1228, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36309b3d-cf6a-5ad0-9373-a1e0d383fb4c'}, {'abstract': 'Christine Kenneally discusses “The Invisible History of the Human Race,” and Zephyr Teachout talks about “Corruption in America.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/17/book-review-podcast-the-story-of-us/', 'snippet': 'Christine Kenneally discusses “The Invisible History of the Human Race,” and Zephyr Teachout talks about “Corruption in America.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In The New York Times Book Review, David Dobbs reviews Christine Kenneally’s “The Invisible History of the Human Race: How DNA and History Shape Our Identities and Our Futures.” Mr. Dobbs writes:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19cover-shadow/19cover-shadow-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 214, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19cover-shadow/19cover-shadow-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 214, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19cover-shadow/19cover-shadow-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19cover-shadow/19cover-shadow-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/books/review/19cover-shadow/19cover-shadow-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Book Review Podcast: The Story of Us', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Alter, Alexandra', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cowles, Gregory', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kenneally, Christine', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Pamela', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Teachout, Zephyr', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-17T18:07:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1e6ea30-f03f-5bf4-a043-381977780367', 'word_count': 150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1e6ea30-f03f-5bf4-a043-381977780367'}, {'abstract': 'Like Russ Hodges in 1951, these announcers got to call a series-ending Giants home run. How did they do?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/18/sports/ishikawa-bobby-thompson-linked-by-announcers-the-giants-win-the-pennant.html', 'snippet': 'Like Russ Hodges in 1951, these announcers got to call a series-ending Giants home run. How did they do?', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Bobby Thomson unloaded a game-ending home run to win the National League pennant for the New York Giants on Oct. 3, 1951, Russ Hodges’s radio call on WMCA 570 was memorable for its volume and touch of hysteria. The Giants had triumphed over the rival Brooklyn Dodgers in a three-game playoff and were headed to the World Series, which they lost to the Yankees.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/18/sports/Y-HODGES/Y-HODGES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 379, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/18/sports/Y-HODGES/Y-HODGES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 379}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'image