: '2014-10-17T21:18:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Doreen Carvajal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Doreen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carvajal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e08816a-dd84-5194-8357-e893c4386780', 'word_count': 613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e08816a-dd84-5194-8357-e893c4386780'}, {'abstract': 'Esa-Pekka Salonen led the New York Philharmonic through Beethoven and Stravinsky on Thursday, while the pianist Jeremy Denk made his Philharmonic debut.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/18/arts/music/esa-pekka-salonen-leads-the-new-york-philharmonic.html', 'snippet': 'Esa-Pekka Salonen led the New York Philharmonic through Beethoven and Stravinsky on Thursday, while the pianist Jeremy Denk made his Philharmonic debut.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Finnish conductor Esa-Pekka Salonen, at 56, is no longer as young as he looks, but he and the pianist Jeremy Denk, 44, gave an impression of youthful vigor with the New York Philharmonic at Avery Fisher Hall on Thursday evening. Mr. Denk, in his Philharmonic debut, performed Beethoven’s Piano Concerto No. 1, and Mr. Salonen added Beethoven’s “King Stephen” Overture and Stravinsky’s “The Firebird.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Night of Excitements and Extremes', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Night of Excitements and Extremes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Philharmonic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Salonen, Esa-Pekka', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Avery Fisher Hall', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-17T21:23:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By James R. Oestreich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Oestreich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9151617-b3ea-5e3d-a8f6-06cfd2ea28ab', 'word_count': 424, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9151617-b3ea-5e3d-a8f6-06cfd2ea28ab'}, {'abstract': 'This week, Joe discusses the potential for a slowdown in global growth.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/17/joe-on-wnycs-money-talking-18/', 'snippet': 'This week, Joe discusses the potential for a slowdown in global growth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is it the bad news out of Europe or those disappointing retail numbers? Maybe it’s just Ebola. Whatever the catalyst, global financial markets were on a roller coaster this week with a deep plunge by the S&P 500 followed by a sharp U-turn thanks to Morgan Stanley’s better-than-expected quarterly earnings. Things look calmer heading into the weekend, but some analysts are questioning whether the new volatility signals a slowdown in global growth. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Joe on WNYC’s Money Talking', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'S & P Dow Jones Indices', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-17T21:26:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lydia Dallett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lydia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dallett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8617ff98-c655-5bfd-b6b4-dcbacefe5137', 'word_count': 115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8617ff98-c655-5bfd-b6b4-dcbacefe5137'}, {'abstract': 'Vladimir Jurowski led the London Philharmonic in works by Shostakovich and others at Carnegie Hall on Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/18/arts/jurowski-leads-london-philharmonic-at-carnegie-hall.html', 'snippet': 'Vladimir Jurowski led the London Philharmonic in works by Shostakovich and others at Carnegie Hall on Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York audiences have had ample opportunities in recent years to get to know the meticulous, brilliant Russian conductor Vladimir Jurowski. Last fall, while conducting Strauss’s “Die Frau Ohne Schatten” rapturously during a run at the Metropolitan Opera, he made time to lead the Juilliard Orchestra in an exciting program, and then returned to the city in the spring for an appearance with the New York Philharmonic.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/18/arts/philharmonic/philharmonic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/18/arts/philharmonic/philharmonic-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/18/arts/philharmonic/philharmonic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/18/arts/philharmonic/philharmonic-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/18/arts/philharmonic/philharmonic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/18/arts/philharmonic/philharmonic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/18/arts/philharmonic/philharmonic-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Russian Guest Returns With Enigmatic Works', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Russian Guest Returns With Enigmatic Works', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tommasini, Anthony', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'London Philharmonic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jurowski, Vladimir', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carnegie Hall', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-17T21:26:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0c22d70-3ba7-5c3a-b716-3ef4bbc4a286', 'word_count': 515, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0c22d70-3ba7-5c3a-b716-3ef4bbc4a286'}, {'abstract': 'Sierra Boggess brings a little Broadway and a lot of other selections to her cabaret act at 54 Below.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/18/arts/music/sierra-boggess-performs-at-54-below.html', 'snippet': 'Sierra Boggess brings a little Broadway and a lot of other selections to her cabaret act at 54 Below.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Broadway singer Sierra Boggess occupies a privileged niche in that magic circle where Disney songs, Andrew Lloyd Webber ballads and stentorian proclamations by Alain Boublil and Claude-Michael Schönberg (“Les Misérables”) melt into a sticky mass of high-flown sentimentality. The theater world is teeming with well-trained singers who have the polished semi-operatic technique to put over such songs. But it’s not always easy to distinguish one from another in the parade of cookie-cutter voices entrusted with such material. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Serenading Broadway, but Never Too Seriously', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Serenading Broadway, but Never Too Seriously', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boggess, Sierra', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': '54 Below (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-17T21:28:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3228639e-1d8f-5b49-ad08-a219d091eb98', 'word_count': 326, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3228639e-1d8f-5b49-ad08-a219d091eb98'}, {'abstract': 'How can it be that minority students seem unhappier when they have a larger presence within institutions that once excluded them?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/19/opinion/sunday/a-paradox-of-integration.html', 'snippet': 'How can it be that minority students seem unhappier when they have a larger presence within institutions that once excluded them?', 'lead_paragraph': 'FROM 2010 to 2013, I spent time on a historically white South African college campus to write its post-apartheid history. As I worked, I discovered what appeared to me to be a peculiar paradox: As black students’ access to the school had grown, so had their dissatisfaction with it.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/sunday-review/19FAIRBANKS/19FAIRBANKS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 785, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/19/sunday-review/19FAIRBANKS/19FAIRBANKS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 785}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/sunday-review/19FAIRBANKS/19FAIRBANKS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 783, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/sunday-review/19FAIRBANKS/19FAIRBANKS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1660, 'width': 1268, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/sunday-review/19FAIRBANKS/19FAIRBANKS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard',