d72-561a-b95c-91a16cf5516c'}, {'abstract': 'The new play “Billy & Ray” is the latest stage work to focus on filmmaking.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/19/theater/billy-ray-and-other-plays-laugh-at-hollywood.html', 'snippet': 'The new play “Billy & Ray” is the latest stage work to focus on filmmaking.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York’s love-hate relationship with Hollywood typically results in plays about fictitious (and often terrible-sounding) films, from “Once in a Lifetime” to “Speed-the-Plow” to “The Money Shot.” But a durable theatrical subgenre concerns itself with movies that were actually made, and these plays tend to focus on those that people remember fondly.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/arts/19MOVIEPLAY2/19MOVIEPLAY2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 388, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/19/arts/19MOVIEPLAY2/19MOVIEPLAY2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 388}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/arts/19MOVIEPLAY2/19MOVIEPLAY2-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 663, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/arts/19MOVIEPLAY2/19MOVIEPLAY2-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1325, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/arts/19MOVIEPLAY2/19MOVIEPLAY2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/19/arts/19MOVIEPLAY2/19MOVIEPLAY2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/arts/19MOVIEPLAY2/19MOVIEPLAY2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Skewers of Stage and Screen ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Skewers of Stage and Screen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grode, Eric', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bencivenga, Mike', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kartheiser, Vincent', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marshall, Garry', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McDonagh, Martin', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Guare, John', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Billy & Ray (Play)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-18T14:34:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Grode', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grode', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f608f190-c4eb-596b-9402-ce052f568f8d', 'word_count': 709, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f608f190-c4eb-596b-9402-ce052f568f8d'}, {'abstract': 'The sculptures of Judith Scott, cocoonlike bundles of yarn, fabric and found objects, look perfectly on-trend.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/19/arts/design/wrapped-in-complexity.html', 'snippet': 'The sculptures of Judith Scott, cocoonlike bundles of yarn, fabric and found objects, look perfectly on-trend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The sculptures of Judith Scott, cocoonlike bundles of yarn, fabric and found objects, look perfectly on-trend in their devotion to fiber art and assemblage. But Ms. Scott (1943-2005) was not trying to insert herself into any particular contemporary-art conversation. She was born with Down syndrome, was largely deaf and mute, and spent 35 years in an Ohio institution. In 1987, her twin sister, Joyce, introduced her to the Creative Growth program for artists with developmental disabilities in Oakland, Calif., where she spent the rest of her life making art. “Judith Scott: Bound and Unbound,” opening Friday at the Elizabeth A. Sackler Center for Feminist Art at the Brooklyn Museum, celebrates her intriguing wrapped sculptures and the disability-rights movement that brought them to prominence. It asks some tough questions about the art world’s handling of artists with disabilities, challenging us to unravel Ms. Scott’s tightly wound creations without looking for a biographical thread. (Through March 29, 200 Eastern Parkway, at Prospect Park; 718-638-5000, brooklynmuseum.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/arts/19GUIDE4/19GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/19/arts/19GUIDE4/19GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 594}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/arts/19GUIDE4/19GUIDE4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1014, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/19/arts/19GUIDE4/19GUIDE4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1871, 'width': 1890, 'subTy