], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/404c4d8c-9b39-5688-952c-90eb487013b3', 'word_count': 939, 'uri': 'nyt://article/404c4d8c-9b39-5688-952c-90eb487013b3'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: Tourism in Russia was not disrupted by leadership changes in 1964.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/19/1964-intourist-carries-on-with-business-as-usual/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: Tourism in Russia was not disrupted by leadership changes in 1964.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — While Moscow remained inaccessible to the foreign press when former Premier Nikita S. Khrushchev took his tumble, one government official was fielding questions from a group of 36 American travel agents and this reporter. The conference, with Intourist, the official government tourist agency, had been scheduled before the big shake-up, and was convened on the appointed hour in a ‘‘business-as-usual’’ fashion. When asked if the change in leadership of government would affect the Soviet attitude toward tourism, Georgiy I. Zubkov, vice-president of in tourist, gave this terse reply: ‘‘We will not make any changes in our business relations. It will not affect in any way our travel business.’’ ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1964: Intourist Carries on With Business as Usual', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cold War Era', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel Agencies and Agents', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'USSR (Former Soviet Union)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-19T11:44:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'se