': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15372540-2444-5b20-a561-8aa868622425', 'word_count': 1058, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15372540-2444-5b20-a561-8aa868622425'}, {'abstract': 'Best known for his operas and symphonies, Mr. Glass’s Piano Études are the focus of a new recording and concerts at BAM.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/20/philip-glass-on-a-smaller-scale/', 'snippet': 'Best known for his operas and symphonies, Mr. Glass’s Piano Études are the focus of a new recording and concerts at BAM.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Philip Glass’s big-ticket works, for the last 35 years, have been operas and symphonies, with works for the smaller, electronic keyboard-based chamber ensembles with which he made his name inevitably taking a back seat. But for many of Mr. Glass’s most ardent fans, some of the composer’s best, most harmonically inventive music is in his Piano Études. That collection of 20 works is about to take the spotlight, thanks to both a new recording by the pianist Maki Namekawa, and concerts at the Brooklyn Academy of Music on Dec. 5 and 6, at which Mr. Glass, Ms. Namekawa and eight other pianists (among them, the composers Nico Muhly, Tania León and Timo Andres) will perform the full set.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/21/arts/20artsbeat-glass/20artsbeat-glass-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 403, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/21/arts/20artsbeat-glass/20artsbeat-glass-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 403}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/21/arts/20artsbeat-glass/20artsbeat-glass-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 688, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url