_count': 172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60e717d6-9ba2-5f19-9ad5-03592fe0c6d4'}, {'abstract': 'The South African track star, who was found guilty of culpable homicide in the killing of his girlfriend, was also given a suspended three-year term on separate firearms charges.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/22/world/africa/oscar-pistorius-sentenced.html', 'snippet': 'The South African track star, who was found guilty of culpable homicide in the killing of his girlfriend, was also given a suspended three-year term on separate firearms charges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Seeking to strike a balance between mercy and retribution, a judge sentenced Oscar Pistorius, the South African track star, to five years in prison on Tuesday for killing his girlfriend, Reeva Steenkamp.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Oscar Pistorius’s 5-Year Sentence May Mean Move to House Arrest', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For Pistorius, Five-Year Term May Mean Move to House Arrest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pistorius, Oscar', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Steenkamp, Reeva', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Masipa, Thokozile Matilda', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-21T08:37:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Cowell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c8b25d6-eb9b-515e-81fc-9a44af423059', 'word_count': 1022, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c8b25d6-eb9b-515e-81fc-9a44af423059'}, {'abstract': 'Surge-pricing comes to the restaurant industry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/magazine/can-you-uber-a-burger.html', 'snippet': 'Surge-pricing comes to the restaurant industry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the spring of 2009, when New York was consumed by a frenzy for extravagant hamburgers, one of the most coveted was the $26 Black Label burger, perhaps smeared with a dab of bone marrow, at the recently revamped Minetta\xad Tavern in Greenwich Village. For most civilians, their best hope of devouring one of these funky, dry-aged meat pucks was to arrive promptly at 5:30 p.m. and wait — perhaps for hours, as I once did — until a table opened. For the select few, however, there was a shortcut. Keith McNally, the restaurant’s proprietor, has long maintained a private reservation line for esteemed regulars and V.I.P.s, who were ranked in their worthiness from A to AAA. For them, a $26 burger — and a table for two at 7:30 p.m. at which to engorge it — was only a phone call away.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/magazine/26economy/26economy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 455, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/26/magazine/26economy/26economy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 455}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/magazine/26economy/26economy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 776, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/magazine/26economy/26economy-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1410, 'width': 1860, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/magazine/26economy/26economy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/26/magazine/26economy/26economy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/magazine/26economy/26economy-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Can You Uber a Burger?', 'kicker': "It's the Economy", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Table for 2 at 7:30? $25, Please . . .', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buchanan, Matt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reservations (Airlines, Hotels, Etc.)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-21T08:58:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Buchanan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buchanan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3cc8d582-01d8-5fa9-84be-f9a091ed01d9', 'word_count': 1247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3cc8d582-01d8-5fa9-84be-f9a091ed01d9'}, {'abstract': '“Yes means yes” means checking in with your partner during sex, but many young men say they don’t know how to have that conversation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/magazine/hooking-up-at-an-affirmative-consent-campus-its-complicated.html', 'snippet': '“Yes means yes” means checking in with your partner during sex, but many young men say they don’t know how to have that conversation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One afternoon during Labor Day weekend, a group of 15 or so Yale freshmen met in a classroom where history and French classes would soon be held. As they snacked on pretzels and Skittles, a few volunteered to act out a series of scenarios in which one student asks another out for frozen yogurt. In the first bit of role playing, one student was told to make it clear, in an easygoing way, that he or she wants to go out. The recipient of the invitation was told that he or she also wants to go but has a paper due. “How can you show enthusiasm while still turning down the invitation?” a prompt on a card asked. The answer generally wasn’t hard to convey or, for the freshmen watching, to interpret. Most students found that they knew how to demur while keeping the door open for next time.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/magazine/26lede1/26lede1-articleLarge-v6.jpg', 'height': 502, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/26/magazine/26lede1/26lede1-articleLarge-v6.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 502}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/magazine/26lede1/26lede1-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 565, 'width': 675, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/magazine/26lede1/26lede1-superJumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 565, 'width': 675, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/magazine/26lede1/26lede1-thumbStandard-v6.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/26/magazine/26lede1/26lede1-thumbStandard-v6.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/magazine/26lede1/26lede1-thumbLarge-v6.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hooking Up at an Affirmative-Consent Campus? It’s Complicated', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Meaning of Yes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bazelon, Emily', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yale University', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-21T08:58:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Bazelon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bazelon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce9c1fd8-1888-5b04-af44-bb2dbeafbf5d', 'word_count': 1360, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce9c1fd8-1888-5b04-af44-bb2dbeafbf5d'}, {'abstract': 'In “Hidden Scars,” Stanley Greene returns to Chechnya to document the aftermath of the 1993 conflict.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/21/revisiting-unhealed-wounds-in-chechnya/', 'snippet': 'In “Hidden Scars,” Stanley Greene returns to Chechnya to document the aftermath of the 1993 conflict.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stanley Greene was a battle-tested conflict photographer when he arrived to cover the first Chechen war in 1993. He had recently covered the violence in nearby Georgia and had almost lost his life amid the shelling of Russia’s Parliament during an attempted coup. But nothing he had seen prepared him for the brute force unleashed by the Russian government in the breakaway republic of Chechnya and the catastrophic loss of life he witnessed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Revisiting Unhealed Wounds in Chechnya', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greene, Stanley', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kadyrov, Ramzan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Putin, Vladimir V', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Terry, Sara', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Caucasus (Russia)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chechnya (Russia)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia (Georgian Republic)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kosovo', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lebanon', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-21T09:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Estrin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Estrin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae08219e-af01-524f-a1b4-3cd15a329406', 'word_count': 706, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae08219e-af01-524f-a1b4-3cd15a329406'}, {'abstract': 'Do you think ordinary people will ever make interplanetary space trips, the way people take intercontinental plane rides today?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/21/will-humans-live-on-mars-someday/', 'snippet': 'Do you think ordinary people will ever make interplanetary space trips, the way people take intercontinental plane rides today?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Humans have long felt a connection with the Red Planet. The Romans named the planet after their god of war. More recently, popular culture imagined strange Martians building canals — and even invading Earth. Lately, Mars is looking like humanity’s new frontier.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/21/science/21MARS-LN/21MARS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/21/science/21MARS-LN/21MARS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/21/science/21MARS-LN/21MARS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/21/science/21MARS-LN/21MARS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1356, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/21/science/21MARS-LN/21MARS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/21/science/21MARS-LN/21MARS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/21/science/21MARS-LN/21MARS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Will Humans Live on Mars Someday?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-21T09:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7bda57e-d2b1-539f-aaef-c858dc875afe', 'word_count': 340, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7bda57e-d2b1-539f-aaef-c858dc875afe'}, {'abstract': 'The barricaded areas in the financial district have served as a backdrop for wedding and graduation photos, as young Hong Kongers intertwine their personal lives with the historical significance of the protests.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/21/love-blooms-amid-the-umbrellas-in-hong-kong/', 'snippet': 'The barricaded areas in the financial district have served as a backdrop for wedding and graduation photos, as young Hong Kongers intertwine their personal lives with the historical significance of the protests.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last Wednesday night, platoons of students, some wearing colorful handkerchiefs across their faces like bank robbers in a spaghetti western, others wearing surgical masks, fanned out across the heart of the Central financial district in Hong Kong. In small packs they approached the Stock Exchange and the skyscrapers housing some of the world’s biggest banks, hoping to set up new street barricades.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': '