 'height': 238, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/17/upshot/18tvbuychart-1413506157891/18tvbuychart-1413506157891-superJumbo.png', 'height': 390, 'width': 1679, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/17/upshot/18tvbuychart-1413506157891/18tvbuychart-1413506157891-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/17/upshot/18tvbuychart-1413506157891/18tvbuychart-1413506157891-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/17/upshot/18tvbuychart-1413506157891/18tvbuychart-1413506157891-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Political TV Ads Can Be Wasteful. But That’s Changing.', 'kicker': 'Campaign Advertising', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Midterm Elections (2014)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kantar Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-21T15:48:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Derek Willis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Derek', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Willis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee5eec41-fb0e-5e96-ba73-dece117b17f5', 'word_count': 1102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee5eec41-fb0e-5e96-ba73-dece117b17f5'}, {'abstract': 'Two politicians, two paths for Republicans in the age of Obamacare.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/21/gillespie-today-or-kasich-tomorrow/', 'snippet': 'Two politicians, two paths for Republicans in the age of Obamacare.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Yesterday, Ohio governor John Kasich briefly made news when the Associated Press quoted him saying that repeal of Obamacare was simply “not gonna happen,”\xa0and adding\xa0that\xa0“the opposition to it was really either political or ideological,” and in either case\xa0insufficiently attuned to the “real improvements in people’s lives.” Liberals pounced, conservatives groaned, and Kasich quickly went into damage control mode, explaining that he was only talking about the Medicaid expansion, which he had already explicitly supported and implemented, and not the rest of the law, which he still officially wants to (say it with me) repealandreplace.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gillespie Today or Kasich Tomorrow', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Midterm Elections (2014)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gillespie, Ed', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kasich, John R', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ohio', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-21T15:50:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec6c9050-4bb3-57e5-acf4-8b4a47516879', 'word_count': 617, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec6c9050-4bb3-57e5-acf4-8b4a47516879'}, {'abstract': 'Curbing emissions has long been a popular cause in the European Union. But leaders have to agree on how to generate and distribute energy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/22/business/international/eu-climate-summit-global-warming.html', 'snippet': 'Curbing emissions has long been a popular cause in the European Union. But leaders have to agree on how to generate and distribute energy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BRUSSELS — The leaders of the 28 members of the European Union are set to meet here on Thursday to reassert their global leadership in climate protection, but they will first need to finesse deep divisions over how to generate and distribute energy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For E.U. Climate Meeting, Deep Divisions and High Stakes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alternative and Renewable Energy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-21T15:53:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'International Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Kanter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kanter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/212601b6-e80d-5f43-a021-e9d29712ee67', 'word_count': 714, 'uri': 'nyt://article/212601b6-e80d-5f43-a021-e9d29712ee67'}, {'abstract': 'The Union’s scorched earth campaign in the Shenandoah Valley was almost defeated at the Battle of Cedar Creek.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/21/sheridans-ride/', 'snippet': 'The Union’s scorched earth campaign in the Shenandoah Valley was almost defeated at the Battle of Cedar Creek.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the 1864 presidential election approached, President Abraham Lincoln’s optimism from the preceding year, fortified by recent victories at Gettysburg and Vicksburg, now seemed wishful thinking. Bloody spring campaigns in Virginia, including The Wilderness, Spotsylvania Courthouse, and Cold Harbor, had turned public opinion in the North against the conflict. Adding insult to injury, in July, Confederate Gen. Jubal Early had marched 14,000 troops to the outskirts of Washington, exchanging artillery fire with Union defenders at Fort Stevens, just 5.5 miles from the White House. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, '