'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cardwell, Diane', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Solar Energy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cisco Systems Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': '3M Company', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kimberly-Clark Corporation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Geographic', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Wildlife Fund', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Geostellar Inc', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-22T11:31:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Energy & Environment ', 'byline': {'original': 'By Diane Cardwell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Diane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cardwell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d492902-0190-57ff-8fd4-b0759d3eee25', 'word_count': 862, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d492902-0190-57ff-8fd4-b0759d3eee25'}, {'abstract': 'As a fight over the city’s political fate unfolds, those who arguably have the most clout with Beijing and the most at stake have maintained a studied silence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/23/world/asia/amid-clamor-over-democracy-hong-kongs-tycoons-keeping-quiet.html', 'snippet': 'As a fight over the city’s political fate unfolds, those who arguably have the most clout with Beijing and the most at stake have maintained a studied silence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — The two events a month ago could scarcely have been more different: As this city’s wealthiest tycoons, impeccably tailored, gathered in an opulent hall in Beijing to meet with President Xi Jinping of China, thousands of scruffy university and high school students hit the streets of Hong Kong, boycotting classes to protest Chinese-imposed limits on voting rights here.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/23/world/23tycoons2/23tycoons2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 797, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/23/world/23tycoons2/23tycoons2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 797}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/23/world/23tycoons2/23tycoons2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 771, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/23/world/23tycoons2/23tycoons2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1542, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/23/world/23tycoons2/23tycoons2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/23/world/23tycoons2/23tycoons2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/23/world/23tycoons2/23tycoons2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Amid Clamor Over Democracy, Hong Kong’s Tycoons Keep Silent', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Amid Clamor Over Democracy, Hong Kong’s Tycoons Keep Silent', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hong Kong', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bradsher, Keith', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Li Ka-shing', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leung Chun-ying', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Xi Jinping', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ip, Regina', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-22T11:33:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Keith Bradsher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Keith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradsher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4da3212e-39fc-504f-953c-9e120dacb72a', 'word_count': 1095, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4da3212e-39fc-504f-953c-9e120dacb72a'}, {'abstract': 'A recent report that Chinese tourists allowed their 3-year-old child to urinate in an upscale Taipei restaurant has raised complaints in Taiwan about the behavior of mainland visitors.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/22/tempest-over-a-chamber-pot-as-taiwan-scrutinizes-chinese-tourists-manners/', 'snippet': 'A recent report that Chinese tourists allowed their 3-year-old child to urinate in an upscale Taipei restaurant has raised complaints in Taiwan about the behavior of mainland visitors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reports of Chinese tourists letting their toddlers urinate in public in Taipei have set off fresh debate about the manners of mainland visitors and whether they’re being unfairly stigmatized.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tempest Over a Chamber Pot as Taiwan Scrutinizes Chinese Tourists’ Manners', 'kicker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ma Ying-jeou', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Xi Jinping', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'APPLE DAILY', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Next Media Ltd', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beijing (China)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hong Kong', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Taipei (Taiwan)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Taiwan', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-22T11:37:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Austin Ramzy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Austin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ramzy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/121a2866-ea26-5800-8e37-6921b9c0a4bb', 'word_count': 741, 'uri': 'nyt://article/121a2866-ea26-5800-8e37-6921b9c0a4bb'}, {'abstract': 'Federal authorities are stepping up efforts to fortify big banks against cyberattacks. | States are easing laws that helped poor borrowers. | Google places a big bet on augmented reality. | Amgen is under pressure to break up.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/10/22/morning-agenda-push-to-bolster-banks-cyberdefenses/', 'snippet': 'Federal authorities are stepping up efforts to fortify big banks against cyberattacks. | States are easing laws that helped poor borrowers. | Google places a big bet on augmented reality. | Amgen is under pressure to break up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Federal and state authorities are stepping up efforts to fortify banks and brokerage firms after this summer’s cyberattack on JPMorgan Chase and a dozen other financial institutions, Jessica Silver-Greenberg and Matthew Goldstein report in DealBook. Top officials at the Treasury Department are said to be discussing the need to improve a critical area of cybersecurity: outside vendors, including law firms, accounting and marketing firms and even janitorial companies. Under discussion is a requirement that the banks put in place more stringent procedures and safeguards to make sure the outside firms have, at the least, basic defenses.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: Push to Bolster Banks’ Cyberdefenses', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-22T11:39:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/29a2fba5-a9cf-521a-bdb4-766c8e9bc3dd', 'word_count': 2318, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29a2fba5-a9cf-521a-bdb4-766c8e9bc3dd'}, {'abstract': 'Karl Rove’s American Crossroads groups is opening an expensive campaign against Senator Mark Udall of Colorado in the last days of the campaign.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/politics/first-draft/2014/10/22/rove-group-ups-its-attack-on-colorado-senator/', 'snippet': 'Karl Rove’s American Crossroads groups is opening an expensive campaign against Senator Mark Udall of Colorado in the last days of the campaign.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the Senate race in Colorado tightening, Karl Rove’s American Crossroads groups have pummeled Senator Mark Udall, the Democratic incumbent, with millions of dollars in negative ads. Crossroads begins its biggest assault on Wednesday: a $3.5 million barrage attacking Mr. Udall’s national security credentials.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/22/us/politics/22firstdraft-rove/22firstdraft-rove-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/22/us/politics/22firstdraft-rove/22firstdraft-rove-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/22/us/politics/22firstdraft-rove/22firstdraft-rove-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/22/us/politics/22firstdraft-rove/22firstdraft-rove-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/22/us/politics/22firstdraft-rove/22firstdraft-rove-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/22/us/politics/22firstdraft-rove/22firstdraft-rove-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/22/us/politics/22firstdraft-rove/22firstdraft-rove-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rove Group Ups Its Attack on Colorado Senator', 'kicker': 'First Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Senate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Midterm Elections (2014)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gardner, Cory S', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rove, Karl', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Udall, Mark', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Crossroads', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Colorado', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-22T12:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy W. Peters', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Peters', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6fac1635-6ce9-50a3-8b8e-d0cd8021fa87', 'word_count': 199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6fac1635-6ce9-50a3-8b8e-d0cd8021fa87'}, {'abstract': 'Changes in the state’s population have helped create a political climate far more favorable to Republicans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/23/upshot/arkansas-a-demographic-challenge-for-democrats.html', 'snippet': 'Changes in the state’s population have helped create a political climate far more favorable to Republicans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Arkansas was one of the most reliably Democratic states of the 20th century. It voted for Southern Democrats like Bill Clinton and Jimmy Carter by large margins. Al Gore lost the state in 2000, but by only a modest five percentage points. When Mark Pryor was first elected senator there in 2002, Arkansas was still fairly fertile ground for Democrats. The son of a Democratic senator, he defeated an incumbent Republican by a comfortable eight points in a good year 