ank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-22T15:02:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51cb0863-a192-5ca1-ae96-2bee8f8c6350', 'word_count': 1000, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51cb0863-a192-5ca1-ae96-2bee8f8c6350'}, {'abstract': 'A program administered by the League of American Orchestras with New Music USA brings 12 composers and 12 orchestras together.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/22/pairing-composers-and-orchestras-with-an-eye-on-younger-audiences/', 'snippet': 'A program administered by the League of American Orchestras with New Music USA brings 12 composers and 12 orchestras together.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The League of American Orchestras has always encouraged its members to champion new music, but lately it has been particularly active in establishing programs to bring composers and orchestras together. Music by the youngest generation of composers has a proven appeal to the younger audiences that orchestras are trying to reach, and as those audiences are drawn in they are finding the appeal in older contemporary (and earlier) works as well.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pairing Composers and Orchestras, With an Eye on Younger Audiences', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'League of American Orchestras', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mellon Foundation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Music USA', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-22T15:09:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/09557124-179a-5563-bef4-26f39b21912f', 'word_count': 233, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09557124-179a-5563-bef4-26f39b21912f'}, {'abstract': 'From the shadows I lift my pen to write. I search for love, goodness and a perpetually beating heart.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/23/opinion/Yan-Lianke-finding-light-in-chinas-darkness.html', 'snippet': 'From the shadows I lift my pen to write. I search for love, goodness and a perpetually beating heart.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — China’s efforts to promote socialism in the late 1950s and early 1960s resulted in what is euphemistically known as the three years of natural disasters, during which more than 30 million people starved to death. One evening when I was a young boy, not long after the catastrophe, I followed my mother as she went to dump garbage outside the wall that surrounded our village, a poor and isolated town in central China.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/23/opinion/23yan/23yan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 429, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/23/opinion/23yan/23yan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 429}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/23/opinion/23yan/23yan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 733, 'width': 1024,