N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warren, Elizabeth', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-23T11:09:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e9837d7-d08e-51d6-ade8-667207d57f8c', 'word_count': 1349, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e9837d7-d08e-51d6-ade8-667207d57f8c'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: Soviet agents entered China in 1939.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/23/1939-soviets-push-into-china/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: Soviet agents entered China in 1939.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKIO — Soviet political agents supported by the Red Army have penetrated into northwestern China and also into Sinkiang — Chinese Turkestan — according to a report of the official Domei Agency. The agency asserted, despite Moscow denials, that the Soviet agents have penetrated as far as Lanchow, capital of Kanau Province — the first Chinese province east of Sinkiang. ‘‘Lanchow is now a typical Red town and its air force is six times larger than two years ago,’’ the agency said. ‘‘All foreigners except Soviet Russians are withdrawing from northwestern China.’’ — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Oct. 24, 1939', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1939: Soviets Push Into China', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'USSR (Former Soviet Union)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-23T11:40:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/087108d7-a14b-57d9-bab7-a66a0b3692d5', 'word_count': 102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/087108d7-a14b-57d9-bab7-a66a0b3692d5'}, {'abstract': 'The government has been giving mortgage bankers nearly everything they want. | Some borrowers sidelined after the housing crisis are beginning to come back. | The white-collar defense lawyers of Washington see banks as victims.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/10/23/morning-agenda-easing-rules-for-mortgage-lending/', 'snippet': 'The government has been giving mortgage bankers nearly everything they want. | Some borrowers sidelined after the housing crisis are beginning to come back. | The white-collar defense lawyers of Washington see banks as victims.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The government has been giving mortgage bankers nearly everything they want, DealBook’s Peter Eavis reports. This week, after the bankers pushed federal agencies to loosen regulations on the home loan market, the regulators seemed happy to make the adjustments, relaxing agreements intended to help shield taxpayers from losses on bad mortgages and watering down a regulation that aimed to set safe standards for home loans.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: Easing Rules for Mortgage Lending', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-23T11:42