1:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a95ae2a3-82da-5057-809f-2eb8ec6e24e1', 'word_count': 598, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a95ae2a3-82da-5057-809f-2eb8ec6e24e1'}, {'abstract': 'With slim funding and artifacts being harder to obtain, small museums connected with religious-affiliated schools struggle to maintain their appeal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/arts/artsspecial/biblical-era-collections-suffer-in-a-new-world-of-archaeology.html', 'snippet': 'With slim funding and artifacts being harder to obtain, small museums connected with religious-affiliated schools struggle to maintain their appeal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a few months, the Siegfried Horn Museum at Andrews University in Berrien Springs, Mich., will exhibit 48 ancient fragments of possibly religious ceramic figures depicting humans and horses. Dating from the 8th to the 6th century B.C., they were excavated over more than a decade ending in 2012 at Tall Jalul in Jordan. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Biblical-Era Collections Suffer in a New World of Archaeology', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Biblical-Era Collections Suffer in a New World of Archaeology', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHAEOLOGY', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-23T17:31:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geraldine Fabrikant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geraldine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabrikant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/10467823-00b4-50ca-bec5-87833d1a074e', 'word_count': 931, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10467823-00b4-50ca-bec5-87833d1a074e'}, {'abstract': 'The Baltimore Museum of Art plans to unveil more phases of its face-lift to coincide with its 100th anniversary, aiming to integrate the local art scene.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/business/revitalized-baltimore-museum-of-art-broadens-its-scope.html', 'snippet': 'The Baltimore Museum of Art plans to unveil more phases of its face-lift to coincide with its 100th anniversary, aiming to integrate the local art scene.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For decades, a large bronze cast of Rodin’s “The Thinker” greeted visitors to the Baltimore Museum of Art as they ascended a grand staircase into the luminous columned lobby that was the museum’s heart. Yet when Doreen Bolger became the B.M.A.’s director 16 years ago, this impressive entrance to John Russell Pope’s Classical Revival building had long been sealed off. It was not accessible to people with disabilities, Ms. Bolger explained, nor was it buffered for proper climate control. In 1982, when it was shuttered, the maintenance of two entrances was a cost Baltimore could not afford.', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/artsspecial/26BALTIMORE/26BALTIMORE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/artsspecial/26BALTIMORE/26BALTIMORE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': '