'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eve M. Kahn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e53c9de0-5ac3-541f-b79d-c62ad3e7b6ba', 'word_count': 950, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e53c9de0-5ac3-541f-b79d-c62ad3e7b6ba'}, {'abstract': 'Bartholomeus Spranger, a 16th-century artist who served a cardinal, a pope, and two Holy Roman Emperors, is the subject of a forthcoming show at the Met.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/24/arts/design/metropolitan-museum-spotlights-bartholomeus-spranger.html', 'snippet': 'Bartholomeus Spranger, a 16th-century artist who served a cardinal, a pope, and two Holy Roman Emperors, is the subject of a forthcoming show at the Met.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Flemish artist Bartholomeus Spranger was something of a celebrity in late-16th-century Europe. He secured the patronage of Cardinal Alessandro Farnese of Italy and Pope Pius V; was the court painter to Emperor Maximilian II in Vienna and served Emperor Rudolf II of Austria. In Prague, by the late 1580s, his renown had grown as he became known as a draftsman working in a style that closely resembled that of the Italian Mannerists. He created paintings based on allegories and depicted sexy nudes. But as famous as he was then, his name remains relatively obscure to American audiences today.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24VOGEL/24VOGEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 853, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24VOGEL/24VOGEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 853}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'imag