', 'snippet': 'In his show at the Metropolitan Room, T. Oliver Reid provides a singing tour of Harlem nightclubs in the 1930s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is a measure of our continued nervousness about the subject of race that “(What Did I Do to Be So) Black and Blue,” the classic Fats Waller-Andy Razaf-Harry Brooks lament, still makes listeners squirm. The unhappy narrator of this song, introduced in the 1929 musical “Hot Chocolates,” couldn’t put it more bluntly: “I’m white inside, it don’t help my case/’Cause I can’t hide what’s on my face.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24REID/REID-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24REID/REID-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24REID/REID-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24REID/REID-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1330, 'width': 1998, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24REID/24REID-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24REID/24REID-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24REID/24REID-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType'