': 'Two exhibitions by Sharon Horvath, “Cosmicomics” and “Varanasi Notebook,” take their inspiration from India.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sharon Horvath’s personable reprises of maps, sky charts, street plans, garden designs and baseball diamonds have long attracted admirers. Their palette is suave, their details rich, their motifs abstracted yet familiar. But they have often seemed as if more could have been made of them.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24HORVATHGALLERY5/24HORVATHGALLERY5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 613, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24HORVATHGALLERY5/24HORVATHGALLERY5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 613}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24HORVATHGALLERY5/24HORVATHGALLERY5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1003, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24HORVATHGALLERY5/24HORVATHGALLERY5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2005, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24HORVATHGALLERY5/24HORVATHGALLERY5-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24HORVATHGALLERY5/24HORVATHGALLERY5-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24HORVATHGALLERY5/24HORVATHGALLERY5-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sharon Horvath: ‘Cosmicomics’ and ‘Varanasi Notebook’', 'kicker': 'Art in Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sharon Horvath: ‘Cosmicomics’ and ‘Varanasi Notebook’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Horvath, Sharon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-23T21:50:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2da7097f-6492-58b1-b87f-485a98fcbe15', 'word_count': 285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2da7097f-6492-58b1-b87f-485a98fcbe15'}, {'abstract': 'Michelle Grabner’s show at James Cohan Gallery highlights her life as a professor and mother.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/24/arts/design/michelle-grabner.html', 'snippet': 'Michelle Grabner’s show at James Cohan Gallery highlights her life as a professor and mother.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the gallery’s foyer, a five-minute video by David Robbins called “A Few Minutes With ... Michelle Grabner” orients viewers to the bland art and enviable lifestyle of Michelle Grabner, a professor at the School of the Art Institute of Chicago and best known lately for being one of the curators for this year’s Whitney Biennial.  We see Ms. Grabner picking vegetables from her backyard garden and making a pie in her beautiful kitchen. In her lovely studio in a small house, and also in her backyard, she makes art by weaving strips of colored paper in and out of parallel cuts she’s made in sheets of white paper. She explains that she started doing these “paper weavings” after her son came home with one he’d made in kindergarten, 20 years ago. In the exhibition, scores of Ms. Grabner’s weavings are displayed on knee-high platforms.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24GRABNERGALLERY5/24GRABNERGALLERY5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 511, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24GRABNERGALLERY5/24GRABNERGALLERY5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 511}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24GRABNERGALLERY5/24GRABNERGALLERY5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 872, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24GRABNERGALLERY5/24GRABNERGALLERY5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1278, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24GRABNERGALLERY5/24GRABNERGALLERY5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24GRABNERGALLERY5/24GRABNERGALLERY5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/arts/24GRABNERGALLERY5/24GRABNERGALLERY5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Michelle Grabner', 'kicker': 'Art in Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Michelle Grabner', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grabner, Michelle', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-23T21:52:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3cb45bd-a28c-5689-a49b-044ea92506e9', 'word_count': 280, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3cb45bd-a28c-5689-a49b-044ea92506e9'}, {'abstract': 'Through the end of September, the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee has refunded more than $343,000 to donors this year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/politics/first-draft/2014/10/23/democrats-refund-343000-to-donors-this-year/', 'snippet': 'Through the end of September, the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee has refunded more than $343,000 to donors this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you’ve raised millions of dollars in small donations in response to repeated email solicitations — like the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee has — sometimes donors want their money back. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Democrats Refund $343,000 to Donors This Year', 'kicker': 'First Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Got a Receipt?: $343,000 in Returned Donations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Midterm Elections (2014)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-23T21:54:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Derek Willis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Derek', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Willis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/29f079a1-ef52-5fdd-bbc7-bfe0d125d1ac', 'word_count': 199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29f079a1-ef52-5fdd-bbc7-bfe0d125d1ac'}, {'abstract': '“Grand Design,” at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, presents 19 large Renaissance tapestries designed by Pieter Coecke van Aelst.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/24/arts/design/grand-design-showcases-pieter-coecke-tapestries-at-the-met.html', 'snippet': '“Grand Design,” at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, presents 19 large Renaissance tapestries designed by Pieter Coecke van Aelst.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Less than two decades into the 21st century, the Metropolitan Museum of Art has already mounted three spectacular shows of European tapestries. The latest is “Grand Design: Pieter Coecke van Aelst and Renaissance Tapestry,” which handily lives up to the standard set by its epic forerunners, the 2002 “Tapestry in the Renaissance: Art and Magnificence” and the 2007 “Tapestry in the Baroque: Threads of Splendor.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Weft and Warp, Earth, Heaven and Hell ', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Weft and Warp, Earth, Heaven and Hell', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Textiles', 'rank': 3, 