ormer editor of Rupert Murdoch’s British tabloid newspapers who became an executive before resigning and standing trial in the phone hacking scandal, is in New York and has been meeting people at News Corporation to explore her next professional moves, the company and people familiar with the matter said on Thursday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rebekah Brooks, Former British Tabloid Executive, Exploring Work in U.S.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News of the World', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, Rebekah', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coulson, Andy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T00:51:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ravi Somaiya', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ravi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Somaiya', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1abe7310-45ce-539b-be44-0d6ec1a5f42e', 'word_count': 266, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1abe7310-45ce-539b-be44-0d6ec1a5f42e'}, {'abstract': 'A gunman exploits the openness at the heart of Canada’s democracy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/24/opinion/ottawa-shooting-shattering-the-peace-on-parliament-hill.html', 'snippet': 'A gunman exploits the openness at the heart of Canada’s democracy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OTTAWA — HERE in Canada’s capital, Parliament Hill is about as majestic as public spaces get. The Parliament buildings, somber and gothic, push into the sky above the river. An expanse of green lawn slopes down to Wellington Street with its tourists and a hot dog vendor. The whole place would be imposing if the locals treated it with deference. But we don’t.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shattering the Peace on Parliament Hill', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shattering the Peace on Parliament Hill', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Petrou, Michael', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ottawa (Ontario)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cirillo, Nathan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zehaf-Bibeau, Michael', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T00:52:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Petrou', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Petrou', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8d83d9e-0d8c-57a5-87f4-4e423d63a50a', 'word_count': 923, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8d83d9e-0d8c-57a5-87f4-4e423d63a50a'}, {'abstract': 'Greg Abbott, the Republican who is running for governor of Texas, is riding a wave of television ads all the way to the election.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/24/us/in-ad-spending-greg-abbott-opens-big-margin-in-texas.html', 'snippet': 'Greg Abbott, the Republican who is running for governor of Texas, is riding a wave of television ads all the way to the election.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AUSTIN — It would take more than 10 days to watch all the ad spots on broadcast television that the campaign of Greg Abbott, the Republican candidate for Texas governor, has bought from July to Election Day.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/us/TTTVads/TTTVads-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/24/us/TTTVads/TTTVads-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/us/TTTVads/TTTVads-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 530, 'width': 796, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/us/TTTVads/TTTVads-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 530, 'width': 796, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/24/us/TTTVads/TTTVads-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/24/us/TTTVads/TTTVads-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Ad Spending, Greg Abbott Opens Big Margin', 'kicker': 'The Texas Tribune', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Airtime, a Candidate Looks Invincible', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Governors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Abbott, Gregory W (1957- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Wendy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Midterm Elections (2014)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T00:56:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Ayala and Bobby Blanchard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ayala', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Bobby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Blanchard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/32d1e8d9-224a-59b0-9449-8c57068cfc16', 'word_count': 608, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32d1e8d9-224a-59b0-9449-8c57068cfc16'}, {'abstract': 'A.I.G. shareholders may be suing the government over their treatment, but it’s the American public that suffered the most.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/24/opinion/the-real-bailout-victims.html', 'snippet': 'A.I.G. shareholders may be suing the government over their treatment, but it’s the American public that suffered the most.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The trial in the suit by Maurice Greenberg against the United States has segued this month from star-witness testimony to routine evidence gathering. As the case proceeds, the farce at its center becomes ever more clear.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Real Bailout Victims ', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Real Bailout Victims', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenberg, Maurice R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emergency Economic Stabilization Act (2008)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN INTERNATIONAL GROUP INC', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T00:56:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b7261a5-6704-5c7e-a839-879da870cc8a', 'word_count': 369, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b7261a5-6704-5c7e-a839-879da870cc8a'}, {'abstract': 'The 21-day monitoring program will be more effective than a travel ban, and it won’t discourage people from going abroad to help fight the epidemic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/24/opinion/beyond-screening-for-ebola.html', 'snippet': 'The 21-day monitoring program will be more effective than a travel ban, and it won’t discourage people from going abroad to help fight the epidemic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The new monitoring rules to be placed on travelers coming into the United States from three Ebola-affected countries in West Africa form a smart and workable response to a complex public health question. The measures should be more effective than a misguided ban on all travelers from Guinea, Liberia and Sierra Leone, which many in Congress have been demanding.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Beyond Screening for Ebola', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beyond Screening for Ebola', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Airport Security', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Epidemics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guinea', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Liberia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sierra Leone', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel Warnings', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ebola Virus', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T00:59:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee92df5a-3b48-56e4-9463-141c24d06e1a', 'word_count': 435, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee92df5a-3b48-56e4-9463-141c24d06e1a'}, {'abstract': 'Schools across the country have a moral and legal obligation to teach children regardless of their immigration status.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/24/opinion/a-betrayal-of-migrant-children-and-the-law.html', 'snippet': 'Schools across the country have a moral and legal obligation to teach children regardless of their immigration status.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When hundreds of unaccompanied children — part of a recent surge of thousands of migrants stopped at the Southern border after fleeing violence and poverty in Guatemala, Honduras and El Salvador — were sent by federal officials to stay with relatives or other sponsors on Long Island, many found themselves blocked at the schoolhouse door. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Betrayal of Migrant Children, and the Law', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Betrayal of Migrant Children, and the Law', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island (NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hempstead (NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T01:01:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec962170-402c-50ba-be83-41a23d139391', 'word_count': 283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec962170-402c-50ba-be83-41a23d139391'}, {'abstract': 'A setback in Kobani, Syria, would show the fragility of the American operation and hand the Islamic State an important victory.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/24/opinion/why-kobani-must-be-saved.html', 'snippet': 'A setback in Kobani, Syria, would show the fragility of the American operation and hand the Islamic State an important victory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If Kobani survives, it will have defied the odds. This embattled city on Syria’s northern border with Turkey has been on the verge of falling for weeks in the face of a brutal siege by Islamic State militants. But the Syrian Kurds who call Kobani home continue to fight hard, and on Sunday the United States made airdrops of weapons and other supplies to bolster them. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype'