gon means the Americans will retain control of forces in the event of war until the South Korean military is better prepared.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEOUL, South Korea — The United States has moved to ease jitters among conservative South Koreans by agreeing to delay the return of wartime control of the South Korean military to Seoul until its forces are better prepared to deter North Korea’s nuclear and missile threats or fight it in a war.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. and South Korea Agree to Delay Shift in Wartime Command', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Seoul (South Korea)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Park Geun-hye', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T10:20:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Choe Sang-Hun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Choe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sang-Hun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c75a25d1-08c2-56ea-873e-a6d2bb3a05a0', 'word_count': 878, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c75a25d1-08c2-56ea-873e-a6d2bb3a05a0'}, {'abstract': 'The American artist Paul McCarthy has infuriated Parisians with a suggestive sculpture in Paris’s Place de Vendôme. Are the French suddenly prudish, or did Mr. McCarthy cross a line?', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/24/is-a-sculpture-that-resembles-a-sex-toy-a-national-scandal/', 'snippet': 'The American artist Paul McCarthy has infuriated Parisians with a suggestive sculpture in Paris’s Place de Vendôme. Are the French suddenly prudish, or did Mr. McCarthy cross a line?', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Oct.\xa016, the American artist Paul McCarthy installed a sculpture called “Tree” in the Place de Vendôme in Paris. The sculpture was presented in advance of FIAC!, Paris’s contemporary art fair, which opens Oct. 23. Mr. McCarthy was given prime real estate to display it: The Place de Vendôme is considered one of the most picturesque squares in one of the chicest quarters of central Paris. It contains both the Ritz Hotel and the Justice Ministry, as well as the Vendôme Column, a monolith erected by Napoleon I to commemorate the battle of Austerlitz.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/22/opinion/nyto-sculpture/nyto-sculpture-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 593, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/22/opinion/nyto-sculpture/nyto-sculpture-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 593}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/22/opinion/nyto-sculpture/nyto-sculpture-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1012, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/22/opinion/nyto-sculpture/nyto-sculpture-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1977, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/22/opinion/nyto-sculpture/nyto-sculpture-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/22/opinion/nyto-sculpture/nyto-sculpture-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/22/opinion/nyto-sculpture/nyto-sculpture-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Is a Sculpture That Resembles a Sex Toy a National Scandal?', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T10:24:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anna Altman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Altman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04a63305-72bb-5345-adb3-d6abefffa4c2', 'word_count': 847, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04a63305-72bb-5345-adb3-d6abefffa4c2'}, {'abstract': 'A slideshow of arts events taking place across the world this coming week.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/24/international-arts-events-happening-in-the-week-ahead-16/', 'snippet': 'A slideshow of arts events taking place across the world this coming week.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'International Arts Events Happening in the Week Ahead', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T10:47:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher D. Shea', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7831b273-44cb-5814-ab0a-71ff55698aa6', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7831b273-44cb-5814-ab0a-71ff55698aa6'}, {'abstract': 'Can China continue its torrid pace of economic growth for decades to come? The long arc of history suggests not, according to a paper by Harvard’s Lant Pritchett and Lawrence Summers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/upshot/china-will-keep-growing-just-ask-the-soviets.html', 'snippet': 'Can China continue its torrid pace of economic growth for decades to come? The long arc of history suggests not, according to a paper by Harvard’s Lant Pritchett and Lawrence Summers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There has been plenty of discussion lately about signs that China’s economy is slowing down, focused on details of a possible housing bubble and vast sums of bad loans that the country will have to reckon with. But put aside the challenges China faces this quarter, or next year, and there is one view that is overwhelming: China is a long-term economic juggernaut that will stand astride the global economy in another generation’s time.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why China Won’t Keep Growing Fast Forever', 'kicker': 'Economic View', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Recalculating the Chinese Century', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Irwin, Neil', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gross Domestic Product', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economics (Theory and Philosophy)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Summers, Lawrence H', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pritchett, Lant', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T11:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Irwin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Irwin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc447e78-04fe-5b88-bd14-3384ce901f5b', 'word_count': 1274, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc447e78-04fe-5b88-bd14-3384ce901f5b'}, {'abstract': 'The Jets coach could change his reputation and the league’s by starting the fourth-down revolution in football.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/25/upshot/how-rex-ryan-can-save-his-job-and-help-reinvent-the-nfl.html', 'snippet': 'The Jets coach could change his reputation and the league’s by starting the fourth-down revolution in football.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Jets’ season, technically not half complete, is in effect over. With the team 1-6, a playoff berth is all but impossible. Rex Ryan’s job may be beyond saving, but there is something he could do in his remaining games to change his reputation and the league’s: He could start the fourth-down revolution in the N.F.L.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/25/upshot/25up-ryan/25up-ryan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/25/upshot/25up-ryan/25up-ryan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/25/upshot/25up-ryan/25up-ryan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo