imself a private eye in pursuit of criminals. Contemplating landmarks and the lives of writers who came before him, Rosenblatt “walks the streets like a poetic stray, embracing chance and accident,” Pete Hamill wrote here.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T14:57:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/8312946d-b356-593e-a02d-67fe860ecbd9', 'word_count': 490, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8312946d-b356-593e-a02d-67fe860ecbd9'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE INVISIBLE HISTORY OF THE HUMAN RACE: How DNA and History Shape Our Identities and Our Futures,  by Christine Kenneally. (Viking, $27.95.) Kenneally takes a smart and highly entertaining look at the revelations DNA can provide.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T14:59:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/010b042d-df7a-50a0-94bb-c8a61a90a15c', 'word_count': 251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/010b042d-df7a-50a0-94bb-c8a61a90a15c'}, {'abstract': 'You can spend tens of millions on a high-end condo, but don’t forget to budget for a place to store your skis or house your children’s au pair.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/realestate/in-new-yorks-luxury-apartments-amenities-are-costly-add-ons.html', 'snippet': 'You can spend tens of millions on a high-end condo, but don’t forget to budget for a place to store your skis or house your children’s au pair.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The uninitiated buyer could be forgiven for thinking that an apartment with a price tag of $10 million, $20 million or much more comes fully loaded with amenities. But as prices for ultraluxury condos in Manhattan continue to climb, practically every extra inch in these fancy new buildings — be it in storage units in the basement or in staff quarters on a separate floor — is being sold à la carte for extraordinary sums.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/realestate/26COVER_COMBO/26COVER_COMBO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 414, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/26/realestate/26COVER_COMBO/26COVER_COMBO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 414}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/realestate/26COVER_COMBO/26COVER_COMBO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 706, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'capti