/26diagnosis1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/magazine/26diagnosis1/26diagnosis1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Pain in the Neck', 'kicker': 'Diagnosis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T15:41:20+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By LISA SANDERS, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'SANDERS', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/8e0600f5-6d29-5fca-8d31-8e89150d0656', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/8e0600f5-6d29-5fca-8d31-8e89150d0656'}, {'abstract': 'Corporate cash, all dressed up with nowhere to go?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/24/the-profits-investment-disconnect/', 'snippet': 'Corporate cash, all dressed up with nowhere to go?', 'lead_paragraph': 'I caught a bit of CNBC in the locker room this morning, and they were talking about stock buybacks. Oddly — or maybe not that oddly, given my own experiences with the show — nobody brought up what I would have thought was the obvious question. Profits are very high, so why are companies concluding that they should return cash to stockholders rather than use it to expand their businesses?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Profits-Investment Disconnect', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T15:41:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa240b7a-83fe-509c-99e8-1242f02fc4fb', 'word_count': 167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa240b7a-83fe-509c-99e8-1242f02fc4fb'}, {'abstract': 'In Edward St. Aubyn’s novel, a large cast of seekers cross paths at Esalen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/books/review/edward-st-aubyn-on-the-edge-review.html', 'snippet': 'In Edward St. Aubyn’s novel, a large cast of seekers cross paths at Esalen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s an interesting statistic regarding the British novelist Edward St. Aubyn: Since The New York Times Book Review initiated its “By the Book” column in 2012, his Patrick Melrose pentalogy has been favorably mentioned almost a dozen times by the authors being interviewed, among them Donna Tartt, Michael Chabon, Amy Bloom, Jonathan Lethem, Maria Semple, James Patterson and (full disclosure) myself.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26LeavittSUVB/26LeavittSUVB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 383, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26LeavittSUVB/26LeavittSUVB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 383}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26LeavittSUVB/26LeavittSUVB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 654, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26LeavittSUVB/26LeavittSUVB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1307, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26LeavittSUVB/26LeavittSUVB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26LeavittSUVB/26LeavittSUVB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26LeavittSUVB/26LeavittSUVB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Edward St. Aubyn’s ‘On the Edge’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Old New Age', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'St Aubyn, Edward', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T15:43:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leavitt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leavitt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b07ab2fa-fdf5-5b0b-bf28-75c5405b667e', 'word_count': 902, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b07ab2fa-fdf5-5b0b-bf28-75c5405b667e'}, {'abstract': 'A 500-year history of apparitions, poltergeists, séances and our longing to believe in the paranormal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/books/review/ghosts-a-natural-history-by-roger-clarke.html', 'snippet': 'A 500-year history of apparitions, poltergeists, séances and our longing to believe in the paranormal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Close to the end of Roger Clarke’s “Ghosts: A Natural History,” the author mentions “silent phone calls from people who have been buried with their phone in their coffin.” Who are these people? He doesn’t say, but he claims there’s a whole genre of “apparently true” mobile phone ghost stories, including “texts from the dead.” There are even haunted spell-checks. When the name “Prudentia” was highlighted on a document during a 1998 investigation in Britain, the alternative spellings that reportedly came up were “dead,” “buried” and “cellar.” We’re not told if investigators dug up the cellar, and if they did, whether they found Prudentia.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26mcgrath/26mcgrath-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 798, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26mcgrath/26mcgrath-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 798}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26mcgrath/26mcgrath-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 770, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26mcgrath/26mcgrath-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1540, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26mcgrath/26mcgrath-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26mcgrath/26mcgrath-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26mcgrath/26mcgrath-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Ghosts: A Natural History,’ by Roger Clarke', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spooked', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clarke, Roger', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ghosts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T15:43:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick McGrath', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGrath', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c73792e4-aabf-5f04-b88a-b79e5c420f3e', 'word_count': 1175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c73792e4-aabf-5f04-b88a-b79e5c420f3e'}, {'abstract': 'Folk and fairy tales are loosely reimagined and rendered with scrupulous realism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/books/review/jean-thompsons-the-witch-and-other-tales-re-told.html', 'snippet': 'Folk and fairy tales are loosely reimagined and rendered with scrupulous realism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The practice of retelling fairy tales in the form of literary fiction is, if not quite hallowed, certainly established. The great Angela Carter’s revelatory 1979 story collection, “The Bloody Chamber” — a brocaded work of heady sensuality, intelligence and violence — remains the benchmark, but Kate Bernheimer’s Fairy Tale Review and the several excellent Bernheimer-edited anthologies spun off from it carry the standard forward. Those are just some of the more overt homages; Western literature owes as much to fairy tales as it does to Greek myth and the Bible. In the right light, “Pride and Prejudice” is a retelling of “Beauty and the Beast.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/1026-bks-Miller/1026-bks-Miller-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/1026-bks-Miller/1026-bks-Miller-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/1026-bks-Miller/1026-bks-Miller-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 769, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/1026-bks-Miller/1026-bks-Miller-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1710, 'width': 1283, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/1026-bks-Miller/1026-bks-Miller-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/1026-bks-Miller/1026-bks-Miller-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/1026-bks-Miller/1026-bks-Miller-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jean Thompson’s ‘The Witch: And Other Tales Re-told’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Twice Upon a Time', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thompson, Jean', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fairy Tales', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T15:44:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laura Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c4f11f1-29ea-547d-a222-02bd3d32bf4f', 'word_count': 1096, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c4f11f1-29ea-547d-a222-02bd3d32bf4f'}, {'abstract': 'An 1816 “ghost story” contest had lasting literary consequences.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/books/review/the-poet-and-the-vampyre-by-andrew-mcconnell-stott.html', 'snippet': 'An 1816 “ghost story” contest had lasting literary consequences.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Trading ghost stories before the fire has, reflexively, become the stuff of fiction. Creating one’s own tales has not. In the summer of 1816, Lord Byron; his doctor, John Polidori; the poet Percy \xadBysshe Shelley; and Shelley’s lover, Mary Godwin, did just that, hatching, with improbable coincidence, “Frankenstein” and “The Vampyre,” the slender precursor to Bram Sto\xadker’s “Dracula.” Andrew McConnell Stott’s “The Poet and the Vampyre” examines the fateful gathering’s personalities, promise and perils.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26CARTER/2Carter-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 808, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26CARTER/2Carter-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 808}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26CARTER/2Carter-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 761, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26CARTER/2Carter-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1522, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26CARTER/2Carter-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26CARTER/2Carter-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26CARTER/2Carter-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Poet and the Vampyre,’ by Andrew McConnell Stott', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fright Night', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stott, Andrew McConnell', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Byron, Lord', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shelley, Percy Bysshe', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T15:44:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maxwell Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maxwell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7acae6f-b3d7-53bb-9406-359e99fb02be', 'word_count': 620, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e7acae6f-b3d7-53bb-9406-359e99fb02be'}, {'abstract': 'Keats, Wordsworth and Lamb attend a famous dinner party.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/books/review/the-immortal-evening-by-stanley-plumly.html', 'snippet': 'Keats, Wordsworth and Lamb attend a famous dinner party.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Dec. 28, 1817, Benjamin Robert \xadHaydon, then England’s pre-eminent history painter, hosted a dinner party to celebrate his progress on his latest work, “Christ’s Entry Into Jerusalem.” He invited, among others, three men anachronistically pictured in that painting: John Keats, William Wordsworth and the essayist Charles Lamb. In “The Immortal Evening” (the phrase is from Haydon’s letters and diaries), the poet and biographer Stanley Plumly offers an idiosyncratic, heartfelt, at once sinuous and expansive exploration of the dinner, its “aesthetic context and the larger worlds of the individual guests, particularly the three ‘immortal’ writers, Keats, \xadWordsworth and Lamb.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26GilmanSUB/26GilmanSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 504, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26GilmanSUB/26GilmanSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 504}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26GilmanSUB/26GilmanSUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 860, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26GilmanSUB/26GilmanSUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1720, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26GilmanSUB/26GilmanSUB-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26GilmanSUB/26GilmanSUB-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/26GilmanSUB/26GilmanSUB-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Immortal Evening,’ by Stanley Plumly', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Omnium-Gatherum', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Plumly, Stanley', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wordsworth, William', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keats, John (1795-1821)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lamb, Charles', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T15:44:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Priscilla Gilman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Priscilla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gilman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1e07153-b263-52c8-9a0e-79d0fa7bf218', 'word_count': 636, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1e07153-b263-52c8-9a0e-79d0fa7bf218'}, {'abstract': 'Must the revolutionary artist ignore the basic laws of decency that govern our world in order to transform that world?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/books/review/art-of-darkness.html', 'snippet': 'Must the revolutionary artist ignore the basic laws of decency that govern our world in order to transform that world?', 'lead_paragraph': 'To what extent is the price of immortality humanity, as you could put it? Must the revolutionary artist ignore — even flout — the basic laws of decency that govern our world in order to transform that world? “Perfection of the life, or of the work,” as Yeats had it. “And if it take the second,” he went on, the intellect of man “must refuse a heavenly mansion, raging in the dark.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/1026-bks-Iyer-sub/1026-bks-Iyer-sub-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 882, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/1026-bks-Iyer-sub/1026-bks-Iyer-sub-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 882}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/1026-bks-Iyer-sub/1026-bks-Iyer-sub-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 697, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/books/review/1026-bks-Iyer-sub/1026-bks-Iyer-sub-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1850, 'width': 1259, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_na