t rich in luxury goods like textiles and ornamented musical instruments, some of which they carried away as exotic souvenirs, leaving behind things of their own — swords, crucifixes — that were absorbed into local traditions.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/26GUIDE4/26GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 877, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/26GUIDE4/26GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 877}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/26GUIDE4/26GUIDE4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 701, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/26GUIDE4/26GUIDE4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1402, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/26GUIDE4/26GUIDE4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/26GUIDE4/26GUIDE4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/26GUIDE4/26GUIDE4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sailing in Shackles, a Culture Survives', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sailing in Shackles, a Culture Survives', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T20:37:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8839961-d52c-590e-8ec3-6c92e6b50d2a', 'word_count': 172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8839961-d52c-590e-8ec3-6c92e6b50d2a'}, {'abstract': 'Flamenco naturally traverses emotional peaks and valleys, and Soledad Barrio plunges her whole being into every ascent and descent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/arts/dance/tragic-greek-rebel-takes-up-flamenco-.html', 'snippet': 'Flamenco naturally traverses emotional peaks and valleys, and Soledad Barrio plunges her whole being into every ascent and descent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Soledad Barrio and her company, Noche Flamenca, often have a story to tell, even if it’s not explicit. Flamenco naturally traverses emotional peaks and valleys, and Ms. Barrio plunges her whole being into every ascent and descent, from furious to rapturous, sorrowful to playful, confrontational to contemplative. The same can be said for her dancers, musicians and vocalists, with whom she shares a sparkling rapport and profound rhythmic intuition. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/26GUIDE3/26GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 435, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/26GUIDE3/26GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 435}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/26GUIDE3/26GUIDE3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 743, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/26GUIDE3/26GUIDE3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1486, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/26GUIDE3/26GUIDE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/26GUIDE3/26GUIDE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/26GUIDE3/26GUIDE3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tragic Greek Rebel Takes Up Flamenco ', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Dance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tragic Greek Rebel Takes Up Flamenco', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-24T20:38:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Siobhan Burke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Siobhan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/91c3c2f8-bc15-5086-b16b-a564b6a3d58b', 'word_count': 154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91c3c2f8-bc15-5086-b16b-a564b6a3d58b'}, {'abstract': 'The Film Society of Lincoln Center’s annual Scary Movies series opens Friday with the New Zealand horror comedy “What We Do in the Shadows.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/movies/like-friends-but-with-blood-ties.html', 'snippet': 'The Film Society of Lincoln Center’s annual Scary Movies series opens Friday with the New Zealand horror comedy “What We Do in the Shadows.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Timed to coincide with Halloween, the Film Society of Lincoln Center’s annual Scary Movies series opens Friday with the New Zealand horror comedy “What We Do in the Shadows,” which was warmly received at the Sundance Film Festival. Written and directed by Taika Waititi and Jemaine Clement, who also star, this mockumentary about four roommates in Wellington who happen to be vampires has been described as an “undead edition of ‘The Real World.’\xa0” Ranging in age from 183 to 8,000, they go to clubs, bicker over household chores and have a running feud with prim local werewolves, who are embarrassed to be bloodthirsty. Each roommate represents a different era. Mr. Waititi’s character, Viago, is a fastidious dandy with a Nazi past, and Mr. Clement’s Vladislav the Poker is a bloodthirsty orgiast. Rounding out the foursome are Deacon (Jonathan Brugh), the rambunctious baby of the group, and Petyr (Ben Fransham), its grouchy, antisocial senior member. (8 p.m., Walter Reade Theater; filmlinc.com.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/26GUIDE1/26GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 389, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/26GUIDE1/26GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 389}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/26GUIDE1/26GUIDE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}},