 'nyt://article/3f3862a7-9226-567e-a67a-0abf714db9bd'}, {'abstract': 'The potentially fatal MERS virus that originated in Saudi Arabia two years ago has spread to Turkey for the first time, the World Health Organization said Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/25/world/europe/turkey-mers-virus-case-is-reported.html', 'snippet': 'The potentially fatal MERS virus that originated in Saudi Arabia two years ago has spread to Turkey for the first time, the World Health Organization said Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The potentially fatal MERS virus that originated in Saudi Arabia two years ago has spread to Turkey for the first time, the World Health Organization said Friday. In a report on its website, the organization attributed the spread to a 42-year-old Turkish citizen who had been working in the Saudi city of Jidda; he fell ill there last month and flew two weeks ago to the Turkish city of Hatay, where he was hospitalized and died within days. The organization said medical investigators confirmed that the man had suffered from MERS, or Middle East Respiratory Syndrome, and they were checking others in Turkey and Saudi Arabia who had been in contact with him during his symptomatic phase. Saudi Arabia has been under enormous pressure to control the disease, which can incubate in camels and other livestock. The Saudi authorities have been faulted for having allowed MERS to proliferate, particularly in Jidda, Riyadh, the Saudi capital, and the holy city of Mecca, where pilgrims from the entire world converge for the annual hajj. Pilgrims are known to have spread the disease to Iran, Jordan and Algeria. The W.H.O. said that as of Friday, it had tallied 883 MERS cases worldwide, including at least 319 deaths. Most cases are in Saudi Arabia. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Turkey: MERS Virus Case Is Reported', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Turkey: MERS Virus Case Is Reported', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'MERS (Middle East Respiratory Syndrome)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Health Organization', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-25T00:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/78cf515a-95f9-505d-b2eb-be7f1439c336', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78cf515a-95f9-505d-b2eb-be7f1439c336'}, {'abstract': 'A Pakistani court on Friday acquitted one of the main suspects in the 2002 murder of the American journalist Daniel Pearl.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/25/world/asia/pakistan-court-acquits-a-suspect-charged-in-the-murder-of-daniel-pearl.html', 'snippet': 'A Pakistani court on Friday acquitted one of the main suspects in the 2002 murder of the American journalist Daniel Pearl.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Pakistani court on Friday acquitted one of the main suspects in the 2002 murder of the American journalist Daniel Pearl. Mohammad Hashim Qadeer had been accused of helping to arrange a meeting between Mr. Pearl and his abductors, but a judge in an antiterrorism court in Hyderabad acquitted him for lack of evidence. Mr. Pearl, a reporter for The Wall Street Journal, was abducted in January 2002 in Karachi and killed the following month. The main suspect, Ahmed Umar Sheikh, a British-born Pakistani, was convicted of luring Mr. Pearl to the meeting and was sentenced to death in July 2002.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pakistan: Court Acquits a Suspect Charged in the Murder of Daniel Pearl', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pakistan: Court Acquits a Suspect Charged in the Murder of Daniel Pearl', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qadeer, Mohammad Hashim', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pearl, Daniel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wall Street Journal', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-25T00:02:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Salman Masood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Salman', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Masood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b65ef8d-16b0-5af9-adc8-83bcb1e8c840', 'word_count': 101, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b65ef8d-16b0-5af9-adc8-83bcb1e8c840'}, {'abstract': 'At a rally in Boston, Mrs. Clinton praised Senator Elizabeth Warren, a liberal Democrat some see as a potential future rival, but did not appear onstage with her.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/25/us/politics/with-praise-hillary-clinton-keeps-an-adversary-close.html', 'snippet': 'At a rally in Boston, Mrs. Clinton praised Senator Elizabeth Warren, a liberal Democrat some see as a potential future rival, but did not appear onstage with her.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — The two most prominent women in the Democratic Party, whom liberals have cast as potential future rivals, stood backstage and swapped stories about being grandmothers. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The