': {'main': 'Me vs. My Smartphone', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Me vs. My Smartphone', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smartphones', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-25T13:25:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef0cc3e6-b3de-5d32-b060-96762673b135', 'word_count': 113, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef0cc3e6-b3de-5d32-b060-96762673b135'}, {'abstract': 'A Danish nonprofit wants to reduce female soccer players’ injuries. Its answer? A soccer ball that is lighter and slightly smaller in circumference.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/business/selling-a-smaller-soccer-ball.html', 'snippet': 'A Danish nonprofit wants to reduce female soccer players’ injuries. Its answer? A soccer ball that is lighter and slightly smaller in circumference.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At age 12 or 13, many soccer players around the world graduate from the soccer balls designed for children to the larger ones used by professionals. But when a Danish youth soccer coach named Majken Gilmartin watched her daughter’s team make that transition in 2007, she was alarmed by what she saw. ', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/business/PROTO-1/PROTO-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 740, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/26/business/PROTO-1/PROTO-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 740}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/business/PROTO-1/PROTO-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 831, 'subType': 'jumbo',