 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f84b669-0fac-5042-b0ed-534dd0635754', 'word_count': 798, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f84b669-0fac-5042-b0ed-534dd0635754'}, {'abstract': 'The notion that today’s lower deficit must represent economic progress does not withstand scrutiny.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/opinion/sunday/deficit-talk-in-an-election-year.html', 'snippet': 'The notion that today’s lower deficit must represent economic progress does not withstand scrutiny.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is one unmistakably good piece of news on the budget deficit this election season, but self-proclaimed deficit hawks in the Republican Party have no grounds to boast about it: Since 2010, the pace of health care spending has fallen dramatically and with it, projected deficits over the next decade and beyond. The slowdown is evident in all major health programs, including Medicare, Medicaid and subsidies to buy insurance under the Affordable Care Act. The expected budget savings from 2015 to 2024 come to an impressive $1.6 trillion. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Deficit Talk in an Election Year ', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Deficit Talk in an Election Year', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'r