inal': 'By Beckley Mason', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Beckley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mason', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/678305cc-875c-5121-b656-528b1400294b', 'word_count': 917, 'uri': 'nyt://article/678305cc-875c-5121-b656-528b1400294b'}, {'abstract': 'The deadly 1992 siege of a white separatist family at Ruby Ridge prompted a debate about government overreach that changed the way the federal authorities handle volatile situations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/27/us/an-idaho-family-and-federal-tactics-under-siege.html', 'snippet': 'The deadly 1992 siege of a white separatist family at Ruby Ridge prompted a debate about government overreach that changed the way the federal authorities handle volatile situations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Convinced in the 1980s that the end of days was nigh, Randall and Vicki Weaver chose to ride it out in a cabin they built on a mountain crest called Ruby Ridge, in northern Idaho. That stretch of country had attracted its share of omnidirectional haters — of blacks, of Jews, of most modern ways, for sure of a government they viewed as an oppressor. The Weavers regarded themselves as white “separatists,” not supremacists, although they brushed against hate groups often enough. And they had guns, plenty of guns, as they prepared with their children for the apocalypse.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Idaho Family, and Federal Tactics, Under Siege', 'kicker': 'Retro Report', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ruby Ridge (Idaho)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weaver, Randy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weaver, Vicki', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Waco (Tex)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Branch Davidians', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gray, John Joe', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Marshals Service', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fringe Groups and Movements', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-27T00:28:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9bd4b74-a652-505a-8fce-56b0f1ef4f2a', 'word_count': 1296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9bd4b74-a652-505a-8fce-56b0f1ef4f2a'}, {'abstract': 'When armed suspects stand off against the law today, one event continues to cast a shadow on both sides of the police line: the 1992 siege at Ruby Ridge.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/us/100000003197362/ruby-ridge-american-standoff.html', 'snippet': 'When armed suspects stand off against the law today, one event continues to cast a shadow on both sides of the police line: the 1992 siege at Ruby Ridge.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When armed suspects stand off against the law today, one event continues to cast a shadow on both sides of the police line: the 1992 siege at Ruby Ridge.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/25/multimedia/retro-rubyridge/retro-rubyridge-articleLarge-v7.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/25/multimedia/retro-rubyridge/retro-rubyridge-articleLarge-v7.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/25/multimedia/retro-rubyridge/retro-rubyridge-jumbo-v7.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/25/multimedia/retro-rubyridge/retro-rubyridge-superJumbo-v7.jpg', 'height': 1151, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/25/multimedia/retro-rubyridge/retro-rubyridge-thumbStandard-v7.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/25/multimedia/retro-rubyridge/retro-rubyridge-thumbStandard-v7.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/25/multimedia/retro-rubyridge/retro-rubyridge-thumbLarge-v7.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ruby Ridge: American Standoff', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ruby Ridge (Idaho)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weaver, Randy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weaver, Vicki', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gray, John Joe', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fringe Groups and Movements', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-27T00:29:19+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'Retro Report', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Retro', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Report', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/e8465a42-f480-5c1e-abce-3bfd86787f2d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/e8465a42-f480-5c1e-abce-3bfd86787f2d'}, {'abstract': 'The undead may best express our anxieties about capitalism: legions of mindless, soulless consumers who sustain its endless production.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/dreamboat-vampires-and-zombie-capitalists/', 'snippet': 'The undead may best express our anxieties about capitalism: legions of mindless, soulless consumers who sustain its endless production.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If popularity on television and movie screens is any indication, a good number of the costumed ghouls knocking on our doors this October 31 will be the modern West’s two preferred trespassers from the afterlife — vampires and zombies. At first blush, these nightmarish cousins couldn’t be further apart. Vampires — which have been with us in their current form at least since the Industrial Revolution — are fast, sexy, cunning and imperishable. They are sleek nocturnal hunters, and even in the violence of the kill they can be depicted as elegant, like great cats. Zombies — not the Afro-Caribbean variety, controlled by a shaman, but the mindless, flesh-eating dead injected into American popular culture by the filmmaker George Romero with his 1968 film, “Night of the Living Dead” — are most often the opposite: slow, mindless, shambling, putrid excrescences in a state of perpetual decay. Vampires represent a kind of higher place on the food chain — one could at least imagine wanting to become a vampire; the current vogue of carnival-like zombie walks notwithstanding, we can safely assume that no one would want to become a zombie.', 'source': 