7/first-draft-video-how-the-election-could-go-into-overtime/', 'snippet': 'Runoffs, quirky candidates and tight races in a number of states may mean that control of the Senate won’t be decided on election day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Runoffs, quirky candidates and tight races in a number of states may mean that control of the Senate won’t be decided on Election Day.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'First Draft Video: How the Election Could Go Into Overtime', 'kicker': 'First Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Midterm Elections (2014)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-27T11:30:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily B. Hager and Carrie Halperin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7dac8f0-9247-5a74-8dce-b410aaca1a4b', 'word_count': 24, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7dac8f0-9247-5a74-8dce-b410aaca1a4b'}, {'abstract': 'Same as it ever was.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/27/open-letters-of-1933/', 'snippet': 'Same as it ever was.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My friend and old classmate Irwin Collier, of the Free University of Berlin, sends me to an open letter to monetary officials warning of the dangers of printing money and debasing the dollar, claiming that these policies will undermine confidence and threaten to create a renewed financial crisis. But it’s not the famous 2010 letter to Ben Bernanke, whose signatories refuse to admit that they were wrong; it’s a letter sent by Columbia economists in 1933 (pdf):', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Open Letters of 1933', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-27T11:34:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da7e24e7-c54b-5b47-a136-cb39b904a7db', 'word_count': 112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da7e24e7-c54b-5b47-a136-cb39b904a7db'}, {'abstract': 'Most European banks pass stress test. | Steven Ballmer could claim huge tax benefits on his Los Angeles Clippers deal. | William A. Ackman’s outsize bets. | Court ruling disarms shareholders.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/10/27/morning-agenda-assessing-europes-bank-stress-tests/', 'snippet': 'Most European banks pass stress test. | Steven Ballmer could claim huge tax benefits on his Los Angeles Clippers deal. | William A. Ackman’s outsize bets. | Court ruling disarms shareholders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The European Central Bank said on Sunday that of the 130 European banks it reviewed, only 13 lacked sufficient capital to withstand a financial crisis or severe economic downturn, concluding a yearlong audit of eurozone lenders that is potentially a turning point for the region’s battered economy, Jack Ewing reports in DealBook. “The highly anticipated assessment of European banks was intended to remove a cloud of mistrust that has impeded lending in countries like Italy and Greece,” he writes. Markets in Europe were losing enthusiasm in Monday morning trading, as investors sought to assess the results. Stocks in the United States were expected to open lower, based on futures trading.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: Assessing Europe’s Bank Stress Tests', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-27T11:48:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8fa0952-1786-59b4-a8fb-08d0206d28a1', 'word_count': 2094, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8fa0952-1786-59b4-a8fb-08d0206d28a1'}, {'abstract': 'The celebrity is teaming up with Sir Philip Green of Topshop to start a clothing line.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/27/beyonce-and-philip-green-of-topshop-to-start-an-activewear-clothing-line/', 'snippet': 'The celebrity is teaming up with Sir Philip Green of Topshop to start a clothing line.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Activewear? Athleticwear? Sportswear? Does anyone else think we need a better word for the gym-to-street sector that has suddenly become the hottest thing to cover two legs\xa0— at least if the latest entrant to the field is any indication?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/27/fashion/27runway/27runway-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 420, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/27/fashion/27runway/27runway-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 420}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/27/fashion/27runway/27runway-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 718, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/2