h Barro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Josh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/acce0e54-b896-5eb4-bbfb-1c020b9bdbe9', 'word_count': 971, 'uri': 'nyt://article/acce0e54-b896-5eb4-bbfb-1c020b9bdbe9'}, {'abstract': 'Rare opportunities to sample the evolution of 3-D are coming, in a Museum of Modern Art series and the theatrical release of Godard’s “Goodbye to Language 3D.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/26/movies/godard-tries-out-3-d-in-goodbye-to-language.html', 'snippet': 'Rare opportunities to sample the evolution of 3-D are coming, in a Museum of Modern Art series and the theatrical release of Godard’s “Goodbye to Language 3D.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the beginning, Hollywood has been on the lookout for the next big thing, and 3-D has fit the bill more than once. The box-office success of the 1952 African adventure “Bwana Devil” showed early on that the format could be a moneymaker. That first wave of 3-D would fizzle in a few years, though Alfred Hitchcock, Douglas Sirk and Raoul Walsh all gave it a whirl, not to mention John Ford and a young Francis Ford Coppola.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/263DFILMS1/263DFILMS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 291, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/263DFILMS1/263DFILMS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 291}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/26/arts/263DFILMS1/263DFILMS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 496, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption'