'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing and Nurses', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-27T20:20:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8e70d8d-6860-5305-8da4-134eec332b0f', 'word_count': 950, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8e70d8d-6860-5305-8da4-134eec332b0f'}, {'abstract': 'The phenomenon of “decision fatigue” has been found in judges, who are more likely to deny parole at the end of the day than at the beginning. Now researchers have found a parallel effect in physicians.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/27/doctors-and-decision-fatigue/', 'snippet': 'The phenomenon of “decision fatigue” has been found in judges, who are more likely to deny parole at the end of the day than at the beginning. Now researchers have found a parallel effect in physicians.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The phenomenon of “decision fatigue” has been found in judges, who are more likely to deny parole at the end of the day than at the beginning. Now researchers have found a parallel effect in physicians: As the day wears on, doctors become increasingly more likely to prescribe antibiotics even when they are not indicated. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/04/26/blogs/well-nostrums/well-nostrums-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 214, 'width': 222, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/04/26/blogs/well-nostrums/well-nostrums-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 214, 'width': 222, 'subType': 'superJum