'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO — Dick Costolo, Twitter’s chief executive, has been sprucing up the social network this year. So far, however, the renovations don’t seem to be flashy enough to bring in many newcomers or persuade the veterans to return more frequently.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/28/business/28twitter-web1/28twitter-web1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/28/business/28twitter-web1/28twitter-web1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/28/business/28twitter-web1/28twitter-web1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/28/business/28twitter-web1/28twitter-web1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/28/business/28twitter-web1/28twitter-web1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/28/business/28twitter-web1/28twitter-web1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/28/business/28twitter-web1/28twitter-web1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Twitter’s Revenue Sharply Rises, but Its Usage Stalls', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Usage Stalls For Twitter; Shares Drop ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-27T21:19:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vindu Goel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vindu', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c9811c8-8302-56e3-8d34-ef32c1765e32', 'word_count': 883, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c9811c8-8302-56e3-8d34-ef32c1765e32'}, {'abstract': 'Low-income and working-class Americans are having trouble attending college.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/27/shut-out-of-higher-education/', 'snippet': 'Low-income and working-class Americans are having trouble attending college.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The United States set out 40 years ago to ensure that people who qualified academically for higher education would not be turned away for financial reasons and would have access to college degrees that allowed them to move up on the social ladder. This plan required the states to subsidize public colleges and universities to keep tuition affordable, while the federal government furnished the poorest students with Pell Grants that largely covered the remaining costs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shut Out of Higher Education', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Center for American Progress', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-27T21:25:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brent Staples', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brent', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Staples', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b33069b6-d130-5505-add0-c1c0b8fdf8a6', 'word_count': 402, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b33069b6-d130-5505-add0-c1c0b8fdf8a6'}, {'abstract': 'The automaker is offering lower-cost leases through an arrangement with U.S. Bank, and a three-month trial period.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/28/business/tesla-announces-a-new-leasing-package-for-its-electric-cars.html', 'snippet': 'The automaker is offering lower-cost leases through an arrangement with U.S. Bank, and a three-month trial period.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tesla Motors has unveiled a new package of lease offers for its Model S all-electric sedan, including a three-month return policy.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/28/automobiles/28tesla/28tesla-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/28/automobiles/28tesla/28tesla-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/28/automobiles/28tesla/28tesla-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 575, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/28/automobiles/28tesla/28tesla-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 894, 'width': 1593, 'subType': 'superJumb