lue': 'Ships and Shipping', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-28T01:24:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Energy & Environment ', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Kanter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kanter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0811f4aa-64f2-5430-919a-288eb611cbf0', 'word_count': 1116, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0811f4aa-64f2-5430-919a-288eb611cbf0'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. O’Malley, the Maryland governor, is sounding more and more like a Democratic presidential candidate. But he still treads lightly on the subject of Hillary Rodham Clinton.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/28/us/a-clinton-loyalist-now-a-potential-alternative.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. O’Malley, the Maryland governor, is sounding more and more like a Democratic presidential candidate. But he still treads lightly on the subject of Hillary Rodham Clinton.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BALTIMORE — Last month, as the national media followed Hillary Rodham Clinton to Iowa, a possible challenger for the Democratic presidential nomination, Gov. Martin O’Malley of Maryland, strummed a green guitar under a white backstage tent at Baltimore’s Patterson Park. “If you lose a verse and get brain tired, just nod to me,” Mr. O’Malley told his longtime fellow members in the rock band O’Malley’s March as they warmed up for a concert celebrating the 200th anniversary of “The Star Spangled Banner.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/28/us/OMALLEY/OMALLEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': '