name': 'Meghan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gourley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/95857ad2-de69-526c-9b23-b63da5e36389', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/95857ad2-de69-526c-9b23-b63da5e36389'}, {'abstract': 'In “Goodbye to Language,” Jean-Luc Godard weaves narratives around a man, a woman and a dog.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/29/movies/goodbye-to-language-the-latest-from-jean-luc-godard.html', 'snippet': 'In “Goodbye to Language,” Jean-Luc Godard weaves narratives around a man, a woman and a dog.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jean-Luc Godard, who will soon celebrate his 84th birthday, holds a special place in the pantheon of modern cinema. He is an imp who is venerated as a deity, a Rorschach test, a lightning rod, a fighting word. His name seems to divide the world into skeptics and worshipers, with not much middle ground. Analogous figures can be found in other zones of 20th-century art: Andy Warhol, Bob Dylan, Ezra Pound. They have their singularity in common. They also tend to confound easy distinctions between genius and trickery, and to marshal armies of exegetes in what may be the futile enterprise of figuring out what they mean.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/29/arts/goodbye/goodbye-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 302, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/29/arts/goodbye/goodbye-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 302}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/29/arts/goodbye/goodbye-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 516, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 's