, 'lastname': 'Hartocollis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/11c0c3b5-80c2-57aa-8550-1b6fd8fdd939', 'word_count': 1216, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11c0c3b5-80c2-57aa-8550-1b6fd8fdd939'}, {'abstract': 'Mixing old foods with new to make everything from cakes and corn breads to chilaquiles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/02/magazine/in-cooking-good-ingredients-gone-bad-can-still-be-delicious.html', 'snippet': 'Mixing old foods with new to make everything from cakes and corn breads to chilaquiles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For most of human history, the use of spoiling ingredients wasn’t viewed as sordid. On any bright morning between roughly 400 A.D. (when the “Apicius” of antiquity included instruction for “spoiled honey made good”) and the middle of last century, any thoughtful cook, stained recipe page before her, might have set about whisking batter for sour-beer pancakes, or gathering slightly moldy vegetables for weeknight soup, or resolving to make a sour-milk cake, and her actions would have been seen as properly belonging to the branch of human activity called cooking. The usefulness of an ingredient existed on a temporal spectrum, with differing but comparable values at both ends; this was intrinsic kitchen knowledge, guided by recipes in cookbooks like Lydia Maria Francis Child’s “The American Frugal Housewife” (1832), or Marion Harland’s “Common Sense in the Household” (1871), or Fannie Farmer’s “Boston Cooking-School Cook Book” (1896).', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/magazine/02eat1/mag-02Eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 572, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/02/magazine/02eat1/mag-02Eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 572}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/magazine/02eat1/mag-02Eat-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 976, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/magazine/02eat1/mag-02Eat-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1953, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/magazine/02eat1/mag-02Eat-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/02/magazine/02eat1/mag-02Eat-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/magazine/02eat1/mag-02Eat-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Cooking, Good Ingredients Gone ‘Bad’ Can Still Be Delicious', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Respect Your Elders', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adler, Tamar', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food Contamination and Poisoning', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bacteria', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-29T14:27:22+0000