Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': "By You're the Boss Editors", 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/84d61a53-c94c-5ca5-affb-32decdac7ec6', 'word_count': 236, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84d61a53-c94c-5ca5-affb-32decdac7ec6'}, {'abstract': '“It Shoulda Been You,” with music by Barbara Anselmi and a book and lyrics by Brian Hargrove, turns on warring mothers squaring off at a wedding. David Hyde Pierce will direct.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/29/tyne-daly-and-harriet-harris-to-star-in-it-shoulda-been-you-on-broadway/', 'snippet': '“It Shoulda Been You,” with music by Barbara Anselmi and a book and lyrics by Brian Hargrove, turns on warring mothers squaring off at a wedding. David Hyde Pierce will direct.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Tony Award winners Tyne Daly (“Gypsy”) and Harriet Harris (“Thoroughly Modern Millie”) will square off as warring mothers at a wedding from hell in the Broadway musical comedy “It Shoulda Been You,” which will start preview performances on March 17 at the Brooks Atkinson Theater, the producers announced on Wednesday. The show will mark the Broadway directing debut of David Hyde Pierce, whose acting has earned a Tony (“Curtains”) and four Emmy Awards for “Frasier.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tyne Daly and Harriet Harris to Star in ‘It Shoulda Been You’ on Broadway', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two Stage Veterans to Team Up on Broadway', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daly, Tyne', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harris, Harriet (1955- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-29T16:15:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ee46337-7db0-52c0-a6a0-b64edfa28916', 'word_count': 268, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ee46337-7db0-52c0-a6a0-b64edfa28916'}, {'abstract': 'Michael E. Shapiro, who transformed and enlarged the collection of Atlanta’s High Museum, will leave next year.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/29/director-of-atlantas-high-museum-to-step-down/', 'snippet': 'Michael E. Shapiro, who transformed and enlarged the collection of Atlanta’s High Museum, will leave next year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael E. Shapiro, who has served for 14 years as director of the High Museum of Art in Atlanta and has overseen a fundamental transformation of the institution, greatly enlarging its collection and its international standing, announced on Wednesday that he planned to step down in the summer of 2015.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/30/arts/arts4/arts4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 795, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/30/arts/arts4/arts4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 795}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/30/arts/arts4/arts4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 774, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/30/arts/a