, '_id': 'nyt://video/47c4aa3e-e9c4-5f5d-95e0-fcf3b045de35', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/47c4aa3e-e9c4-5f5d-95e0-fcf3b045de35'}, {'abstract': 'A concert version of “Invisible Cities,” a Pulitzer Prize nominee for music, will be performed at Los Angeles Union Station tonight.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/29/invisible-cities-opera-yuval-sharon-the-industry/', 'snippet': 'A concert version of “Invisible Cities,” a Pulitzer Prize nominee for music, will be performed at Los Angeles Union Station tonight.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Most people think of opera as institutionalized, like museums,” confides the director Yuval Sharon. “I believe that opera is an emerging art. If you look at developments in all the other fields — visual, theater, music, architecture — everything is moving toward the interdisciplinary; and the interdisciplinary is the kernel of what opera is.” Sharon, who is 35, leads the Industry, which in its four years as an experimental opera company has staged productions that “move opera away from the fixed experience of proceedings.” In fact, he says, his projects are actively “immersive and participatory — your experience is prioritized.” That might sound like buzzy lip service in a post-“Sleep No More” performance landscape, but the Industry’s bona fides back up its ambitious mission statement. The company, which is based in Los Angeles (“a city that has always been a frontier for new explorations,” Sharon notes), has commissioned and produced three operas to date, all of which have been site-specific enterprises that reimagine the roles of both the performers and the audience. “Invisible Cities,” based on Italo Calvino’s postmodern book of the same name, has received the most acclaim; its composer, Christopher Cerrone, was a finalist for a 2014 Pulitzer Prize.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/29/t-magazine/29symonds-opera/29symonds-opera-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/29/t-magazine/29symonds-opera/29symonds-opera-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/29/t-magazine/29symonds-opera/29symonds-opera-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/29/t-magazine/29symonds-opera/29symonds-opera-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/29/t-magazine/29symonds-opera/29symonds-opera-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/29/t-magazine/29symonds-opera/29symonds-opera-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/29/t-magazine/29symonds-opera/29symonds-opera-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In a Busy Train Station, a Postmodern Opera Takes Shape', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Calvino, Italo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMTRAK', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-29T20:30:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Baumgardner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baumgardner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/53a65445-bd32-5d9e-a9cb-fd2917bc2a42', 'word_count': 483, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53a65445-bd32-5d9e-a9cb-fd2917bc2a42'}, {'abstract': 'After the Securities and Exchange Commission adopts a regulation to allow virtually anyone to participate as a lender, Lendoor plans to use crowdfunding to give loans to small businesses.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/10/29/start-up-lendoor-aims-to-use-crowdfunding-for-small-business-loans/', 'snippet': 'After the Securities and Exchange Commission adopts a regulation to allow virtually anyone to participate as a lender, Lendoor plans to use crowdfunding to give loans to small businesses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Crowdfunding — gathering lots of small checks from individuals to finance a company or a project — is a hot area in Silicon Valley at the moment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/30/business/dbpix-lendoor/dbpix-lendoor-articleLarge.png', 'height': 475, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/30/business/dbpix-lendoor/dbpix-lendoor-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 475}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/30/business/dbpix-lendoor/dbpix-lendoor-jumbo.png', 'height': 811, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit'