', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lauren', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f39d1227-83e0-5aec-950b-e0c8c0b7b8fc', 'word_count': 1433, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f39d1227-83e0-5aec-950b-e0c8c0b7b8fc'}, {'abstract': 'Check-cashing joints and payday lenders aren’t always a bad idea.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/30/opinion/are-banks-too-expensive-to-use.html', 'snippet': 'Check-cashing joints and payday lenders aren’t always a bad idea.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT was a slow afternoon at Check Center, the check casher/payday lender in a storefront on a busy corner in downtown Berkeley, Calif., where I worked as a teller. My manager, Joe, and I were both dressed in red polo shirts embroidered with the Check Center logo. Joe took advantage of the lull to tell me how to sell prepaid cards — reloadable cash cards that operate like debit cards but aren’t linked to a bank account — to customers.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/30/opinion/1030OPEDshopsin/1030OPEDshopsin-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1032, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/30/opinion/1030OPEDshopsin/1030OPEDshopsin-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1032}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/30/opinion/1030OPEDshopsin/1030OPEDshopsin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 596, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/30/opinion/1030OPEDshopsin/1030OPEDshopsin-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1191, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/30/opinion/1030OPEDshopsin/1030OPEDshopsin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/30/opinion/1030OPEDshopsin/1030OPEDshopsin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/30/opinion/1030OPEDshopsin/1030OPEDshopsin-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Are Banks Too Expensive to Use?', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Are Banks Too Expensive to Use?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Servon, Lisa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Checks and Checking Accounts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Savings', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Payday Loans', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-29T22:35:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Servon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Servon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/88284222-ad0f-53b5-bfe4-bfc465730812', 'word_count': 1046, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88284222-ad0f-53b5-bfe4-bfc465730812'}, {'abstract': 'Designed by UNStudio, the house near the sea has a skin that looks like wind rippling through dunes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/30/garden/an-energy-efficient-home-celebrates-the-dutch-landscape.html', 'snippet': 'Designed by UNStudio, the house near the sea has a skin that looks like wind rippling through dunes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The exterior, with its pattern of rippling pine strips, looks a bit like wind combing through dunes. Designed by UNStudio of Amsterdam for a cat-loving, musical-instrument-collecting family of four, the 4,400-square-foot house near the sea in the Noord-Holland province in the Netherlands pays tribute to the landscape and weather while regulating their effects. Glass walls and a roof deck are for admiring the polders — the fields that stretch out beneath a moody sky. But the use of solar and geothermal energy and thermally efficient clay cladding results in a building that is almost net zero, said its architect, Ben van Berkel. Four small wings surround a core like petals around a flower, each positioned to capture a quality of light or particular view. At the intersection is a staircase that Mr. van Berkel described as the “central furniture of the house,” adding, “It’s where everyone meets.” Information: unstudio.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/30/garden/30CURR1/30CURR1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 265, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/30/garden/30CURR1/30CURR1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 265}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/30/garden/30CURR1/30CURR1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 452, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'lega