}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/29/blogs/20141029-lens-duane-2-slide-1WTO/20141029-lens-duane-2-slide-1WTO-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Death Comes to the Old Lady', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-29T22:41:57+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Blogs', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/157ff1bd-5970-5e4b-b688-76028d2f0561', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/157ff1bd-5970-5e4b-b688-76028d2f0561'}, {'abstract': 'Skepticism made the supernatural seem safe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/30/opinion/ghosts-are-back.html', 'snippet': 'Skepticism made the supernatural seem safe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN 1628, a young woman in the town of Dole, in what is now eastern France, believed she was visited by a ghost. The young woman was ill in bed. At first she saw an ordinary woman who had tidied up and taken care of her. She began to think that her nurse might be a spirit after the kind woman appeared at her side without opening the locked door. The spirit, she believed, was the ghost of her aunt, who came to help her out as a form of penance to lessen the aunt’s time in purgatory.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/opinion/tanya-lurmann/tanya-lurmann-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/05/opinion/tanya-lurmann/tanya-lurmann-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheigh