'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-30T18:45:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roxana Popescu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roxana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Popescu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6fa8f146-4c5a-5c24-b0f2-4c15fce13f2d', 'word_count': 639, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6fa8f146-4c5a-5c24-b0f2-4c15fce13f2d'}, {'abstract': '“The Imitation Game” tells the story of Alan Turing, the gay British mathematician and World War II code breaker who was convicted in 1952 on charges of indecency.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/02/movies/the-imitation-game-dramatizes-the-story-of-alan-turing.html', 'snippet': '“The Imitation Game” tells the story of Alan Turing, the gay British mathematician and World War II code breaker who was convicted in 1952 on charges of indecency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alan Turing, the figure at the center of the already much praised film, “The Imitation Game,” which opens Nov. 28, is probably better known to computer scientists than he is to most moviegoers. \xa0Turing, a British mathematician, is now widely credited with helping to develop the theoretical underpinnings for \xa0modern computing. He was also a war hero of sorts, \xa0largely responsible for cracking the notoriously difficult Enigma code, which the Germans used for virtually all their military communication in World War II. Churchill believed that his was the single biggest contribution to Allied victory. \xa0', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/arts/02MCGRATH/02MCGRATH-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_