': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/79b18ed0-a884-50a4-92da-ccdcdb8beab0', 'word_count': 507, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79b18ed0-a884-50a4-92da-ccdcdb8beab0'}, {'abstract': 'Just 18 percent of female graduates from masters in business administration programs took managerial jobs at tech companies.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/30/why-arent-there-more-women-in-tech/', 'snippet': 'Just 18 percent of female graduates from masters in business administration programs took managerial jobs at tech companies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When officials at technology companies are asked why they employ so few women, they often blame the education system for not producing enough female computer programmers and engineers. While it is true that there are far too few women graduating with degrees in computer science and engineering, that does not completely explain why the large majority of employees of tech companies are men.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Aren’t There More Women in Tech?', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-30T19:52:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vikas Bajaj', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vikas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bajaj', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fcb397a3-a6df-50a3-8f5c-c2e87d50d504', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fcb397a3-a6df-50a3-8f5c-c2e87d50d504'}, {'abstract': 'Jeremy Workman’s “Magical Universe” is a home-video introduction to Al Carbee and his odd, glam Barbie photography.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/31/movies/in-magical-universe-the-outsider-art-of-al-carbee.html', 'snippet': 'Jeremy Workman’s “Magical Universe” is a home-video introduction to Al Carbee and his odd, glam Barbie photography.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ten years ago, the outsider artist Henry Darger was the subject of Jessica Yu’s documentary “In the Realms of the Unreal,” which animated his baroque collage world of frolicking and warring girls. Now comes Jeremy Workman’s “Magical Universe,” an overworked, home-video introduction to Al Carbee and his glam Barbie photography, which filled his cavernous Maine house (alongside tanks of guppies and much else).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/31/arts/MAGICAL/MAGICAL-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 324, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/31/arts/MAGICAL/MAGICAL-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 324}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/31/arts/MAGICAL/MAGICAL-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 554, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/31/arts/MAGICAL/MAGICAL-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1035, 'width': 1914, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/31/arts/MAGICAL/MAGICAL-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/31/arts/MAGICAL/MAGICAL-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/31/arts/MAGICAL/MAGICAL-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'wid