of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/25b76c57-36bf-5f04-9b24-1f7ef38ae63f', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/25b76c57-36bf-5f04-9b24-1f7ef38ae63f'}, {'abstract': 'Doreen Carvajal traces the decades-long saga of Orson Welles’s “The Other Side of The Wind.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/times-insider/2014/10/30/finishing-orson-welless-last-film/', 'snippet': 'Doreen Carvajal traces the decades-long saga of Orson Welles’s “The Other Side of The Wind.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To trace the history of Orson Welles’s final film for her front-page article on Wednesday, a picture that Hollywood buffs consider the most famous movie never released, Doreen Carvajal interviewed the many interested parties, including the movie’s actors and Welles’s heir. When she found herself in a Parisian warehouse amid thousands of Welles’s negatives, she realized that the rumor that the film might finally be finished by the spring of 2015, after more than four decades, could be true. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/29/blogs/welles/welles-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/29/blogs/welles/welles-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/29/blogs/welles/welles-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/29/blogs/welles/welles-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/29/blogs/welles/welles-thumbSt