document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phoebe Eaton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phoebe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eaton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/046184e2-a2d7-590c-948b-5fc6aec26138', 'word_count': 2755, 'uri': 'nyt://article/046184e2-a2d7-590c-948b-5fc6aec26138'}, {'abstract': 'The French art director and Interview Magazine editorial director traveled to Western Greenland to document the once-grand glaciers in Ilulissat with Moncler’s customized Leica X camera.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/31/t-magazine/fabien-baron-tundra-monuments-project.html', 'snippet': 'The French art director and Interview Magazine editorial director traveled to Western Greenland to document the once-grand glaciers in Ilulissat with Moncler’s customized Leica X camera.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The French art director and Interview Magazine editorial director Fabien Baron traded the creature comforts of New York City for the treacherous tundra as part of his Monuments Project, a photo exhibition created in collaboration with Moncler and Leica. Toting the oufitter’s customized Leica X camera (featured in T’s Winter Travel issue), and as seen in the above behind-the-scenes images, Baron documented the once-grand glaciers in Ilulissat (which translates to “iceberg”) in Western Greenland, which are melting into the ocean. “It required moving around a lot,” he remembers. “The icebergs were moving and the light could not be on the ground; we needed to be on a boat for it to work.” So he hired a boat crew to navigate safely through the icy waters. And at one point, he even brought on a fisherman, but not to help cast lines and bring in a big one; the sportsman instead fixed a broken strobe light Baron needed in order to get the right shot (“the way you shoot the icebergs is at night, to make them even more monumental than they were before,” he explains).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fabien Baron’s Treacherous Tundra Adventure', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T04:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooke Bobb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bobb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e9adf02-1a95-52bd-b10b-a50233a17032', 'word_count': 310, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e9adf02-1a95-52bd-b10b-a50233a17032'}, {'abstract': 'Christine and John Gachot, whose client list has included Marc Jacobs, discuss the new interiors they created for the hotel, which will be unveiled to the public next week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/31/t-magazine/gachot-studios-redesign-smyth-tribeca-hotel.html', 'snippet': 'Christine and John Gachot, whose client list has included Marc Jacobs, discuss the new interiors they created for the hotel, which will be unveiled to the public next week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s not surprising that Christine and John Gachot of Gachot Studios would infuse their recent redesign of the Smyth TriBeCa hotel with the feeling of home. The couple, whose SoHo-based firm is known for its warm, comfortably chic midcentury aesthetic, have worked on commercial residential properties such as TriBeCa’s Sterling Mason and private homes for boldfaced names like Marc Jacobs and Tumblr’s David Karp. What’s noteworthy, however, is just how closely the hotel’s revamped downstairs library and aptly named Living Room now mirror the Gachots’ own. “We set out to make it feel extremely residential,” says Christine, who previously worked as vice president of design development at Andre Balazs Properties before co-founding Gachot Studios two years ago. “And along the way, when you’re curating a living room, of course it starts to resemble your own living room.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/29/t-magazine/29beddie-smyth/29beddie-smyth-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/29/t-magazine/29beddie-smyth/29beddie-smyth-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/29/t-magazine/29beddie-smyth/29beddie-smyth-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/29/t-magazine/29beddie-smyth/29beddie-smyth-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/29/t-magazine/29beddie-smyth/29beddie-smyth-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/29/t-magazine/29beddie-smyth/29beddie-smyth-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/29/t-magazine/29beddie-smyth/29beddie-smyth-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A First Look at the Recently Redesigned Smyth TriBeCa', 'kicker': 'By Design', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T04:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TDesign', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laura Neilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Neilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bfcc68cd-a4e4-5f58-a364-f52cd914dda3', 'word_count': 430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bfcc68cd-a4e4-5f58-a364-f52cd914dda3'}, {'abstract': 'Thanks to a doubling of air traffic in the past 15 years, eating preflight no longer means preflipped burgers and cafeteria seating.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/31/t-magazine/the-golden-age-of-airport-restaurants.html', 'snippet': 'Thanks to a doubling of air traffic in the past 15 years, eating preflight no longer means preflipped burgers and cafeteria seating.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In recent years, as airlines around the world have cut back on in-flight food, a wave of business-savvy chefs have given rise to the golden age of airport dining. Want Champagne and caviar? Take a seat at the Petrossian bar at LAX. Have a taste for perfectly marinated goose meat? Check out Hung’s Delicacies at Hong Kong International. Even local joints — Ivar’s fish bar in Seattle, the Salt Lick in Driftwood, Tex. — have gotten into the act, opening airport outposts that make it feel as if you’ve visited even when you’re just passing through. (Chefs at Phoenix Sky Harbor International think so highly of their food they’re pushing to host a James Beard Foundation dinner at the airport next year.) Indeed, thanks to a doubling of air traffic in the past 15 years — not to mention a general refinement of taste on the part of travelers — eating at the airport no longer means just preflipped burgers and cafeteria seating. Here, some stats on food’s rapidly ascending staging ground.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/31/t-magazine/31btn-airports/31btn-airports-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 726, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/31/t-magazine/31btn-airports/31btn-airports-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 726}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/31/t-magazine/31btn-airports/31btn-airports-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 846, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/31/t-magazine/31btn-airports/31btn-airports-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1725, 'width': 1425, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/31/t-magazine/31btn-airports/31btn-airports-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/31/t-magazine/31btn-airports/31btn-airports-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/31/t-magazine/31btn-airports/31btn-airports-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Golden Age of Airport Restaurants', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRPORTS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T04:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TCulture', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jacob Z. Gross', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jacob', 'middlename': 'Z.', 'lastname': 'Gross', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7e81054-96ef-580c-b995-1d45dfb71bfc', 'word_count': 172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7e81054-96ef-580c-b995-1d45dfb71bfc'}, {'abstract': 'Chad Brown has built a powerhouse stable that will be showcased when he sends out 11 starters in the 13-race Breeders’ Cup World Championships on Friday and Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/31/sports/breeders-cup-young-trainer-positioned-to-further-his-standing.html', 'snippet': 'Chad Brown has built a powerhouse stable that will be showcased when he sends out 11 starters in the 13-race Breeders’ Cup World Championships on Friday and Saturday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ARCADIA, Calif. — Chad Brown, who appears to be on an inevitable course to someday emerge as the country’s leading trainer, was always a young man in a hurry.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/31/sports/Y-BREEDERS/Y-BREEDERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 432, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/31/sports/Y-BREEDERS/Y-BREEDERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 432}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/31/sports/Y-BREEDERS/Y-BREEDERS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/31/sports/Y-BREEDERS/Y-BREEDERS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1473, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/31/sports/Y-BREEDERS/Y-BREEDERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/31/sports/Y-BREEDERS/Y-BREEDERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/31/sports/Y-BREEDERS/Y-BREEDERS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Young Trainer Set to Grow His Standing in Breeders’ Cup Races', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Young Trainer Set to Grow His Standing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pedulla, Tom', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jockeys, Drivers and Trainers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Santa Anita Park (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pletcher, Todd', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Breeders' Cup (Horse Races)", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Chad C (1978- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T04:04:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom Pedulla', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pedulla', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cdc828c0-9470-5c77-9ac5-1ddd285ba034', 'word_count': 824, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cdc828c0-9470-5c77-9ac5-1ddd285ba034'}, {'abstract': 'This word has appeared in 196 New York Times articles in the past year.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/31/word-of-the-day-divest/', 'snippet': 'This word has appeared in 196 New York Times articles in the past year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'divest •\\di-ˈvest, də-\\• verb', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Word of the Day | divest', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T04:07:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a7a4dd4-f24a-55f4-bdd3-7b8dc692f70a', 'word_count': 233, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a7a4dd4-f24a-55f4-bdd3-7b8dc692f70a'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/31/arts/television/whats-on-tv-friday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '6 A.M. (TCM) 24 HOURS OF HORROR Turner Classic Movies kicks off its annual fright fest with “London After Midnight” (1927), Tod Browning’s lost masterpiece starring Lon Chaney as a vampire, reconstructed entirely from stills and backed by a new score by the composer Robert Israel. Other highlights include “Mark of the Vampire” (1935), Browning’s talkie remake of “London After Midnight,” this time starring Bela Lugosi, at 7; “Cat People” (1942), with Simone Simon as a Serbian fashion designer who turns into a panther when aroused, at 11; Francis Ford Coppola’s “Dementia” (1963), at 3:15; Roman Polanski’s “Repulsion” (1965), starring Catherine Deneuve as an introverted Belgian manicurist living in London who is increasingly tormented by sexuality, at 6:15; “Night of the Living Dead” (1968), George A. Romero’s zombie classic, at 8; “House of Wax” (1953), with Vincent Price in his first major horror role, as a sinister sculptor, at 11:45; and Tobe Hooper’s “Poltergeist” (1982), at 1:30 a.m.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Friday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Friday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T05:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/20a54ffc-e3a9-57e7-9f17-b6d93d284962', 'word_count': 694, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20a54ffc-e3a9-57e7-9f17-b6d93d284962'}, {'abstract': 'The 15th hole at Bethpage Black humbled the top players at the U.S. Open on Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2009/06/19/sports/0619-tiger-graphic.html', 'snippet': 'The 15th hole at Bethpage Black humbled the top players at the U.S. Open on Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 15th hole at Bethpage Black humbled the top players at the U.S. Open on Friday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tiger Woods’s Double Bogey on 15', 'kicker': 'U.S. Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woods, Tiger', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Golf)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T05:01:33+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/3267ddcf-c9fb-5a24-83ff-659d74f6f8c7', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/3267ddcf-c9fb-5a24-83ff-659d74f6f8c7'}, {'abstract': 'After a lost season, the Mets got some good news Thursday when Juan Lagares was named the best center fielder in baseball, winning the annual Fielding Bible award.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/31/sports/baseball/mets-juan-lagares-named-best-center-fielder.html', 'snippet': 'After a lost season, the Mets got some good news Thursday when Juan Lagares was named the best center fielder in baseball, winning the annual Fielding Bible award.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After a lost season, the Mets got some good news Thursday when Juan Lagares was named the best center fielder in baseball, winning the annual Fielding Bible award. Lagares, who improved dramatically as a hitter this season, kept up the strong defense he showed as a rookie, saving 28 runs, according to the website Baseball Info Solutions. Unlike the Gold Gloves, the Fielding Bible awards are presented to only one player at each position regardless of league. The other 2014 winners were Adrian Gonzalez (first base); Dustin Pedroia (second base); Josh Donaldson (third base); Andrelton Simmons (shortstop); Alex Gordon (left field); Jason Heyward (right field); Jonathan Lucroy (catcher); Dallas Keuchel (pitcher); and Lorenzo Cain (multiposition). ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mets’ Juan Lagares Named Best Center Fielder', 'kicker': 'Sports Briefing | Baseball', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mets’ Lagares Named Best Center Fielder', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lagares, Juan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_d