l': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af6ebb06-f2f3-58a5-9b5b-e2f7be300efd', 'word_count': 1422, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af6ebb06-f2f3-58a5-9b5b-e2f7be300efd'}, {'abstract': 'Citigroup says it has to cut its third-quarter profit by $600 million because of legal expenses. | Apple chief’s coming out. | Judge approves bankruptcy plan for Stockton, Calif.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/10/31/morning-agenda-citigroups-about-face/', 'snippet': 'Citigroup says it has to cut its third-quarter profit by $600 million because of legal expenses. | Apple chief’s coming out. | Judge approves bankruptcy plan for Stockton, Calif.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CITIGROUP CUTS THIRD-QUARTER PROFIT | Citigroup can’t seem to catch a break. On Thursday, the bank startled investors with an announcement that it had to lower its third-quarter profit by $600 million because of “rapidly evolving regulatory inquiries and investigations,” DealBook’s Michael Corkery, Peter Eavis and Ben Protess write. Citigroup reported its results for the quarter two weeks ago, just before the legal development arose. Although the bank did not specify the development, people briefed on the matter attributed the nearly 18 percent drop in earnings to an investigation into whether Citigroup manipulated the price of foreign currencies. Since reporting its third-quarter results, the bank has edged nearer a settlement with at least one regulator in the case, the people said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: Citigroup’s About-Face', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T12:19:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9878234-9774-5644-906a-533f148092ff', 'word_count': 2415, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9878234-9774-5644-906a-533f148092ff'}, {'abstract': 'A sharp increase in renters is fueling a boom in apartment construction, while the homeownership rate in America is at a 20-year low.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/01/business/Homeownership-renting-and-apartment-construction.html', 'snippet': 'A sharp increase in renters is fueling a boom in apartment construction, while the homeownership rate in America is at a 20-year low.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE homeownership rate in the United States plunged during the Great Recession. Many families lost their homes as prices collapsed and unemployment rose.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Americans Are Preferring the Lease to the Mortgage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'More Americans Are Preferring the Lease to the Mortgage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Renting and Leasing (Real Estate)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T12:20:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6262a5a3-3c03-519b-b9df-ca9d17