 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/magazine/02talk/mag-02Talk-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Megan Smith: ‘You Can Affect Billions of People’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘You Can Affect Billions of People’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Megan J (1964- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T14:30:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Susan Dominus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Susan', 'lastname': 'Dominus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/65e407d2-7915-5605-9145-babad333f113', 'word_count': 701, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65e407d2-7915-5605-9145-babad333f113'}, {'abstract': 'Animal arms races always unfold in the same way. But those with the biggest weapons don’t always win.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/02/magazine/the-astonishing-weaponry-of-dung-beetles.html', 'snippet': 'Animal arms races always unfold in the same way. But those with the biggest weapons don’t always win.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a still-sweltering evening in Tanzania more than a decade ago, my colleagues and I crouched around a fresh pile of elephant dung to witness an epic struggle of wills. Immediately upon our arrival, beetles began plonking into the grass around us. Soon dozens were raining down from the sky at once, and, not being the most agile fliers, they tumbled into our hair and bounced down the backs of our necks. Within a few short minutes, as many as 50,000 dung beetles landed, all racing to carve out a meal for themselves and their young.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/magazine/02eureka/mag-02Eureka-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 587, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/02/magazine/02eureka/mag-02Eureka-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 587}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/magazine/02eureka/mag-02Eureka-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1001, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/magazine/02eureka/mag-02Eureka-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2000, 'width': 2045, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/magazine/02eureka/mag-02Eureka-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/02/magazine/02eureka/mag-02Eureka-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/magazine/02eureka/mag-02Eureka-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Astonishing Weaponry of Dung Beetles', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'You Call That a Horn?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Emlen, Douglas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMALS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beetles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reproduction (Biological)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T14:30:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Douglas Emlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Douglas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Emlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2acbde65-01cb-5ff7-9545-6be0173e53d3', 'word_count': 1288, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2acbde65-01cb-5ff7-9545-6be0173e53d3'}, {'abstract': 'Say nothing; pay something.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/02/magazine/is-it-wrong-to-divulge-a-secret-to-protect-someones-honor.html', 'snippet': 'Say nothing; pay something.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m an expat living in a country where H.I.V./AIDS is a severe taboo. My friend, also an expat, was diagnosed with H.I.V. while living here. She received almost no support or therapy of any form. Her attitude and behavior changed as she became ill, and she alienated and frustrated mutual friends. While I knew the truth, I was entrusted with her secret and stayed silent when I heard people bad-mouth her. Now that she has returned home, I feel compelled to defend her honor — I believe she was judged too harshly given unfathomable pressure and circumstances. But I also feel obligated to hold on to her secret. Is it acceptable to divulge a secret to protect someone’s honor? NAME WITHHELD, CAIRO', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/magazine/02ethicist/mag-02Ethicist-t_CA0-articleLa