torneys General Assn', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T14:41:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Lipton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lipton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77ccb67a-a270-5b30-a7b0-a364f9b0ce63', 'word_count': 433, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77ccb67a-a270-5b30-a7b0-a364f9b0ce63'}, {'abstract': 'Two books about Berlin, past and present, explore its dualities of sex and violence, freedom and fascism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/02/books/review/peter-schneiders-berlin-now-and-rory-macleans-berlin.html', 'snippet': 'Two books about Berlin, past and present, explore its dualities of sex and violence, freedom and fascism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the German writer Peter \xadSchneider published “The Wall Jumper,” his celebrated elliptical novella about the divided Berlin of the Cold War, in 1982, the city’s central importance to the 20th century was unquestioned. First the kaiser and then the Führer had touched off world wars from Germany’s capital. And when the world was split between Soviet and American blocs, Berlin could rightly claim to be the front line. The concrete barrier zigzagging through its streets stood as the tangible symbol of that division.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-bks-KULISH/1102-bks-KULISH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 318, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-bks-KULISH/1102-bks-KULISH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 318}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-bks-KULISH/1102-bks-KULISH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 544, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-bks-KULISH/1102-bks-KULISH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 767, 'width': 1445, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-bks-KULISH/1102-bks-KULISH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-bks-KULISH/1102-bks-KULISH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-bks-KULISH/1102-bks-KULISH-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Peter \xadSchneider’s ‘Berlin Now’ and Rory MacLean’s ‘Berlin’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Wall Within', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kulish, Nicholas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schneider, Peter', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'MacLean, Rory', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berlin (Germany)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T14:50:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kulish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kulish', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/09dc83ed-c860-551e-bc18-54d83f853346', 'word_count': 1229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09dc83ed-c860-551e-bc18-54d83f853346'}, {'abstract': 'From the mid-19th century through the 1930s, gay people were at home in Berlin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/02/books/review/gay-berlin-by-robert-beachy.html', 'snippet': 'From the mid-19th century through the 1930s, gay people were at home in Berlin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the night of June 30, 1934, a unit of Heinrich Himmler’s Schutzstaffel (the SS), with logistical support from the German Army, pulled up outside a hotel in Bad Wiessee, south of Munich, and in Hitler’s presence hauled a bewildered Ernst Röhm, leader of the Nazi brownshirts (or SA), out of bed. He and his cronies were taken to Munich’s Stadelheim prison, where they were shot on the spot — except for Röhm, whose execution occurred shortly thereafter. Similar raids took place that night against other SA leaders, who were charged, like Röhm, with having planned a coup against Hitler and his government. In reality, this “Night of the Long Knives” was the violent end to power struggles between Hitler and the army officer corps on the one hand, and Röhm, as the head of roughly two million paramilitary storm troopers, on the other. They had challenged Hitler’s absolute leadership and also the professional officer corps’s claim to be the sole bearer of arms in Nazi Germany.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-bks-BERGHAHN/1102-bks-BERGHAHN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 599, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-bks-BERGHAHN/1102-bks-BERGHAHN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 599, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-bks-BERGHAHN/1102-bks-BERGHAHN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-bks-BERGHAHN/1102-bks-BERGHAHN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-bks-BERGHAHN/1102-bks-BERGHAHN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Gay Berlin,’ by Robert Beachy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Open City', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beachy, Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T14:50:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By V R. Berghahn', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2692e8cd-ad52-5d00-8a8c-6c7c328c64a1', 'word_count': 1082, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2692e8cd-ad52-5d00-8a8c-6c7c328c64a1'}, {'abstract': 'Lincoln dealt shrewdly with the publishers and editors of politically powerful 19th-century newspapers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/02/books/review/lincoln-and-the-power-of-the-press-by-harold-holzer.html', 'snippet': 'Lincoln dealt shrewdly with the publishers and editors of politically powerful 19th-century newspapers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Abraham Lincoln has been portrayed in many roles — as emancipator, politician, military leader, orator, self-made man and others — but his canny manipulation of the popular press has received little attention. Harold Holzer, a prominent authority on America’s 16th president, opens many vistas on this fascinating topic in his new book, “Lincoln and the Power of the Press,” a monumental, richly detailed portrait of the world of 19th-\xadcentury journalism and Lincoln’s relation to it. Holzer demonstrates that even as Lincoln juggled many war-related demands, he kept a close eye on American newspapers and tried to influence them however he could. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/112-REYNOLDS/112-REYNOLDS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 492, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/112-REYNOLDS/112-REYNOLDS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 492}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/112-REYNOLDS/112-REYNOLDS-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 840, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/112-REYNOLDS/112-REYNOLDS-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1680, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/re