le': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3da2668f-9876-51cd-b18e-69cb9940f6bf', 'word_count': 1191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3da2668f-9876-51cd-b18e-69cb9940f6bf'}, {'abstract': 'Scientists warn that a potent fungus poses a grave threat to American salamanders, with the pet trade the probable path without more controls.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/10/31/can-congress-act-to-block-fungal-threat-to-u-s-amphibians/', 'snippet': 'Scientists warn that a potent fungus poses a grave threat to American salamanders, with the pet trade the probable path without more controls.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I hope you’ll read and disseminate Jim Gorman’s story on the new Science paper pointing to a potential ecological catastrophe in North America should a potent\xa0chytrid fungus that attacks salamanders arrive on these shores. The fungus originated in Asia and its arrival in Europe has already caused substantial salamander and newt losses there. A related fungus has already devastated a host of frog species around the tropics, as I’ve explored in many posts. [Update, Nov. 14 | Two herpetologists, Karen Lips and Joseph Mendelson, have written an excellent Op-Ed article on the issue.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/31/world/31salamander/31salamander-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 367, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/31/world/31salamander/31salamander-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 367}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/31/world/31salamander/31salamander-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 626, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'c