: 'Boyagoda, Randy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morton, Brian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T16:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Boyagoda', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boyagoda', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/992b1629-4c72-5825-8ce9-d1475cf9c8e2', 'word_count': 689, 'uri': 'nyt://article/992b1629-4c72-5825-8ce9-d1475cf9c8e2'}, {'abstract': 'In 1787, George Washington again rode to the nation’s rescue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/02/books/review/edward-j-larsons-the-return-of-george-washington-1783-1789.html', 'snippet': 'In 1787, George Washington again rode to the nation’s rescue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If the American Revolution has been remembered like a five-act play, the years after the war are the tension-filled fourth, so suspenseful as to border on melodrama. Even the heroes seem almost hysterical. John Quincy Adams, 20 years old in 1787, deemed it the “critical period” even before its conclusion. The new states went their own democratic ways in dealing with a credit crisis and left the Continental Congress unable to pay its debts. A high point of revolutionary radicalism, it was a low point for what Adams called the “bonds of union.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-WALDSTREICHER/1102-WALDSTREICHER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 705, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-WALDSTREICHER/1102-WALDSTREICHER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 705}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-WALDSTREICHER/1102-WALDSTREICHER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 872, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-WALDSTREICHER/1102-WALDSTREICHER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1744, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-WALDSTREICHER/1102-WALDSTREICHER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-WALDSTREICHER/1102-WALDSTREICHER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-WALDSTREICHER/1102-WALDSTREICHER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Edward J. Larson’s ‘The Return of George Washington: 1783-1789’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wilderness Years', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Larson, Edward J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T16:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Waldstreicher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname'