0', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brock Clarke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brock', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clarke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/03f52d22-14af-5816-9987-07585eceb5e2', 'word_count': 1059, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03f52d22-14af-5816-9987-07585eceb5e2'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/02/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE BURGLARY: The Discovery of J. Edgar Hoover’s Secret FBI,  by Betty Medsger. (Vintage, $16.95.) This is an impeccably researched account of the eight antiwar activists who broke into an F.B.I. office in Media, Pa., in 1971, and exposed how the bureau, as “secret judge, secret jury and secret warden,” engaged in illegal surveillance and harassment of American citizens. Medsger, who first wrote about the break-in as a Washington Post reporter in 1971, now brings the burglars’ stories into the public eye for the first time.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T16:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1cf5bd1-1273-5442-895c-d74a45797dfc', 'word_count': 483, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1cf5bd1-1273-5442-895c-d74a45797dfc'}, {'abstract': 'Resistance in a provincial French town helped save thousands.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/02/books/review/village-of-secrets-by-caroline-moorehead.html', 'snippet': 'Resistance in a provincial French town helped save thousands.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Between 1940 and 1944, France’s Vichy government collaborated actively with the Nazis, and the French police conducted arrests and deportations. During these harrowing years, the village of Le \xadChambon-sur-Lignon in the Haute-Loire region hid Jews after the Nazi invasion and is said to have saved thousands of people. Le Chambon became a light in dark times, heralded by the former French president Jacques Chirac as “the conscience of our country.” In 1998, Yad Vashem honored it as \xadRighteous Among the Nations. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-DEAN/1102-DEAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-DEAN/1102-DEAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 391}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-DEAN/1102-DEAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-DEAN/1102-DEAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1333, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-DEAN/1102-DEAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-DEAN/1102-DEAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-DEAN/1102-DEAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Village of Secrets,’ by Caroline Moorehead', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ordinary Courage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moorehead, Caroline', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T16:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carolyn J. Dean', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carolyn', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Dean', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/da297d9a-8b3c-5935-b0f9-4763379a6734', 'word_count': 620, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da297d9a-8b3c-5935-b0f9-4763379a6734'}, {'abstract': 'In Michael Connelly’s “The Burning Room,” Harry Bosch tries to impart wisdom to his new partner.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/02/books/review/michael-connellys-burning-room-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In Michael Connelly’s “The Burning Room,” Harry Bosch tries to impart wisdom to his new partner.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What do you do with a tired old cop? You give him a bright young rookie to keep him on the ball. In return for this infusion of energy, the kid gets the benefit of the broad experience and infinite wisdom of the veteran. That’s the way it’s supposed to work, anyway. But in THE BURNING ROOM (Little, Brown, $28), Michael Connelly’s latest Harry Bosch mystery, there’s precious little wisdom the L.A.P.D. detective can impart to his new partner, Lucia (Lucy) Soto, about the curious case of a homicide victim who took 10 years to die.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-bks-CRIME/1102-bks-CRIME-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 325, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-bks-CRIME/1102-bks-CRIME-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 325}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-bks-CRIME/1102-bks-CRIME-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 555, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-bks-CRIME/1102-bks-CRIME-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1111, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-bks-CRIME/1102-bks-CRIME-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-bks-CRIME/1102-bks-CRIME-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-bks-CRIME/1102-bks-CRIME-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Michael Connelly’s ‘Burning Room,’ and More', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Deadly Embers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Connelly, Michael (1956- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rendell, Ruth', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Neville, Stuart', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Littlefield, Sophie', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T16:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6659f70d-ac56-5254-a749-ced4929ebf52', 'word_count': 906, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6659f70d-ac56-5254-a749-ced4929ebf52'}, {'abstract': 'A recounting of Martin Luther King Jr.’s difficult final year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/02/books/review/death-of-a-king-by-tavis-smiley-with-david-ritz.html', 'snippet': 'A recounting of Martin Luther King Jr.’s difficult final year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In one of those tragic coincidences that seemed to occur, chockablock, throughout the 1960s, exactly one year separated Martin Luther King Jr.’s momentous antiwar polemic at Riverside Church in Manhattan on April 4, 1967, and his assassination at the Lorraine Motel in Memphis in 1968. The year between was more than King’s last; it was also his worst, as the television and radio host Tavis Smiley explains in his lucid, if not exactly groundbreaking, book “Death of a King: The Real Story of Dr. Martin Luther King Jr.’s Final Year.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/1102-RISEN/1102-RISEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 458, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/02/books/1102-RISEN/1102-RISEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 458}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/1102-RISEN/1102-RISEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 781, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/1102-RISEN/1102-RISEN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1562, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/1102-RISEN/1102-RISEN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/02/books/1102-RISEN/1102-RISEN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/1102-RISEN/1102-RISEN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Death of a King,’ by Tavis Smiley with David Ritz', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dream Deferred', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Risen, Clay', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smiley, Tavis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ritz, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Martin Luther Jr', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T16:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clay Risen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Risen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dee39c22-a7da-5ce6-97c1-9e2058812c12', 'word_count': 652, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dee39c22-a7da-5ce6-97c1-9e2058812c12'}, {'abstract': 'Recovering Aristotle as a scientist who explored and cataloged the Mediterranean’s natural world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/02/books/review/the-lagoon-how-aristotle-invented-science-by-armand-marie-leroi.html', 'snippet': 'Recovering Aristotle as a scientist who explored and cataloged the Mediterranean’s natural world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Armand Marie Leroi is a scientist, and Aristotle is his hero. This conjunction is interesting because, in the official telling of modern science’s origins, Aristotle is hardly regarded as heroic. Instead he’s portrayed as the obstacle over which the early heroes of the scientific revolution — Copernicus, Kepler, Galileo — had to leap in order to impose a genuinely explanatory methodology over the often deceptive input of sense perception. After all, what could be clearer to the senses than that the Earth is stationary and the heavens revolve around it? If there was any ancient whom these pioneers of modern science esteemed it was Plato. He, too, had emphasized the deceptiveness of appearances. What’s more he had suggested, however obliquely, most especially in his “Timaeus,” that mathematics reveals the true structure of the cosmos, offering us the means by which we can distinguish between reliable and unreliable appearances. So, at least, did men like Galileo read the “Timaeus,” finding insights there to topple the formidable edifice of a Church-fortified Aristotelianism. The new scientists discarded at least two of the four Aristotelian causes, the formal and the final, the latter having injected a pernicious teleology into all physical explanations. (Flames leap and rocks fall because their constituent elements are striving to get to their right places.) In place of Aristotle’s qualitative categories, the new scientists put quantitative descriptions of matter in motion. What Aristotle had sundered, the celestial and the terrestrial, were united under one mechanics. And the rest, as they say, is history.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-Goldstein/1102-Goldstein-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 442, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-Goldstein/1102-Goldstein-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 442}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-Goldstein/1102-Goldstein-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 755, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-Goldstein/1102-Goldstein-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1509, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-Goldstein/1102-Goldstein-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-Goldstein/1102-Goldstein-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/1102-Goldstein/1102-Goldstein-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Lagoon: How Aristotle Invented Science,’ by Armand Marie Leroi', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bodies of Knowledge', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leroi, Armand Marie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T16:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca Newberger Goldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': 'Newberger', 'lastname': 'Goldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/82e4bfc8-d2e8-5851-86fc-922040c7548a', 'word_count': 1215, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82e4bfc8-d2e8-5851-86fc-922040c7548a'}, {'abstract': 'How did America become a postwar haven for Nazis?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/02/books/review/the-nazis-next-door-by-eric-lichtblau.html', 'snippet': 'How did America become a postwar haven for Nazis?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the wake of World War II, America recruited a few leading German scientists in order to advance our space and military programs and to keep these valuable assets from falling into Soviet hands. This is the broadly accepted script about Nazis in America. In fact, as Eric Lichtblau, a Pulitzer Prize-winning investigative reporter for The New York Times, relates in “The Nazis Next Door,” we welcomed approximately 10,000 Nazis, some of whom had played pivotal roles in the genocide.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/02LIPSTADTSUB/02LIPSTADTSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 942, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/02LIPSTADTSUB/02LIPSTADTSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 942}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/02LIPSTADTSUB/02LIPSTADTSUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 653, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/02LIPSTADTSUB/02LIPSTADTSUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1305, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/02LIPSTADTSUB/02LIPSTADTSUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/02LIPSTADTSUB/02LIPSTADTSUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/02LIPSTADTSUB/02LIPSTADTSUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Nazis Next Door,’ by Eric Lichtblau', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Safe House', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lipstadt, Deborah E', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lichtblau, Eric', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T16:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deborah E. Lipstadt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deborah', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Lipstadt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0cde227a-d42b-5488-a4c6-97c76c1e8d86', 'word_count': 593, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0cde227a-d42b-5488-a4c6-97c76c1e8d86'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/02/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A BRIEF HISTORY OF SEVEN KILLINGS,  by Marlon James. (Riverhead, $28.95.) Revolving around the assassination attempt on Bob Marley in 1976, this mesmerizingly powerful novel addresses politics, class, race and violence in Jamaica.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T16:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/1906b495-3012-5329-bea2-f5c1e953fe00', 'word_count': 246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1906b495-3012-5329-bea2-f5c1e953fe00'}, {'abstract': 'Bryan Stevenson, whose “Just Mercy” is No. 10 on the hardcover nonfiction list, says that “if we’re really committed to justice, we’ve got to do better for the poor than we’re doing.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/09/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Bryan Stevenson, whose “Just Mercy” is No. 10 on the hardcover nonfiction list, says that “if we’re really committed to justice, we’ve got to do better for the poor than we’re doing.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mockingbird Redux: “Just Mercy,” a memoir by the human rights lawyer and MacArthur genius Bryan Stevenson, who founded the Equal Justice Initiative in Alabama, enters the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 10. As Stevenson told Jon Stewart during a recent appearance on “The Daily Show,” one story in the book involves a man wrongly convicted of murder and sentenced to death before Stevenson took him on as a client. “The case that I talk about was out of Monroeville, Ala.,” he said. “Monroeville is the community where Harper Lee grew up and wrote ‘To Kill a Mockingbird.’ And if you go to people there, they love that story. And they love celebrating that story. But we have an innocent African-American man being sent to death row for a crime he didn’t commit, and there’s no consciousness about that. . . . One of the most powerful things for me in this book was trying to get people to appreciate that we have a criminal justice system that treats you better if you’re rich and guilty than if you’re poor and innocent. I’m not trying to beat up on people who are wealthy, but I want us to understand if we’re really committed to justice, we’ve got to do better for the poor than we’re doing. We’ve got to do better for the mentally disabled, better for women, better for drug-dependent people than we’re doing.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '54', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/02insidelist/02insidelist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 456, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/02insidelist/02insidelist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 456, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/02insidelist/02insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/02insidelist/02insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/books/review/02insidelist/02insidelist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T16:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '',