ns to Carnegie Hall for a solo recital on Tuesday with the pianist David Zobel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fresh from her arresting portrayal of the title role in Handel’s “Alcina” at Carnegie Hall, the mezzo-soprano Joyce DiDonato returns there for a solo recital on Tuesday with the pianist David Zobel. As part of her Perspectives Series, Ms. DiDonato — an artist admired for her technical prowess, emotional commitment and charismatic stage presence — offers a musical snapshot of Venice from the Baroque era to the 20th century, featuring works by Vivaldi, Fauré, Rossini, Reynaldo Hahn and Michael Head. (8 p.m.; 212-247-7800, carnegiehall.org.) ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Voyage to Venice, a Nod to Youth', 'kicker': 'Classical', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Voyage to Venice, a Nod to Youth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DiDonato, Joyce', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T20:05:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1f075dc-f114-5003-9e8e-add74daaccba', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1f075dc-f114-5003-9e8e-add74daaccba'}, {'abstract': 'On the 100th anniversary of the composer Irving Fine’s birth, American Neo-Classical music will be back on some concert programs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/02/arts/music/celebrating-the-works-of-irving-fine-born-100-years-ago.html', 'snippet': 'On the 100th anniversary of the composer Irving Fine’s birth, American Neo-Classical music will be back on some concert programs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Soon after arriving at Harvard as a freshman in 1947, the composer Martin Boykan stumbled upon a choir rehearsal. “I sat and listened to them, and it was very clear to me that it was by Stravinsky,” he recalled in a telephone interview. “It was Stravinsky’s style, and I thought it was absolutely wonderful. The music was just really great. Stravinsky was absolutely the best.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/arts/02FINE1/02FINE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 425, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/02/arts/02FINE1/02FINE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 425}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/arts/02FINE1/02FINE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 726, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/arts/02FINE1/02FINE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 1129, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/arts/02FINE1/02FINE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/02/arts/02FINE1/02FINE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/arts/02FINE1/02FINE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Revisiting Our Neo-Classical Moment', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Revisiting Our Neo-Classical Moment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robin, William', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fine, Irving (1914-62)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T20:05:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Robin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/016f7677-06b5-58d9-ab47-2ffb023156c9', 'word_count': 1448, 'uri': 'nyt://article/016f7677-06b5-58d9-ab47-2ffb023156c9'}, {'abstract': 'Jules Feiffer will be appearing at an event to promote his acclaimed graphic novel “Kill My Mother” and a screening of “The Big Sleep.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/02/movies/there-is-nothing-like-a-dame.html', 'snippet': 'Jules Feiffer will be appearing at an event to promote his acclaimed graphic novel “Kill My Mother” and a screening of “The Big Sleep.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'This fall, the indefatigable Jules Feiffer — cartoonist, playwright, screenwriter, novelist, children’s book illustrator — has, at the age of 85, drawn another trick out of his tireless pen: his acclaimed graphic novel “Kill My Mother,” a rainy, twisty homage to hard-boiled crime fiction. He’ll be signing the book, which is dedicated to James M. Cain, Billy Wilder, John Huston and Howard Hawks (as well as the cartoonists Will Eisner and Milton Caniff, among others), Thursday at a reception at the Cinema Arts Center on Long Island. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/arts/02GUIDE5/02GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 502, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/02/arts/02GUIDE5/02GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 502}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/arts/02GUIDE5/02GUIDE5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 563, 'width': 673, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/arts/02GUIDE5/02GUIDE5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 563, 'width': 673, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/arts/02GUIDE5/02GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/02/arts/02GUIDE5/02GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/arts/02GUIDE5/02GUIDE5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'There Is Nothing Like a Dame', 'kicker': 'Film', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'There Is Nothing Like a Dame', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-10-31T20:07:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a806d5f-38bc-5b00-b8ca-f303178e1e2a', 'word_count': 172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a806d5f-38bc-5b00-b8ca-f303178e1e2a'}, {'abstract': 'Lamenting the loss of a favorite spot you haven’t visited in a decade.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/02/nyregion/its-taylor-swifts-city-now.html', 'snippet': 'Lamenting the loss of a favorite spot you haven’t visited in a decade.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you have been tallying up reasons to despair over living in New York of late, you may have added to the list the recent naming of Taylor Swift as the city’s first Global Welcome Ambassador. The designation, made by the department of tourism, has been synchronized to the release of her new single, “Welcome to New York,” a song so bloodless and indistinct in its vision of the city that it presents to you, as the blogger David Colon put it, the New York you would get “if you populated it entirely with humans raised in the Times Square Bubba Gump Shrimp Co., then let them out into the world with only a penthouse apartment, an Amex black card and leopard-print Prada luggage.”', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/nyregion/02BIG/02BIG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/02/nyregion/02BIG/02BIG-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/02/nyregion/02BIG/02BIG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJu